window.LECTURES = [{"id": "acid-base", "title": "E5 Acid Base Disturbances ZN", "file": "/lectures/acid-base.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "ACID-BASE BALANCE"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Content\n• 1. Introduction\n• Definition of acidemia and alkalemia\n• General effect of acidemia and alkalemia\n• Difference between acidosis and acidemia\n• Difference between alkalosis and alkalemia\n• Blood sampling to determine the acid-base status\n• 2. Examples of biochemical pathways that produce H+\n• 3. Buffer: \n• pH basics\n• Buffer system and organs\n• 4. Clinical Approach to Acid-Base Disturbances\n• Henderson-hasselbalch equation\n• Astrup\n• Respiratory component with/without compensation\n• Metabolic component with/without compensation\n• Anion gap and BE\n• Typical examples of metabolic acidosis with high AG, normal AG and \nmetabolic alkalosis\n• Simple and mixed disturbances\n• Cases"}, {"page": 3, "text": "1. Introduction"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Extreme value that is \ncompatible with life \nunder exceptional \nconditions\nExtreme value that is \ncompatible with life \nunder exceptional \nconditions\nSerious \nacidosis\nNormal \npH\npH increase\nH+ increase\nantialgorithm\nIn comparison with alkalosis, acidosis can be compensated better, \nbecause we accept larger drop of pH than its increase.\nDefinition of acidemia and alkalemia"}, {"page": 5, "text": "• Negative inotropic (contractility), and chronotropic (heart rate) \neffect but it is neutralized by catecholamines that are also \nstimulated by acidosis.\n• Inhibit CNS including respiratory centers\n• In systemic vessels, \n• acidosis causes vasodilation in peripheral arterioles but is \nalso offset by catecholamine, so that peripheral vascular \nresistance remains relatively constant. \n• In brain, vasodilation can cause edema.\n• in the venous system, the direct effect of acidosis \nis vasoconstriction, which is further enhanced by \ncatecholamine release: prevent lung edema\n• Osteoporosis : Ca2+ and phosphate liberated from bone \n(hydroxyapatite, Ca5(PO4)3(OH)) \n• Final stage: coma (due to H+-mediated protein denaturation in \nCNS, dehydration, hyperosmolarity in diabetic ketoacidosis)\nAcidemia: general effect"}, {"page": 6, "text": "• Acute alkalosis increases binding and reduces free plasma Ca2+ \nleading to tetany\n• Compensation of MA by hypoventilation is limited by O2\n• In brain, vasoconstriction that decrease its perfusion\n• Final stage: coma or asphyxia\nAlkalemia: general effect"}, {"page": 7, "text": "acidosis vs acidemia and alkalosis vs alkalemia\nBLOOD\nLocal process that does not have to change blood"}, {"page": 8, "text": " Sampling:\n◦ arterial blood\n◦ capillary blood\n◦ venous blood\n◦ arterial and venous sampling\nBlood sampling to determine the acid-base status"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Blood sampling to determine the acid-base status\n Into heparinized capillaries immediately hermetically sealed to \nprevent uncontrolled contact with air (02 CO2)\n Check whether the column of blood in the capillaries is without \nbubbles\n The sample immediately into crushed ice or in a refrigerator \n(temperature up to 00 C) in order to immediate arrest metabolism of \nerythrocyte-lactate production – it may affect the evaluation of the \nsample ABR !!!!)\n the fastest sample processing - barrier of inadequate results"}, {"page": 10, "text": "• Anaerobic glycolysis (lactic acid)\n• Lipolysis\n• Ketone bodies in the liver\n• Degradation of some amino acids: Sulphur containing amino acids and \ncationic amino acids produce H2SO4 and H3PO4\n2. Examples of biochemical pathways that \nproduce H+"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Production of  H+\nanaerobic metabolism \n• The electron transport chain cannot work \nwithout O2, leading to accumulation of \nNADH. Therefore, lactic acid is \nsynthetized to replenish NAD+. The lactic \nacid create rapidly lactate and H+.\n• Lactate can be used as a source of \nenergy.\n• Lactate undergoes oxidation (O2 is \nrequired) predominantly in the hepatocytes, but also in \nthe kidneys as well as cardiac and skeletal\n• Lactate undergoes gluconeogenesis, predominantly in \nthe liver and to a lesser extent in the kidneys."}, {"page": 12, "text": "Other sources of H+ production\n• Production of free fatty acids from adipose tissue (lipolysis)\n• Triglycerides → glycerol + 3 fatty acids + 3H+\nIn the mitochondria, beta-oxidation generates one acetyl CoA, one \nNADH and one FADH2 for every removal of a two-carbon group from \nfatty acids. Acetyl-CaA enters to the citric acid cycle while NADH and \nFADH2 are used in the electron transport chain where O2 is required."}, {"page": 13, "text": "Production of ketone bodies\n• Ketone bodies are produced mainly in the mitochondria of liver cells in \nresponse to an unavailable of blood glucose, such as during fasting.\n• Fatty acids → ketone bodies + nH+\n• Ketone bodies are alternative source of energy, e.g. for heart\n• The most important ketone bodies are acetone, acetoacetate and beta-\nhydroxybutyrate"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Other Sources of H+ \n• H2SO4 - sulphuric acid is produced by oxidation of sulfhydryl groups, e.g. \nThe degraded amino acids that contain sulphur such as cysteine, \nmethionine.\n• H3PO4 - phosphoric acid is produced by hydrolysis of phosphoproteins, \nphospholipids, nucleic acids "}, {"page": 15, "text": "3. Buffers"}, {"page": 16, "text": "• Acids create hydrogen ions in the\nsolution (\"proton donors\")\n• The bases bind hydrogen ions in\nsolution with (\"proton acceptors\")\n• Strong acids ionize completely in\nsolution to form H+ and base\n• Weak acids and bases are only\npartially ionized in solutions\n• Buffers are substances that reduce\nthe changes in concentration of H+\n(pH), where H+ added to the solution\nor removed therefrom: It is a weak\nacid and a base.\nWhat is buffer: pH basics\n"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Buffer system and organs\n• Blood:\n• Hydrogen carbonate \n• Hemoglobin\n• Plasma protein\n• Renal buffer:\n• Hydrogen phosphate\n• NH3/NH4\n+ buffer\n• Ca2+ bone buffer\n• Liver\n• Urea vs. glutamine\n• H+/K+ or Cl-/HCO3\n- exchange between extra and intracellular \nfluid \n• The bicarbonate buffer accounts for 53% of all \nbuffers. The second most important buffer is \nhemoglobin (Hemoglobin is an intracellular \nbuffer)\n• extracellular HCO3\n- (53%)\n• intracellular (18% in erythrocytes)"}, {"page": 18, "text": "The bicarbonate buffer: HCO3\n-\nWeak acids:pKa=6.1\n• The bicarbonate buffer is a good buffer because of two:\n• H2CO3 is weak acid\n• A loss of CO2 in the lung\nCarbonic \nanhydrase\nCO2 is removed by \nrespiration leading to \nloss of acids (H2CO3)\nThis reaction is slow unless the enzyme carbonic anhydrase is present. This \nenzyme is abundant in the wall of the lung alveoli, where CO2 is released and \nin renal tubules, where CO2 with H2O reacts and form H2CO3."}, {"page": 19, "text": "The hemoglobin buffer is intracellular buffer\nThe hemoglobin buffer is main non-bicarbonate buffer (less plasmatic \nproteins and phosphates)\nIn tissue, hemoglobin liberates\nO2 and binds H+ that is formed \nby enzymatic (carbonic \nanhydrase) reaction CO2+H2O \n= H+ + HCO3\n-\n(HCO3\n- is release from the red \ncells and Cl- enter red cells to \nkeep neutral el. charge, \nvenous blood has less Cl-) \nIn lung, hemoglobin \nbinds O2 and \nliberates H+ that \nreacts with HCO3\n-\nand forms CO2 and \nH2O . New HCO3\n-\nenter the red cells \nand Cl- exit to keep \nneutral el. charge, \narterial blood has \nmore Cl-) \n• Carbaminohemoglobin \nis a weaker acid \n(pKA=7.8):  Tissue H+\nare buffered by \nhemoglobin.\n• Oxyhemoglobin \nis a stronger \nacid (pKA=6.2)"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Hemoglobin: dissociation curve in acidosis\nAs acidosis progresses, 2,3-diphophoglycerate produced by glycolysis is going down \nand causes left shift\nNormal pH: \namount of \nreleased O2\nAcidosis: left shift: \nmore O2 is \nreleased"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Hemoglobin: dissociation curve in alkalosis\nAlkalosis: ↑ affinity of Hb binds ↑ amount ofO2 that is not released in tissue \nand the cells produce lactate that will compensate respiratory alkalosis\nAlkalosis: ↓ \namount of \nreleased O2 in \ntissue\nNormal pH: \namount of \nreleased O2\n"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Hypoproteinemia (hypoalbuminemia) by itself causes a nonrespiratory \n(“metabolic”) alkalosis.\nBuffering by proteins accounts for two-thirds of the buffering power of the \nblood and most of the buffering within cells.\nThe proteins buffer\npositively charged amino groups and negatively charged carboxyl groups.\nThe plasma proteins buffer accounts for 7% of all buffers"}, {"page": 23, "text": "The liver: a normal source of urea \n• Amino acids are deaminated (removal of an amino group) so that they can be \nprocessed into the Krebs/Citric Acid/TCA cycle.\n• The amino group becomes ammonium and later undergoes the urea cycle to \nbecome urea in the liver.\n• CO2 + NH4\n+ → urea + 2H+ (e.g. Sulphur containing amino acids and \ncationic amino acids → acidification) \n• CO2 + NH4\n+ → urea without 2H+ (neutral amino acid) "}, {"page": 24, "text": "The role of hepatocytes in acid base disturbance:\nproduction of urea or glutamine \n• Urea is produced in less extent in acidosis. Hepatocytes synthetize \nmore glutamine, because its production does not generate H+.\n• NH4\n+ → glutamine synthesis → glutamine \n• Glutamine is taken up by the kidneys. In the kidney H+ is excreted as \nNH4\n+"}, {"page": 25, "text": "Lactic acid in the liver is firstly metabolized to pyruvic acid and then \nconverted to glucose by gluconeogenesis. Both the release of lactic acid \nfrom the muscles and its metabolism into glucose is called the Cori cycle. \nThis complete process results in the equivalent release of an [HCO3−] \nradical.\nThe role of hepatocytes in acid base disturbance:\nthe consumption of excessive lactic acid "}, {"page": 26, "text": "Kidney: normal function\n The kidney excretes H+: normal urine pH 4.5 – 8  and urine anion \ngap is negative.\n The kidney reabsorbed all filtered HCO3\n- .\nIn details: For every H+ ion secreted into \nthe tubular lumen (H+ is actively \nexchanged for Na+), HCO−\n3 enters the \nblood. 90% of filtered HCO−\n3 is \nreabsorbed in the form of CO2 and \nHCO−\n3. H+ normally do not contribute to \nthe urinary excretion of acid or the \naddition of acid to the blood.\n"}, {"page": 27, "text": "The role of kidney in acid base disturbance\n1. Role of proximal tubule:\n1. HCO3\n- reabsorption\n2. Metabolize glutamine\n2. Role of distal tubule: \n1. de novo HCO3\n- formation\n2. renal buffers:\n• The phosphate buffer \naccounts for 5% of all \nbuffers\n• Important in urine due \nto acidic pH and higher \nconcentration\n• NH3/NH4\n+ Cl buffer"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule: \nthe effect of diuretics, hormones and genetic defect\n• Acetazolamide: Inhibits carbonic anhydrase and thus blocks hydrogen ion \nsecretion and reabsorption of sodium and bicarbonate“ leading to increased \ndiuresis and side effects: hypokalemia, hyperchloremic, systemic acidosis, \nalkaline urine\n• Na+/H+ pump: depend on available HCO−3\n• arterial↑pCO2stumulate Na+/H+ pump \n• ↑ angiotenzin II stumulate Na+/H+ pump\n• ↑ Parathormone decrease Na+/H+ pump (hyperparathyroidism leads to m. \nacidosis and alkaline urine)\n• Proximal tubular acidosis: genetic change in Na+/H+ pump: not absorbed \nHCO−3\n• IV with HCO−3 will not help because HCO−3 is no reabsorbed"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule: HCO3\n- reabsorption\n"}, {"page": 30, "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule in metabolic acidosis: \nglutamine and NH4\n+ buffer\nFrom \nthe liver"}, {"page": 31, "text": "Intercalated cells of distal and collecting tubules:  \n• H+ secretion to \nurine to be buffer \nwith NH3 or \nH2PO4\n- (the next \n2 slides) \n• De novo \nHCO3\n-\nproduction \nand secretion \nto blood\n• Cl- enter from \nblood to urine \n(antiporter \nwith HCO3\n-)"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Distal tubule in metabolic acidosis: NaH2PO4 buffer\n• H+\nsecretion to \nurine to be \nbuffered \nwith H2PO4\n-\n"}, {"page": 33, "text": "• H+ secretion to \nurine to be \nbuffered with \nNH3\nCollecting tubule in metabolic acidosis: \nNH4\n+ Cl buffer"}, {"page": 34, "text": "The recycling of NH3\nNH4\n+ from glutamine:\nReabsorbed via Na+2Cl-NH4\n+ in ascending loop\nRelease NH3 that diffuse to interstitium and partially diffuse to descending loop \nwhere NH3 binds H+.\nThe interstitium releases as much NH3 as H+ is in collecting tubules"}, {"page": 35, "text": "principle cells  Intercalated cells  Intercalated cells\nA \nintercalat\ned cells\nsecrete \nH+ into \nthe \ntubular \nlumen \nand \nHCO3\n-\ninto blood \nin \nexchange \nfor Cl-\nThe distal tubule: a role of aldosterone "}, {"page": 36, "text": "Interchangeable anions and cations\n• Interchangeable K+ and H + (two—way mechanism)\n• Acidosis can be associated with hyperkalemia but may lead to \nhypokalemia due to the increased excretion.\n• Alkalosis can be associated with hypokalemia\n• Interchangeable Cl- and HCO3\n-\n• Acidosis can be associated with increased Cl- (hyperchloremic / \nnormal anion gap) or normal Cl- (normochloremic / high anion \ngap)\n• Alkalosis can be hypochloremic\n"}, {"page": 37, "text": "Interchangeable ions in acidosis: K+ and H + \n• Hypoaldosteronism: ↑ plasma K + is not excreted: hyperkalemia (↓aldosterone \ndoes not stimulate its excretion)\n• RTA: ↑ plasma K+ is excreted as negative luminal charge (↓urinary acidification) \nsupport K+ excretion: hypokalemia\n• In high anion gap acidosis (e.g. \nexcess of organic acid in lactic \nacidosis or diabetic acidosis), a \nproton enters cell with anions and \nboth pumps H+/Na+ and Na+/K+ work, \nleading to hypokalemia that can be \nsupported by osmotic diuresis or the \neffect of insulin in diabetic acidosis.\n• In normal anion gap acidosis, both \nintra and extracellular concentrations \nof H+ are elevated and inhibit both \npumps: hyperkalemia"}, {"page": 38, "text": "• Interchangeable Cl- and HCO3-\n• Acidosis can be associated with increased Cl- (hyperchloremic=\nnormal anion gap) or normal Cl- (normochloremic= high anion \ngap)\n• Alkalosis can be hypochloremic\n• Red cells:\n• Nephron:\n• Intercalated cells A: basolateral side\n• Intercalated cells B: apical side\nInterchangeable ions: HCO3\n- and Cl-"}, {"page": 39, "text": "Bone buffer: Calcium\n• Most Ca2+ (99%): in bone as hydroxyapatite (Ca5(PO4)3(OH))\n• Small fraction of Ca2+ circulating within the intracellular (primarily in \nthe mitochondria) and extracellular (in the blood):\n• 50% is bound to plasma proteins, (primarily albumin) and\n• 40% is in the free or ionized form (5.5 to 5.6 mg/dl). \nCa+"}, {"page": 40, "text": "The ionized calcium\nIn states of acidosis, levels of ionized Ca2+ increase. \nWhen alkalosis develops, with an increase in pH, the amount of \nprotein-bound calcium increases and the physiologically active, ionized \nCa2+ level decreases. The decreased concentration of ionized Ca2+ \nmay be great enough to cause symptoms of hypocalcemia, such as \ntetany.\nCa+"}, {"page": 41, "text": "3. Clinical Approach to Acid-Base Disturbances"}, {"page": 42, "text": "It refers to 4 parameters that \nassess acid-base disturbance:\npH\npO2\npCO2\nHCO3\n-\nAstrup"}, {"page": 43, "text": "↑pCO2\n(normal = \n40 mmHg \n(33-44))\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\n(normal = 24 \nmmol/L\n(22-28)\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2 \n(normal = 40 \nmmol/L\n(33-44))\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\n(normal = 24 \nmmol/L\n(22-28))\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\nAstrup"}, {"page": 44, "text": "Henderson-hasselbalch equation: \ncompensation\n• 6.1: Dissociation constant\n• 0.03: Solubility coefficient for CO2\n• There is a simple fraction in the equation. \n• It means that if a numerator (HCO3\n-) is increased, a denominator (CO2) \nalso must increase to keep normal (physiological) pH.\n• It means that if a numerator (HCO3\n-) is decreased, a denominator (CO2) \nalso must decrease to keep normal (physiological) pH."}, {"page": 45, "text": "All simple AB disturbances\nwith their compensations together\n"}, {"page": 46, "text": "↑pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n• Without compensation: \n• Hypoventilation\n• Pickwickian syndrome\n• With compensation after (3-5 \ndays for kidney) : COPD\nRespiratory component with/without \ncompensation: acidosis"}, {"page": 47, "text": "↑pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\nWithout compensation:\n• Hyperventilation\n• staying in high altitude\n• anxiety attack\nWith compensation after (3-5 days for kidney)\nRespiratory component with/without \ncompensation: alkalosis"}, {"page": 48, "text": "↑pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\nWith compensation as the healthy lung can \ncompensate rapidly (6-12 hours)\n• high anion gap = normochloremia\n• normal anion gap= hyperchloremia\nMetabolic component with/without \ncompensation: acidosis"}, {"page": 49, "text": "↑pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\nWith compensation after 6-12 hours for lung\n• Alkalosis with high Cl- loss in urine\n• Alkalosis with lower Cl- loss in urine\nMetabolic component with/without \ncompensation: alkalosis"}, {"page": 50, "text": "↑pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↓HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\n↓ pCO2\nCause: \nlung diseases\n↑HCO3\n-\nCause: renal, liver \nor other diseases\nWith compensation after 6-12 hours \nfor lung\n• Alkalosis with high Cl- loss in \nurine\n• Alkalosis with lower Cl- loss in \nurine\nMetabolic and respiratory component \nwith/without compensations: summary\nWithout compensation:\n• Hyperventilation\n• staying in high \naltitude\n• anxiety attack\nWith compensation after (3-\n5 days for kidney)\nWith compensation as the healthy \nlung can compensate rapidly (6-\n12 hours)\n• high anion gap = \nnormochloremia\n• normal anion gap=\nhyperchloremia\n• Without compensation: \n• Hypoventil\nation\n• Pickwickia\nn\nsyndrome\n• With compensation after \n(3-5 days for kidney) : \nCOPD"}, {"page": 51, "text": "Compensation and correction\nPowerful compensation of \nrespiratory acidosis takes time:\n• Kidney: ↑ pCO2 leads to excess \nof H+ over HCO3\n- in the tubular \nfluid  and the ↑ H+ induce Na+/H+\npump leading to intensive renal \nreabsorption of HCO3 and mainly \nrenal formation of new HCO3\n-\n• Liver: glutamine over urea is \nsynthetized\nCompensation of metabolic acidosis: \n• Fast and less powerful lung: decreases \npCO2 (acids) by hyperventilation\nCorrection of metabolic acidosis:\n• ↓ filtration of HCO3\n- leads to an excess \nof H+ over HCO3\n- in the renal tubular \nfluid and the ↑ H+ induce Na+/H+ pump \nleading to renal formation of new HCO3\n-\nif met. acid is not due to renal diseases\n• ↑  NH4\n+Cl and NaH2PO4 in urine"}, {"page": 52, "text": "Maintenance of the acid-base balance: timetable\n1) Chemical buffering systems\n• “Quick” regulation. Buffers react immediately (ms). Capacity of \nbuffers is not indefinite that is why chemical buffers act only in the \nshort-term. Chemical buffering systems deal with pH deviations in \ncommon metabolism.\n2) Respiratory system “Intermediate”\n• Respiration starts to react in 1-3 minutes. Respiratory system \nregulates PaCO2, max activation after 12 hours\n3) Kidneys (“Slow” )\nReact in hours-days, max activation after 3-5 days.\n4) Myocardium\n• Influences acid-base balance through lactate and ketone bodies \noxidation."}, {"page": 53, "text": "Metabolic acidosis: „ANION- GAP“ \nAG = (Na+ + K +) - [(HCO3\n- + Cl-)] \nAG = 12 +/- 2 mmol/L\nThe AG value represents the difference between measured cations \nand anions, ie, the presence of anions in the plasma that are not \nroutinely measured including:\n• Lactate (anion of lactic acid) in lactic acidosis\n• Phosphates and sulfates in last stage of renal failure\n• Keto bodies (acetoacetic acid, hydroxybutyric acid and acetone) in\nketoacidosis\n• Other anions in ingestion of certain toxins. \n• Albumins\n55"}, {"page": 54, "text": "Metabolic acidosis: with ↑ AG or normal AG\nNa+\nCl-\nA normal AG \nacidosis is \ncharacterized by a \nlowered HCO3\n-\nconcentration, which \nis counterbalanced \nby an equivalent \nincrease in plasma \nCl- concentration \ndue to ↑ renal \nreabsorption\nHigh AG acidosis is \ncharacterized by \n• a lowered HCO3\n-\nconcentration, \nwhich is not \ncounterbalanced \nby an equivalent \nincrease in plasma \nCl- concentration.\n• Accumulation of \nstrong acid anions: \nlactate anion or \nketo bodies\nUA: Unmeasured cations / anions\nHigh Anion Gap \nwithout \nhyperchloremia\nNormal Anion Gap \nwith \nHyperchloremia\nNormal plasma"}, {"page": 55, "text": "1 Buffer base and Buffer excess\n“Buffer base” (BB) is the sum of bicarbonate and non-bicarbonate \nbuffer anions (ie, HCO3\n− + K*albumins +K2*hemoglobin).\nBase excess is the quantity of base (HCO3\n-, in mEq/L) that is above \nor below the normal range of buffer base in the body (22 -28 \nmEq/L)."}, {"page": 56, "text": "2 Buffer base and Buffer excess\n“BE” is defined as the amount of acid or base that must be added in \nvitro to a sample of whole blood to restore the pH to 7.40 while \nPaCO2 is kept constant at 40 mmHg (5.33 kPa). Oxygenated blood \nat PaCO2 40 mmHg (5.33 kPa) and Hb 150 g/L has a BE of zero\nNormal Base excess is between -3 and +3mEq/L.\nNegative base excess (e.g. BE –8 mmol/L) indicates metabolic \nacidosis \nAbnormally positive base excess (e.g. BE +8 mmol/L) indicates \nmetabolic alkalosis."}, {"page": 57, "text": "High Anion Gap without hyperchloremia: causes\nCauses\n• Lactic acidosis (buildup of lactate)\n• Ketoacidosis (DM I type, starvation, alcoholic ketoacidosis)\n• Last stage of chronic renal failure (accumulation of sulfates, \nphosphates, uric acid)\n• Intoxication:\n• Organic acids (salicylates, ethanol, methanol, formaldehyde, \nethylene glycol, paraldehyde, INH, toluene)\n• Sulfates, metformin (Glucophage)\n• Massive rhabdomyolysis"}, {"page": 58, "text": "Delta Ratio\n• Delta Ratio is AG scale\n• Calculation: (AG – 12)/(24 - [HCO3¯])\n• < 0.4 due to NAGMA (normal anion gap metabolic acidosis) \n• 0.4 - 0.8 due to a mixed NAGMA + HAGMA \n• 0.8 - 2.0 due to a pure HAGMA (high anion gap metabolic \nacidosis e.g. Lactic acidosis)\n• >2.0 due to a mixed HAGMA + metabolic alkalosis (or pre-\nexisting compensated respiratory acidosis) (e.g. COPD)"}, {"page": 59, "text": "High Anion Gap without hyperchloremia: mnemonics\n• MUDPILES\n• M-Methanol poisoning\n• U-Uremia\n• D-Diabetic Ketoacidosis\n• P-Paraldehyde\n• I-Infection, Iron, \nIsoniazid\n• L-Lactic acidosis\n• E-Ethylene Glycol\n• S-Salicylates\n GOLDMARK\n◦ G — glycols (ethylene \nglycol & propylene glycol)\n◦ O — oxoproline, a metabolite \nof paracetamol\n◦ L — L-lactate, the chemical \nresponsible for lactic acidosis\n◦ D — D-lactate causes of anion gap \nmetabolic acidosis include\n◦ M — methanol\n◦ A — aspirin\n◦ R — renal failure\n◦ K — ketoacidosis, ketones generated \nfrom starvation, alcohol, and diabetic \nketoacidosis\n KULT is probably the easiest of the mnemonics to use (Ketones, \nUreamia, Lactate, Toxins)."}, {"page": 60, "text": "Normal Anion Gap with hyperchloremia: causes\n• GIT\n• kidney"}, {"page": 61, "text": "• If kidney function is normal, urine\nanion gap negative.\n• Bicarbonate loss from body fluids\nthrough the GI tract, with\nsubsequent relative chloride\nretention: hyperchloremic\nmetabolic acidosis (e.g.\nLongstanding diarrhea, vomiting\nthe content from deeper in the\ngastrointestinal tract). Negative\nurine gap as the kidney\ncompensate by ↑NH4\n+ excretion\nthat are accompanied by ↑Cl-\nexcretion\nurine anion gap AG = ([ Na+] + [ K +]) - [Cl-]\n• If kidney function is not\nnormal, urine anion gap is 0 or\npositive\n• ↓ HCO3\n- reabsorption or ↓H+\nexcretion in the kidney with\nsubsequent Cl- retention:\nhyperchloremic metabolic\nacidosis (e.g. renal tubular\nacidosis (RTA) .Positive or 0\nurine gap as the kidney\nreabsorb Cl-: ↓ urinary\nacidification increases Cl-\nreabsorption\nNormal Anion Gap with hyperchloremia: 2 causes"}, {"page": 62, "text": "Diarrhea: normal AG in blood and urine \nBicarbonate loss from body fluids through the GI tract , with \nsubsequent relative chloride retention: hyperchloremic metabolic \nacidosis with normal AG in the blood: AG = ([ Na+] + [ K +]) - [Cl-]\n+ HCO3\n-]\nNormal (negative) urine gap as the kidney compensate by ↑NH4\n+\nexcretion that are accompanied by ↑Cl- excretion: urine anion gap AG \n= ([ Na+] + [ K +]) - [Cl-]\nHypokalemia may be present: the stool has ↑K+ and low Na+ and low \nCl-"}, {"page": 63, "text": "• Proximal renal tubular acidosis (type \nII): with hyperchloremia and \nhypokalemia. Rare\n• Distal renal tubular acidosis (type I.): \nwith hyperchloremia and hypokalemia. \nRare\n• Renal tubular acidosis (type III):  with \nhyperchloremia and normokalemia but \nlater normochloremia, and \nhyperkalemia. It  is combination of \ntype I and II. Rare\n• Generalized renal tubular acidosis \n(type IV): with hypochloremia and \nhyperkalemia\nRenal tubular acidosis (RTA) \n↓ amount of blood HCO3\n-\nand↑ urinary HCO3\n- leads \nto accumulation of Cl- and \n↑ K+ excretion (due to \nmore negative charge in \ntubular lumen)\nThe renal interstices is \ndamaged by the \ninflammation or decreased \nglomerular filtration: ↑ \nurinary NH4\n+ reabsorbed \nto blood"}, {"page": 64, "text": "Distal renal \ntubular \nacidosis (type \nI.) is caused \nby a failure of \nthe distal \ntubular cells to \nsecrete H+ \ninto lumen of \nnephron.\nProximal renal tubular \nacidosis (type II.) is \ncaused by a failure of \nthe proximal \ntubular cells to \nreabsorb bicarbonate\nfrom the urine. Renal tubular \nacidosis Type 4  \nabsolute \nhypoaldosteronism \nor aldosterone \ninsensitivity\n(principal cells of  \ncollecting ducts)\nAll RTA with normal AG\nRenal tubular acidosis\ntype III. the renal \ninterstitium is damaged \nby the inflammation or \ndecreased glomerular \nfiltration\nAll RTA: ↓ urinary H+ increases Cl-\nreabsorption as luminal el. charge \nis more negative"}, {"page": 65, "text": "Type II: proximal renal tubular acidosis \nis caused by a failure of the proximal tubular cells to reabsorb \nbicarbonate from the urine. There is defect in H+/Na+ antiporter that \nis associated with HCO3\n- reabsorption. Type II is associated with \nhyperchloremia and hypokalemia \n• Hyperchloremia: To keep electric  equilibrium, Cl- are \naccumulated leading to hyperchloremia (*). \n• Hypokalemia: Excess of urinal bicarbonate in distal tubule \nincreases excretion of K+ leading to hypokalemia: Excessive \nurinal bicarbonates bind Na + that is reabsorped by principal cells \nand bicarbonates negative charge stimulate K + excretion "}, {"page": 66, "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule: Type II RTA \nType II is a defect \nin H+/Na+ \nantiporter that is \nassociated with \nHCO3\n-\nreabsorption."}, {"page": 67, "text": "Type I: distal renal tubular acidosis \nis caused by a failure of the distal tubular cells to secrete H+ into \nlumen of nephron. Low H+ in distal lumen decreases bicarbonate \nreabsorption. Type II is associated with hyperchloremia and \nhypokalemia \n• Hyperchloremia: To keep electric  equilibrium, Cl- are \naccumulated leading to hyperchloremia (normal AG). \n• Hypokalemia: Excess of urinal bicarbonate in distal tubule \nincreases excretion of K+ leading to hypokalemia: Excessive \nurinal bicarbonates bind Na + that is reabsorbed by the principal \ncells and bicarbonates negative charge stimulate K + excretion "}, {"page": 68, "text": "Type III: renal tubular acidosis with decreased \nglomerular filtration \nis caused by low ammonia (NH3) in distal tubules. The inflammation of renal \ninterstices or low glomerular filtrations leads to ↓ concentration ability in the \ndescendent Henle loop and less concentrated HCO3\n- do not lead to NH3\nformation. Normally NH3 diffuse to renal interstitium and later collecting \ntubules where it buffer H+. In Type III, the increased urinary NH4\n+ is \nreabsorbed by ascendent Henle loop (Na + /2Cl-/ NH4\n+ ) pump to blood (not \nrenal interstices) and contribute to acidosis. \nType III is caused by \n• ↓ low glomerular filtration: hyperchloremia, normokalemia with normal AG\n• ↓ ↓ low glomerular filtration (uremic end-stage renal failure with \nhyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism and \nosteodystrophy): normochloremia, hyperkalemia with ↑ AG due to \naccumulation of anions of strong acids"}, {"page": 69, "text": "Type III RTA: the recycling of NH3\nThe primary urine \nis made denser \n(more urinary \nHCO3\n-) due to the \nhyperosmolar \ninterstitium. If \nprimary urine is not \nconcentrated, NH3\nis not produce from \nNH4\n+ that \ncontinues to Henle\nloop and is more \nreabsorbed to \nblood via Na+/2Cl-\n/NH4\n+ (K+) pump.\nLow NH3 reach \ncollecting tubules and \nthus urinary H+ is not \nbuffered and high H+\ninhibit H+ pump. \nThe inflammation in interstitium"}, {"page": 70, "text": "Type IV: generalized distal tubular acidosis \nProblem with Na+ resorption and K + and H + excretion due to low \naldosterone. It is associated with high amount of salt in urine, \nhypochloremia and hyperkalemia. AG is normal as hyperchloremia \nis relative one: ↓ aldosterone decreases Na+ and Cl- reabsorption \nbut Na + reabsorption is more affected than Cl - reabsorption. \nType IV is caused by\n• Hypoaldosteronism: aldosterone should stimulate H+ ATPase \n• Relative hypoaldosteronism: due to low renin activity in diabetic \nnephropathy or hypertensive nephrosclerosis"}, {"page": 71, "text": "Hypochloremic alkalosis based on Urinary \nChloride\nUrine chloride < 25 mEq/L\n• often associated with volume depletion (contraction alkalosis)\n• respond to saline infusion\n• common causes:\n✓vomiting gastric content (90% of cases)\n✓alkalotic therapy, such as bicarbonate \nadministrated in peptic ulcer\n✓Diuretic therapy : loop \ndiuretics and thiazide\n✓Cystic fibrosis\nUrine chloride > 40 mEq/L\n✓ often associated with volume expansion and hypokalemia\n✓ resistant to therapy with saline infusion\n✓ causes: excess aldosterone, severe K+ deficiency, current \ndiuretic therapy, Bartter syndrome"}, {"page": 72, "text": "VOMITING\n• acid loss\n• water depletion\n• depletion of ECF sodium, potassium and chloride.\nLow Na+\ninduces \nsodium \nretention \nand \nincreases \nK+ \nexcretion\nLow K+\ninduces H+\nshift toward \nintracellular \nspace \ncontributes \nto alkalosis\nLow Cl-\n• The kidneys increase HCO3\n− reabsorption to maintain an anionic \nbalance because the ECF Cl− concentration is decreased. (Like \nchloride shift: In venous blood, the red cells release Cl− in exchange \nfor HCO3\n−)\n• Low Cl- cannot neutralize the reabsorbed Na+ and more positive \nbasolateral side potentiate the effect of aldosterone supporting K+ \nand H + excretion to urine (hypochloremic alkalosis with paradoxical \nacidification of urine)\nhydrochloric acid in the gastric \njuice\nAldosterone:\n• In the principal cells of the distal tubule aldosterone \nactivates the basolateral Na+/K+ pumps. Cl- follow the \nreabsorbed Na+ to neutralize e. charge.\n• In intercalated cells of collecting tubules aldosterone \nstimulates secretion of H+ via the H+/ATPase. It also \nincreases generation of \"new\" bicarbonate within these \nsame cells, which will be reabsorbed\nTherapy: Cl- must be replenisched in infusion t."}, {"page": 73, "text": "Vomiting: the effect of Cl- loss\nNormal situation:\nprinciple cells (aldosterone): \nNa+ is  absorbed. K+ is \nsecreted. Passive transport of \nCl- follow Na+ reabsorption \nand lumen is less negative \nbut enough negative to \nstimulate H + and K+\nexcretion.\nVomiting: ↓ Cl- potentiate the \neffect of aldosterone \nsupporting K + and H + \nexcretion (intercalated cells): \nhypochloremic alkalosis with \nparadoxical acidification of \nurine) \nTherapy: replenished Cl- stimulate B-intercalated cells to excrete HCO3\n-\nMore negative charge support H+ excretion in hypochloremia\nMore positive charge without Cl-\nprinciple \ncells\n Intercalated\ncells\n Intercalated\ncells\n++\n---"}, {"page": 74, "text": ""}, {"page": 75, "text": "primary or secondary (e.g. due to ↓ oncotic pressure in liver cirrhosis) \nhyperaldosteronism\n• ↑ aldosterone: ↑ renal excretion of K+ causing hypokalemia and ↑ H+\nexcretion causing m. alkalosis and ↑ Na+ reabsorption causing ↑ Cl-\nreabsorption with low urinary Cl- (but higher compare that in vomiting). \n• Hypokalemia is also supported by \n• K + shift from the extracellular space to intercellular space in \nexchange for H +\n• ↑  intracellular K + in renal tubular cells that also support K + \nexcretion\nHyperaldosteronism"}, {"page": 76, "text": "Bartter syndrome (BS) \nmutations involving the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending \nlimb of the loop of Henle. Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport one sodium, one \npotassium, and two chloride ions across the apical membrane of the \ntubule.\n• Primary hypereninism and secondary \nhyperaldosteronism\n• Hypokalemia\n• Hypochloremia \n• Metabolic alkalosis\n• Tendency to hypovolemia\n"}, {"page": 77, "text": "Bartter syndrome (BS) \nAbolishment of the \nhypertonicity of the \nrenal medulla, \nseverely impairing \nthe ability to \nreabsorb water \nlater in the distal \nnephron \nand collecting duct \nsystem, leading to \nnormal extracellular \nvolume with \ntendency to \nhypovolemia.\n↓ Na+, K+, 2Cl-\nreabsorption \nin the Henle loop ↑ urinary Na+ load to the \ndistal nephron\n↑ Na+\nreabsorption\nChronic stimulation \nof macula densa \n(hyperplasia of \nJuxtaglomerular apparatus \n) and renin\n↑ urinary Cl- load to the distal \nnephron and their excretion\n↑ aldosterone\nHypokalemiaH+ ATPase stimulation and H+\nexcretion\nNa+ combines with HCO3\n- as \nrelative ↓ Cl- in urine. HCO3\n-\nare nor reabsorbed and abolish \nthe lumen-positive voltage, \nresulting in decreased calcium \nand magnesium reabsorption\nIntra and extracellular exchange \nbetween K+ and H+Metabolic alkalosis\n↑ PGE2\n79"}, {"page": 78, "text": "genetic mutation in epithelial sodium channel that transports sodium \ninto cells. This mutation leads to loss of ability to be degraded and \nhigh amounts of the channel being chronically present on the \ncollecting duct epithelium.(hyperaldosteronism-like state)\n• high blood pressure\n• low plasma renin, normal to low levels of aldosterone,\n• kidney: excessive reabsorption of Na + and loss of K+ (metabolic \nalkalosis, hypokalemia)\nLiddle's syndrome\nor Liddle syndrome"}, {"page": 79, "text": "Diuretic therapy\n• Loop diuretics: alkalosis (in ascendent loop Na+-K+-2Cl− symporter)\n• Thiazide diuretics: alkalosis (in distal tubules Na+Cl− symporter) \n• (note: inhibitors of carbonic anhydrase induce acidosis)\n"}, {"page": 80, "text": "Diuretic therapy\nLoop diuretics act on the Na+-K+-2Cl− symporter in the thick ascending \nlimb of the loop of Henle to inhibit sodium, chloride and potassium \nreabsorption. \n• Thus more Na+ continue to distal tubule \nwhere the increased Na+ increase \nexcretion of K+and H+ (hypokalemia and \nalkalosis)\n• It also \ninhibits magnesium and calcium reabsor\nption that depends on electric gradient\n• Used in treatment of lung edema: \ndecreased venous return to heart"}, {"page": 81, "text": "Diuretic therapy\n• Thiazide inhibit the sodium-chloride \nsymporter which has the function of \nreabsorbing sodium and chloride ions \ncausing the body to release NaCl and \nwater\n• hypokalemia and alkalosis\n"}, {"page": 82, "text": "Simple (with or without compensation) and \nMixed acid base disturbances\n"}, {"page": 83, "text": "Davenpost diagram\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=snp5oq-hLpg&t=237s\nA shift of the parameters toward \nnormal pH on a diagonal"}, {"page": 84, "text": "Normal pH doesn‘t have to be healthy \nperson.\n"}, {"page": 85, "text": "• Negative inotropic (contractility), and chronotropic (heart rate) \neffect but it is neutralized by catecholamines that are also \nstimulated by acidosis.\n• Inhibit CNS including respiratory centers\n• In systemic vessels, \n• acidosis causes vasodilation in peripheral arterioles but is \nalso offset by catecholamine, so that peripheral vascular \nresistance remains relatively constant. \n• In brain, vasodilation can cause edema.\n• in the venous system, the direct effect of acidosis \nis vasoconstriction, which is further enhanced by \ncatecholamine release: prevent lung edema\n• Osteoporosis : Ca2+ and phosphate liberated from bone \n(hydroxyapatite, Ca5(PO4)3(OH)) \n• Final stage: coma (due to H+-mediated protein denaturation in \nCNS, dehydration, hyperosmolarity in diabetic ketoacidosis)\nAcidemia: general effect"}, {"page": 86, "text": "• Acute alkalosis increases binding and reduces free plasma Ca2+ \nleading to tetany\n• Compensation of MA by hypoventilation is limited by O2\n• In brain, vasoconstriction that decrease its perfusion\n• Final stage: coma or asphyxia\nAlkalemia: general effect"}, {"page": 87, "text": "A 65-year-old man comes to the physician with a 3-hour history of \nshortness of breath after feeling ill for the past week. His BMI is 30 kg/m2. \nHis temperature is 38.3°C (101°F), pulse is 96/min, respirations are \n20/min and shallow, and blood pressure is 145/90 mm Hg.\npH 7.33\nPO2 61 mm Hg\nPCO2 50 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 26 mEq/L\n1. Case"}, {"page": 88, "text": "1. Compensations for Respiratory Acidosis\n• Acute respiratory acidosis\n• HCO3 increases by 1 for every 10 mm increases in pCO2\n• Chronic respiratory acidosis\n• HCO3 increases by 3 for every 10 mm increases in pCO2"}, {"page": 89, "text": "1. Description\npH 7.33\nPO2 61 mm Hg\nPCO2 50 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 26 mEq/L\n1-2 mEq/L increase in HCO3\n- for every 10 mm Hg increase in PCO2.\nPCO2 increase = 50-40 = 10 mm Hg.\nHCO3\n- increase predicted = (1-2) x (10/10) = 1-2 mEq/L\nadd to 24 mEq/L (reference point) = 25-26 mEq/L\nR\nacid\n acute\nHistory suggests hypoventilation, supported by increased PCO2 and lower than \nanticipated PO2.\nRespiratory acidosis (acute) due to no renal compensation."}, {"page": 90, "text": "Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS)\nor Pickwickian Syndrome\n• is defined as a combination of Obesity (BMI>40 kg/m2), daytime \nhypoventilation characterized by hypercapnia and hypoxemia \n(PaCO2 > 45 mm Hg and PaO2 < 70 mm Hg at sea level) and sleep-\ndisordered breathing (SDB) in the absence of an alternative cause \nfor hypoventilation like obstructive or restrictive lung disease, chest \nwall disorders like kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disorders and \ncongenital central hypoventilation"}, {"page": 91, "text": "A 56-year-old woman with COPD is brought to the physician with a 3-\nhour history of shortness of breath. Her temperature is 37°C \n(98.6°F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 22/min and shallow, and \nblood pressure is 135/80 mm Hg.\npH 7.39\nPO2 62 mm Hg\nPCO2 52 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 29 mEq/L\n2. Case"}, {"page": 92, "text": "2. Compensations for Respiratory Acidosis\n• Acute respiratory acidosis\n• HCO3 increases by 1 for every 10 mm increases in pCO2\n• Chronic respiratory acidosis\n• HCO3 increases by 3 for every 10 mm increases in pCO2"}, {"page": 93, "text": "2. Description\npH 7.39\nPO2 62 mm Hg\nPCO2 52 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 29 mEq/L\n3-4 mEq/L increase in HCO3\n- for every 10 mm Hg increase in PCO2.\nPCO2 increase = 52-40 = 12 mm Hg.\nHCO3\n- increase predicted = (3-4) x (12/10) = 4-5 mEq/L\nadd to 24 mEq/L (reference point) = 28-29 mEq/L\nHistory suggests hypoventilation, supported by increased PCO2.\nRespiratory acidosis (chronic) with renal compensation.\nR\nacid\n chronic"}, {"page": 94, "text": "A 17-year-old woman is brought to the physician with a 3-hour history \nof epigastric pain and nausea.  She admits taking a large dose of \naspirin.  Her respirations are 20/min and full.\npH 7.55\nPO2 104 mm Hg\nPCO2 25 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 22 mEq/L\n3. Case"}, {"page": 95, "text": "3. Compensations for Respiratory Alkalosis\n• Acute respiratory alkalosis\n• HCO3 decreases by 1-2 for every 10 mm decrease in pCO2\n• Chronic respiratory alkalosis\n• HCO3 decreases by 4 for every 10 mm decrease in pCO2"}, {"page": 96, "text": "3. Description\npH 7.55\nPO2 104 mm Hg\nPCO2 25 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 22 mEq/L\n1-2 mEq/L decrease in HCO3\n- for every 10 mm Hg decrease in PCO2.\nPCO2 decrease = 40-25 = 15 mm Hg.\nHCO3\n- decrease predicted = (1-2) x (15/10) = 2-3 mEq/L\nsubtract from 24 mEq/L (reference point) = 21-22 mEq/L\nR\nalk\n acute\nHistory suggests hyperventilation, supported by decreased PCO2.\nRespiratory alkalosis (acute) due to no renal compensation."}, {"page": 97, "text": "A 81-year-old woman with a history of anxiety is brought to the \nphysician with a 2-day history of shortness of breath.  She has been \nliving at 9,000 ft elevation for the past 1 month.  Her respirations are full \nat 20/min.\npH 7.44\nPO2 69 mm Hg\nPCO2 24 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 16 mEq/L\n4. Case"}, {"page": 98, "text": "4. Compensations for Respiratory Alkalosis\n• Acute respiratory alkalosis\n• HCO3 decreases by 1-2 for every 10 mm decrease in pCO2\n• Chronic respiratory alkalosis\n• HCO3 decreases by 4 for every 10 mm decrease in pCO2"}, {"page": 99, "text": "4. Description\npH 7.44\nPO2 69 mm Hg\nPCO2 24 mm Hg\nHCO3\n- 16 mEq/L\n4-5 mEq/L decrease in HCO3\n- for every 10 mm Hg decrease in PCO2.\nPCO2 decrease = 40-24 = 16 mm Hg.\nHCO3\n- decrease predicted = (4-5) x (16/10) = 6-8 mEq/L\nsubtract from 24 mEq/L (reference point) = 16-18 mEq/L\nR\nalk\n chronic\nHistory suggests hyperventilation, supported by decreased PCO2.\nRespiratory alkalosis (chronic) with renal compensation."}, {"page": 100, "text": "A 75-year-old man with severe congestive heart failure is brought to the \nemergency department.  He takes none of his prescribed medications.  \nHis respirations are 24/min and blood pressure is 80/50 mm Hg.  He \nhas decreased urine output; his baseline creatinine concentration has \nbeen 1.6 mg/dL.\npH 7.19 Na+ 135 mEq/L\nPO2 80 mm Hg K+ 4.0 mEq/L\nPCO2 21 mm Hg Cl- 97 mEq/L\nHCO3\n- 8 mEq/L CO2, total 8 mEq/L\nLactate 20 mEq/L Urea 54 mg/dL\nCreatinine 2.5 mg/dL\n5. Case"}, {"page": 101, "text": "5. Compensations for Metabolic Acidosis\n• Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation \n• 1.2 mm Hg decrease in PCO2 for every 1 mEq/L decrease in HCO3\n-."}, {"page": 102, "text": "5. Description\npH 7.19 Na+ 135 mEq/L\nPO2 80 mm Hg K+ 4.0 mEq/L\nPCO2 21 mm Hg Cl- 97 mEq/L\nHCO3\n- 8 mEq/L CO2, total 8 mEq/L\nLactate 20 mEq/L Urea 54 mg/dL\nAG = 135-97-8=30 mEq/L Creatinine 2.5 mg/dL\nDR = (30-12)/(24-8) = 1.1\n1.2 mm Hg decrease in PCO2 for every 1 mEq/L decrease in HCO3\n-.\nHCO3\n- decrease = 24-8  = 16 mEq/L \nPCO2 decrease predicted = 1.2 x 16 = 19 mm Hg.\nsubtract from 40 mm Hg (reference point) = 21 mm Hg\nM\nacid\n increased AG\nHistory suggests congestive heart failure (poor perfusion).\nMetabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation."}, {"page": 103, "text": "Strong Ion Difference (SID)\n• Another approach to assess acid-base disturbance that is based on the \ndifference between the concentrations of strong cations and strong anions. \n(Astrup measures pH, HCO3\n- and pCO2) \n• SID = [strong cations] – [strong anions]\n• apparent SID = SIDa = (Na+ + K+ + Ca2+ + Mg2+) – (Cl– + L-lactate + urate)\n• In normal human plasma the SID is 42 mEq/L\n• the number of positive and negative ions in a solution must be equal (SID = 0), \nso there are unmeasured anions\n• increased SID (>0) leads to alkalosis (increase in unmeasured anions)\n• decreased SID (<0) acidosis"}, {"page": 104, "text": "References\nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\n• Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer \nHealth | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \n• McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, \n2018. \n• Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. \n• https://pfyziol.upol.cz/ - selected text passages (according to \nteachers' recommendations) "}]}, {"id": "dka", "title": "E5 Diabetic ketoacidosis ZN", "file": "/lectures/dka.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Diabetic ketoacidosis"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Diabetic ketoacidosis\n• Serious complication of diabetes mellitus due to an absolute or \nrelative deficiency of insulin and an increase in the levels of insulin \ncounterregulatory hormones (glucagon)\n• This is most common in individuals with type 1 diabetes but can \noccur in those with type 2 diabetes as well. Younger age is also a \nrisk factor.\n• The most common precipitating factor for DKA is intercurrent illness, \nsuch as infection, trauma, surgery, or myocardial infarction.\n• Excess production of keto acids: (acetoacetic acid, hydroxybutyric\nacid and acetone) "}, {"page": 3, "text": "Ketone bodies, an alternative source of \nenergy \n• Physiology: \n– They are produced from acetyl coenzyme A mainly in the \nmitochondria within hepatocytes and used peripherally as an \nenergy source when glucose is not readily available. (e.g. The \nbrain cannot utilize fatty acids for energy when blood glucose levels \nbecome compromised.)\n– They are always present in the blood and their levels increase \nduring fasting and prolonged exercise. \n• Diabetic ketoacidosis:\n– A high concentration and it is  a cause of metabolic acidosis with \nhigh AG.\n– Fatty acids are degraded by β-oxidation faster than the formed \nacetyl coenzyme A can enter the Krebs cycle. Excess acetyl \ncoenzyme A gives ketone bodies"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Insulin and glucagon\n• Physiology: \n– Insulin \n• stimulates glycogenesis and glycolysis \n• inhibits lipolysis, gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, \n– Glucagon\n• increases blood glucose level by stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver\n• Diabetic ketoacidosis:\n– insulin deficiency:\n• lipolysis is enhanced and there is an increase in the amount of fatty acids \ndelivered to the liver. \n– The consequence is increased gluconeogenesis (generation \nof glucose from certain non-carbohydrate carbon substrates) \ncontributing to hyperglycemia. \n– increases production of ketone bodies (beta-oxidation generates one \nacetyl CoA for every removal of a two-carbon group from the fatty acid \nchain. ) by the mitochondria of the liver at a rate that exceeds peripheral \nuse. \n– Increased glucagon \n• contribute to activation of gluconeogenesis"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Clinical manifestation\n• Polyuria and dehydration result from the osmotic diuresis due to \nhyperglycemia and ↑glucose in urine.\n• Although the serum K+ concentration may appear normal or elevated \nbecause of volume contraction (there is not a shift of K + out of the cell into \nthe blood caused by metabolic acidosis with ketone bodies), there is the \ntotal body deficiency of K+ due to:\n– polyuria \n– ↑ urinary anion of hydroxybutyric acid: It is not reabsorbed and its neg. charge attracts K+\nto urine) \n– shift K+ to renal tubular cells support K+ excretion.\n• Others: Kussmaul respirations with acetone odor on the breath, postural \ndizziness, central nervous system depression, ketonuria, anorexia, \nnausea, abdominal pain and thirst. \n• Treatment: intravenous administration of\n– insulin to decrease glucose levels\n– fluids to replace lost fluid volume. \n– K+, Na+and phosphorus\n• After the administration of insulin, the concentration of β-hydroxybutyrate \npromptly begins to decrease, and after a slight increase, acetoacetate \nconcentration also begins to decrease. "}, {"page": 6, "text": "Davenpost diagram\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=snp5oq\n-hLpg&t=237s\nA shift of the parameters toward \nnormal pH on a diagonal"}]}, {"id": "edema", "title": "E4 Edema + infusion therapy +cerebral edema ZN", "file": "/lectures/edema.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Pathophysiology of edemas, infusion therapy and \ncerebral edema\n1"}, {"page": 2, "text": "2\n1. Pathogenesis of swelling\n1. Basics: Starling capillary forces \n2. Edema: the classification based on cause (4 forms)\n1. Increased hydrostatic pressure\n1. Venous pressure increase\n1. Generalized:\n1. Right congestive heart failure\n2. Cirrhosis of the liver\n2. Local:\n1. Venous insufficiency \n1. Phlebothrombosis\n2. Superficial thrombophlebitis \n3. Tumor oppression\n2. Kidney water and salt retention\n1. Acute or chronic renal failure with water and salt retention \n3. Arteriolar dilatation\n2. Decreased oncotic pressure\n1. Nephrotic syndrome (3.5g/day)\n2. Hemorrhage (profuse bleeding)\n3. Serious drainage from open wounds, burn,\n4. Enteropathy\n5. Malnutrition (Kwashiorkor)\n6. Liver cirrhosis and other causes of ascites\n3. Lymphatic edema \n4. Inflammation\n3. Other classifications of edema\n4. Other edemas: anasarca, myxedema, angioedema (Quincke’s edema)\n2. Infusion therapy \n3. Cerebral edema\n4. Some swellings in the diagrams\nContent"}, {"page": 3, "text": "3\n• Edema:\n1. Interstitial edema is a disturbance of starling capillary forces = \nCapillary  interstitium exchange (note: It is not same as Frank–\nStarling law). This presentation is primarily about interstitial edema, \nshortly edema.\n2. Cellular edema: swollen or shrinkage of red cells in the states of  \nhyperosmolality or hypoosmolality (mainly: Na+)\nPathogenesis of swelling"}, {"page": 4, "text": "edema\nIncreased \nhydrostatic \npressure \nDecreased \noncotic \npressure \nIncreased \npermeability \n(inflammatory edema)\nLymphatic \ndrainage\n4\n1. Edema: classification based on cause\nA: Venous pressure increase \nB: Kidney water and salt retention\nC: Arteriolar dilation"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Basics: Starling capillary forces \nCapillary  interstitium exchange\nΔPhydrost\nΔPonc\n90%\n10%\nArterial \nend\nVenous \nend\n5\nLymphatic drainage\nCapillary permeability"}, {"page": 6, "text": "1. Edema: classification based on cause\nIncreased hydrostatic pressure A\nA: Venous pressure increase\nGeneralized:\n• Right congestive heart failure: venous obstruction causes the increased \nhydrostatic pressure behind the obstruction and swelling in the legs, ankles, \nand liver\n• Cirrhosis of the liver\nLocal:\n• Venous insufficiency \n• Phlebothrombosis = deep vein thrombosis (or the presence of a clot \nwithin a vein, unassociated with inflammation due to Virchow's \ntriad) may lead to pulmonary embolism\n• Superficial thrombophlebitis: phlebitis = inflammation of a superficial \nvein (e.g. skin infection or varicose veins) has preceded the formation of \na thrombus and may lead to varicose veins \n• Tumor oppression\n6"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Ascites: liver cirrhosis\nLiver cirrhosis\n Proteosynthesis\nHypoalbuminaemia\n ΔPonc\nPortal\nhypertension\npcap in hepatic \nsinusoids and fluid \nleakage to the liver \ninterstitium and later \nvia the liver surface\nALDS\ninactivation\nNa+ and H2O \nretention\nAscites\nAccumulation of water in \nthe in the abdomen \n(peritoneal cavity)\n IV\nVicious circle\n7\n• Accumulation of water in the in the abdomen (peritoneal cavity)\n• the most common cause is liver cirrhosis"}, {"page": 8, "text": "Ascites results from transudation from hepatic and peritoneal veins. \nIt is infrequently caused by \nCongestive heart failure. An increase in the preload or central venous \npressure due to right ventricular dysfunction may cause direct liver \ndamage due to increasing retrograde pressure to the venous and \ncapillaries into the liver. Massive ascites in the setting of heart disease \noccurs most frequently in patients with tricuspid valve disease and \nconstrictive pericarditis\nNephrotic syndrome\nPancreatitis\n13\nAscites: other causes"}, {"page": 9, "text": "16\nPitting edema of the legs \nand ankle swelling\nPitting edema: Congestive heart failure \n"}, {"page": 10, "text": "18\nUlcus crurus venosum vs deep vein thrombosis \n"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Varicose veins are \nsuperficial veins\nChronic venous \ninsufficiency\nUlcus crurus venosum due to Ischemia in the \nskin\nLocal edema: Chronic venous insufficiency\nBlood flow from \nsuperficial vein to \ndeep vein (to \nheart) mainly at \ncalf muscle \ncontraction\nBlood flow from \ndeep to superficial \nvein (back flow) \nmainly at calf \nmuscle contraction\nPigmentation due to leaked erythrocytes\nFirst edema due to ↑ hydrostatic pressure and \nlater microangiopathy die to increased \npermeability\nNote: thrombus formation \nin deep veins: \nPhlebothrombosis\nSuperficial \nthrombophle\nbitis\n↑risk\n↑ risk for lung embolization"}, {"page": 12, "text": "1. Edema: classification based on cause\nIncreased hydrostatic pressure B and C\nB: Kidney water and salt retention\n• Acute or chronic renal failure with water and salt retention (nephritic \nsyndrome)\nC: Arteriolar dilatation\n• Drugs, toxins, anaphylaxis, inflammation, sympathetic NS insufficiency \nor excessive heating or fever\n22\n! In arterial hypertension → constriction of arterioles→ capillary hydrostatic \npressure NORMAL (except cerebral edema in hypertensive crisis)"}, {"page": 13, "text": "1. Edema: classification based on cause: oncotic \npressure, lymphatic edema and inflammation\n• Decreased oncotic pressure\n• Nephrotic syndrome (3.5g/day)\n• Hemorrhage (profuse bleeding)\n• Serious drainage from open wounds, burn,\n• Enteropathy\n• Malnutrition (Kwashiorkor)\n• Liver diseases (cirrhosis)\n• Lymphatic edema: (local edema): tumor, infection or obstruction block \n• Inflammatory edema: increased permeability of capillary membrane\n(exudate: more proteins in the inflammation)\n24"}, {"page": 14, "text": "2. Classification based on extent\n• Local/asymmetric edema: local edema \ndo not activate hormones\n• Generalized/systemic edema: usually \nextracellular e.g. anasarca\nOther classifications of edema \n25\n4. Classification based on a role of kidney \n• Primary edema: accumulation of Na+ due to renal diseases „theory of \noverflow“\n• Secondary: adaptive response ( secondary activated RAAS) of kidney \n„theory of refilling“ e.g. The shift of fluid from the blood to the\ninterstitium leading to secondary RAAS activation in ascites.\n3. Classification based localization\n• Intracellular: SIADH swollen red cell due \nto hyponatremia X shriveled cells\n• Extracellular: ascites"}, {"page": 15, "text": "26\nOther edemas\n• Anasarca\n• Myxedema\n• Angioedema (Quincke’s edema)"}, {"page": 16, "text": "a severe and generalized form of edema, with subcutaneous tissue \nswelling throughout the body and can involve the cavities of the body \nin addition to the tissues.\nIt reflects a severe disease state:\nLiver failure\nKidney failure\nRight-sided heart failure\nMalignancy\nSevere protein deficiency (decreased oncotic pressure)\nNephrotic syndrome\nprotein-losing enteropathies\nCapillary leak syndrome\nAnasarca\n27"}, {"page": 17, "text": "• a term used synonymously with severe hypothyroidism.\n• refers to deposition of mucopolysaccharides in the dermis, \nwhich results in swelling of the affected area\n• firm inelastic edema (opposite to pitting edema)\nMyxedema\n28"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Angioedema (Quincke’s edema)\nAn allergic reaction: histamine\nAn non-pitting and non-inflammatory edema of the lower layer of \nskin and tissue just under the skin or mucous membranes. The \nswelling may occur in the face (little supporting connective \ntissue), tongue, larynx (stridor), abdomen, or arms and legs. \nOften it is associated with hives (urticaria) and pruritus\nOther causes: an inherited C1-inhibitor protein deficiency \n(continuous activation of the complement system and kallikrein): \nbradykinin\nSwollen eyelids, lips tongue\n 29"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Infusion therapy (IV)\nCrystalloids and colloids are plasma volume expanders used to increase a \ndepleted circulating volume.\nCrystalloid solutions are isotonic plasma volume expanders that contain \nelectrolytes.\n• sodium chloride 0.9% (normal saline 0.9%) (a side-effect of the excessive use: hyperchloraemic acidosis) \n• in isotonic extracellular dehydration\n• in sodium depletion \n• as vehicle or diluent of compatible drugs \n• sodium lactate solutions (Ringer’s lactate solution = Hartmann’s solution) \n• in fluid resuscitation after blood loss\n• in metabolic acidosis: metabolic conversion of Ringer’s lactate (no lactic acid in IV) to bicarbonate\n• glucose solutions \n• in dehydration\n• in malnutrition to prevent ketosis, \n• carrier and diluent for parenteral nutrition (a side-effect of the excessive use: hyponatraemia or polyuria)\n• additives such as potassium in hypokalaemia\nColloids are gelatinous solutions that maintain a high osmotic pressure in \nthe blood. Colloids (=particles) stay in the intravascular spaces longer than \ncrystalloids. side-effects can be hypertension, tachycardia, cardiac failure, \npulmonary and peripheral oedema or an increased risk of anaphylaxis\n• Albumin in cirrhosis and ascites, \n• Dextran antithrombotic (antiplatelet), to reduce blood viscosity, and as a volume expander in hypovolaemia\n• Dydroxyethyl starch (or hetastarch):\n30"}, {"page": 20, "text": "• Classification I\n• Local: local infection\n• Generalized: severe hyponatremia or hyponatremia fast onset\n• Classification II\n• Intracellular: cytotoxic cerebral edema, some osmotic cerebral edema\n• Interstitial cerebral edema: hydrostatic, vasogenic, hydrocephalic, some osmotic \ncerebral edema\nCerebral edema: classification I and II\n31"}, {"page": 21, "text": "• A single mechanism or, more commonly, a combination of several \nmechanisms leads to brain edema.\n• Ionic (osmotic) cerebral edema\n• Main mechanism is the disruption of osmolarity: osmotically \nactive particles\n• Vasogenic cerebral edema\n• Main mechanisms is a disruption of the blood-brain barrier \n• Cytotoxic cerebral edema\n• Any mechanism leading to low ATP\n• Interstitial (hydrocephalic) cerebral edema\n• Main mechanism is an obstruction to the outflow of cerebrospinal \nfluid\n• Hydrostatic cerebral edema\n• Main mechanism is ↑blood pressure\n• Other combined types of cerebral edema: e.g. High-altitude cerebral \nedema\nCerebral edema: classification II\n32"}, {"page": 22, "text": "• The intact blood-brain.\n• Improper administration of intravenous fluids, isotonic or \nhypotonic.\n• Excessive water intake, SIADH, hyponatremis\n• Rapid correction of\n• ↑ Na+ in hypernatremia\n• ↑ blood glucose in diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar \nhyperglycemic state.\nIonic (Osmotic) Cerebral edema\n33"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Hypernatremia implies a plasma sodium level above\n145 mEq/L (145 mmol/L) and characterized cellular dehydration.\n• Acute hypernatremia: it has been present for 48 hours or less.\nFast intervention or the exsiccation of the nerve cells\n• Chronic hypernatremia: it has been present for longer than 48 hours.\nSlow intervention or brain edema\nHyponatremia represents a plasma sodium concentration below 135 mEq/L \n(135 mmol/L) and characterized cellular hyperhydration.\n.\n• acute hyponatremia: it has developed over a period of less than 48 \nhours, \n• Fast intervention or fatal herniation (foramen magnum)\n• chronic hyponatremia: it has been present for more than 48 hours\n• Slow intervention or osmotic demyelination syndrome\nThe cerebral edema during hypernatremia and \nhyponatremia I\n34\nNa+"}, {"page": 24, "text": "BRAIN ADAPTATION TO SEVERE CHRONIC HYPERNATREMIA\n• Endogenous/idiogenic osmoles (The brain can produce more particles \nfrom its cellular metabolism):  taurine, glycine, glutamine, sorbitol and \ninositol and urea.\n• Fast intervention: cerebral edema \nBRAIN ADAPTATION TO HYPONATREMIA:\n• increased intracranial pressure forces interstitial sodium and water out \nof the brain into the cerebrospinal fluid, ameliorating the increased \nbrain volume. In addition, astrocytes begin to lose intracellular solutes, \nprincipally potassium and organic osmolytes.\n• Fast intervention: osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS)\nHypernatremia and hyponatremia II\n35\nNa+"}, {"page": 25, "text": "1. On admission to hospital, profound dehydration and hypotension associated with a \nhyperglycaemic crisis will require stabilisation of blood pressure by rapid fluid \ninfusion in preference to slow fluid administration as directed in guidelines. Thus, \naggressive resuscitation begins with the administration of 1-1.5 L of saline in the \nfirst hour and should continue until blood pressure is stabilized (Step 1). It is \nimperative that insulin not be started during Step 1, because the addition of insulin \nduring rapid fluid administration can cause unpredictable changes in blood glucose \nand serum osmolality. \n2. Once the patient is adequately resuscitated, insulin should be started......\n1. Insulin crosses the blood-brain barrier, but insulin is not required for glucose \ntransport to most brain cells. \n2. Insulin lowers glycemia because insulin stimulates GLUT4-mediated glucose \nutilization by skeletal cells and adipose tissue. The drop in blood glucose is \ntransmitted to the cerebral fluid with a delay (blood-brain barrier) and creates \nan osmotic gradient between the blood and the brain leading to interstitial \ncerebral edema.\n1. GLUT1 in glia and brain endothelial cells is independent of insulin\n2. GLUT3 in most neurons is independent of insulin\n3. GLUT4 in a few selected nuclei but common in adipose tissue, skeletal \nand cardiac cells is sensitive to insulin\nCerebral Edema during correction of hyperglycemic \ncrises"}, {"page": 26, "text": "• Vasogenic: a disruption of the blood-brain barrier leading to the\nincreased permeability of the capillary endothelium and accumulation of \nprotein and fluid in the extracellular space.\n• The blood–brain barrier consists \nof astrocytes and pericytes joined together with adhesion proteins \nproducing tight junctions\n• This is the most common form of cerebral edema\n• Examples:\n• Infections: meningitis\n• Inflammatory central nervous system: multiple sclerosis\n• Brain hemorrhage\n• Return of blood flow after an ischemic stroke (cytotoxic \nedema) can cause oxidative stress leading to dysfunction of the \nendothelial cells and disruption of the blood-brain barrier\n• Hypertensive encephalopathy\nVasogenic cerebral edema\n38"}, {"page": 27, "text": "• Cerebral ischemia\n• Low ATP leading to low function of Na+/K+ pump, cellular retention of Na+, \nCl- and water accumulation \n• Examples:\n• intracerebral hemorrhage \n• ischemic stroke\n• acute liver failure: Ammonia accumulates in the blood stream and \ncrosses the blood–brain barrier. Hyperammonemia in central \nnervous system (CNS) cells causes oxidative\nstress and mitochrondrial dysfunction, leading to damaged function \nof Na+/K+ pump and astrocytic cell swelling. Additionally, ammonia \nis converted to glutamine in CNS cells which acts as \nan osmolyte and draws further water into the cell through osmosis.\nCytotoxic Cerebral edema\n39"}, {"page": 28, "text": "• A complication of diabetes type I in children\n• Ketoacidosis \n• A state of near absolute insulinopenia\n• A higher increase in counter regulatory hormones that support \ngluconeogenesis\n• Sever state  of DKA can leads to combination of cytotoxic and \nvasogenic edema. Osmotic edema is less likely as the edema in \nDKA does not have to develop after fluid replacement like osmotic \nedema in pure hyperglycemia. On the other hand, hyperglycemia \ncontributes to cellular injury by inducing dehydration a low \nperfusion\nCerebral edema in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)\n40"}, {"page": 29, "text": "• Hydrocephalus: an excess of fluid within the cerebral ventricles\n• There is an obstruction to the outflow of cerebrospinal fluid within \nthe ventricular system. The obstruction creates a rise in the \nintraventricular pressure and causes CSF to flow through the wall of the \nventricles into the extracellular fluid within brain\nInterstitial (hydrocephalic) cerebral edema\n41"}, {"page": 30, "text": "It happens because the rapid fall in plasma urea concentration produced by \nhemodialysis in a uremic patient. In this setting, the removal of plasma urea \noccurs more quickly than urea can equilibrate across the hemo-cerebral \nbarrier (delayed in the brain due to hemo-cerebral barrier), which promotes \nosmotic water movement into the brain. In the brain, the water shift can result \nin cerebral edema and acute neurologic dysfunction, changes that partially \nexplain the dialysis disequilibrium syndrome\n42\nThe cerebral edema during the dialysis \ndisequilibrium syndrome\n"}, {"page": 31, "text": "The cerebral edema during the dialysis \ndisequilibrium syndrome\n2 hypotheses:\n– “Reverse Urea Effect” \n• The urea concentration in the CNS remains elevated because of its \nslower diffusion from the CNS to the blood than the diffusion of urea \nfrom the blood into the dialysate compartment.\n• Magnetic resonance imaging suggests interstitial edema and not \nintracellular (cytotoxic) edema: brain extracellular water increases \nand/or intracellular water decreases after hemodialysis\n– “Idiogenic Osmoles” may contribute to “Reverse Urea Effect”\n• “Idiogenic Osmoles” hypothesis refers to idiogenic osmoles being \nresponsible for cytotoxic edema.\n• Uremic toxins themselves could be the “idiogenic osmoles.” These \nare questions that remain unanswered at the current time.\n43\n"}, {"page": 32, "text": "• An intact blood-brain\n• Hypertensive encephalopathy occurs when pressure exceeds the upper \nlimit of cerebral autoregulation, which normally maintains cerebral blood \nflow constant over a range of MAP from 60 to 150 mm Hg.\n• Autoregulation:\n• ↑MAP leads to vasoconstriction in the brain\n• ↓MAP leads to vasodilatation in the brain\n• Examples:\n• Severe arterial hypertension: about 1% of patients with \nhypertension will develop a hypertensive crisis (defined as a \nsystolic pressure 180 mmHg or a diastolic pressure of \n110mmHg).\n• Preeclampsia\n• Eclampsia is a seizure of CNS leading to hypoxic cerebral edema. \nIt starts as pre-eclampsia and can be accompanied by tonic-clonic\nseizure with progression to coma. Pre-eclampsia presents with \nthree main features: new onset of high blood pressure, protein in \nthe urine and edema. Pathophysiology is not clear but there is \ndecreased level of placental vasodilators compared to normal \nplacenta.\nHydrostatic cerebral edema\n44"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Cerebral edema: manifestation\nIntracranial Hypertension (ICH)\n• defined as ICP greater than 20 mm Hg (normal range: 5–10 mm Hg)\n• defined by volume of brain, blood and cerebral fluid\n• Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) - Intracranial Pressure (ICP) = \nCerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP)\n• poor effect of mean arterial pressure due to autoregulation:\n• ↑MAP leads to vasoconstriction in the brain\n• ↓MAP leads to vasodilatation in the brain\nStages:\n• 100% compensation:  due to ↓production and ↑resorption of cerebrospinal \nfluid (liquor)\n• Decreased compensation: ↓ blood volume in the brain, unchanged brain \nperfusion due to ↑blood pressure\n• Decompensation: ↑blood pressure but heart rate ↓, ischemia that ↑ CO2 \nleading to vasodilatation that even more ↑ ICH, herniation 45"}, {"page": 34, "text": "46\n• Central tentorial herniation\n• In central herniation, the diencephalon and parts of the temporal lobes of both \nof the cerebral hemispheres are squeezed through a notch in the tentorium\n(dura mater) cerebelli (tentorial opening).\n• Tonsillar herniation (downward cerebellar herniation)\n• The occipital conus is a herniation of the cerebellar tonsil through the foramen \nmagnum. There is compression of the medulla oblongata.\nHerniation\n"}, {"page": 35, "text": "Cerebral edema and other causes of Intracranial \nHypertension \n• Cerebral edema (common): \n• Direct damage:↑ diffusion length\n• Indirect damage: compression\n• Bleeding \n• Epidural (extradural) \nhematomas: an artery is the \nsource of bleeding in 85%\n• Subdural hematomas: veins\n• Subarachnoid hematomas: \nrupture of aneurysms\n• Intracerebral hematomas: \ncombination of hypertension \n(note: single no due to \nautoregulation MAP do not affect \nblood flow)) and arteriosclerosis\n• Intraventricular: bleeding into the \nbrain's ventricular system\n• Cancer\n• Liquor circulation disruption\n47\n"}, {"page": 36, "text": "References \n• Look up the particular theme in the following indexes\n• Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer \nHealth | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \n• McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, \n2018. \n• Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. "}]}, {"id": "sodium-potassium", "title": "E4 Disorders of Na, K balance ZN", "file": "/lectures/sodium-potassium.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Ion balance: content \n• K+: ECG\n• Na+: osmolarity\n• Ca2+: tetany and renal osteodystrophy\n• Parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcitonin\n• Phosphates: Renal osteodystrophy\n• Mg 2+:\n1"}, {"page": 2, "text": "2\n3Na+/2K+ ATPase pump\nExtracellular fluid: \nplasma\nIntracellular fluid\nElectrolytes mmol/L Electrolytes mmol/L\nCations Cations\nNa+ 135-145 K+ 150\nK+ 3.5-5.5 Mg+ 40\nCa+ 5 Na+ 10\nMg+ 2\nAnions Anions\nCl- 103 Phosphates and sulfates 150\nHCO3\n- 26 HCO3\n- 10\nHPO4\n2- 2 Proteinate 40\nHSO4\n- 1\nOrganic acids 5\nProteinate 17\nApproximate major electrolyte content in body fluid\n"}, {"page": 3, "text": "3\nK+\nK+ \nproximal \ntubules K+\nascendent \nloop\nK+\n• Renal excretion of K+ is regulated by aldosterone that is stimulated by \nRAAS, ↑ blood K+ and ↑ Na+ in urine. \n• Aldosterone activates Na+/K+ ATPase pump on basolateral \nmembrane. It ↑ intracellular K+ that is excreted to urine. It also ↓ \nintracellular Na+ that is replaced by Na+ from urine.\nThe distal tubules\nK+: a role of aldosterone\n↑ urinary Na+"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Hyperkalemia (above 5.5 mmol/L): causes\n• Intra-extracellular shift: \n• Acidemia but final excretion leads to hypokalemia\n• Low insulin\n• Rhabdomyolysis\n• Decreased renal loss\n• Hypocortisolism: Addison disease (low aldosterone)\n• Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism in diabetic nephropathy or \ninterstitial nephritis\n• Acetylsalicylic acid that inhibits prostaglandin and renin\n• Kidney failure in last stage. Chronic renal insufficiency (K+ shift from \nintracellular space to extracellular space and the kidney cannot \nexcrete K+) \n5\n↑K+"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Hyperkalemia (above 5.5 mmol/L): manifestation\n• Neuromuscular : muscle weakness, muscle twitching, paresthesia\n(prickling sensation)\n• ECG: Bradyarrhythmia: sinus bradycardia, high-grade AV block with \nslow junctional and ventricular escape rhythms\n6\n↑K+"}, {"page": 6, "text": "ECG hyperkalemia-fast repolarization\n• Potassium: above 5.5 mmol/L\n7\n↑K+"}, {"page": 7, "text": "ECG hyperkalemia: stages\n8\n↑K+\n• Severe \nhyperkalemia:\n• Sine wave \nappearance\n• Ventricular \nfibrillation\n• Asystole\n• Mild hyperkalemia may not be associated with any acute symptoms and \nvital signs may be normal.\n• Moderate hyperkalemia may present clinically with acute extremity \nparesthesia and bradyarrhythmias (↑K + decreases Na+ influx), e.g.sinus \nbradycardia.\nSine wave = QRS and T wave fuse each other"}, {"page": 8, "text": "9\nECG hyperkalemia↑K+"}, {"page": 9, "text": "small Na+ enter\n• The decreased resting membrane potential which decreases Na+ \ninflux can lead to bradyarrhythmia, e.g. sinus bradycardia\n10\nECG hyperkalemia - bradyarrhythmia↑K+"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Hypokalemia (below 3.5 mmol/L): causes\n• Intra-extracellular shift: \n• Alkalemia (acidemia)\n• High insulin and adrenalin (pheochromocytoma)\n• Massive catabolism of intracellular proteins and glycogen: decreased \nstorage\n• Increased renal loss\n• Hyperaldosteronism\n• Hyperreninism in renal arterial stenosis\n• ↑ Na + availability in the renal tubules\n• Type I and Type II renal tubular acidosis \n• Bartter syndrome and Loop diuretics (in ascendent loop Na+-K+-\n2Cl− symporter) leading to alkalosis \n• Thiazide diuretics(in distal tubules Na+Cl− symporter) : alkalosis\n• Extrarenal K+ losses from the body are usually small, but can be marked in \nchronic diarrhea, severe burns or prolonged sweating\n• Diarrhea: Loss in GIT leads to hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with \n↓K+\n• Vomiting leading to chypochloremic metabolic alkalosis\n• An abuse of laxatives and the use of diuretics- both loop and thiazides.11\n↓K+"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Hypokalemia (below 3.5 mmol/L): manifestation\n• Neuromuscular : smooth muscle weakness leading to obstipation, paralytic \nileus or even paralysis of respiratory muscle.\n• ECG: tachyarrhythmias with palpitation\n12\n↓K+\n"}, {"page": 12, "text": "ECG hypokalemia-delayed repolarization\nor T wave may be inverted 13\n↓K+\n• Potassium: below 3.5 mmol/L\n• Hypokalaemia causes widespread downsloping ST depression with T-wave \nflattening/inversion, prominent U waves and a prolonged QU interval.\n"}, {"page": 13, "text": "ECG hypokalemia- tachyarrhythmias\n14\n↓K+\n• Frequent supraventricular and ventricular ectopics\n• Supraventricular tachyarrhythmias: AF, atrial flutter, atrial tachycardia\n• Potential to develop life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias, e.g. VT, VF \nand Torsades de Pointes\n• Hypokalaemia\ncausing Torsades \nde Pointes: Note \nthe atrial ectopic \ncausing ‘R on T’ \n(or is it ‘R on U’?) \nthat initiates the \nparoxysm of TdP"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Triggered activity in hypokalemia leading to tachycardia \nor tachyarrhythmia\n• The repeated \nspontaneous \ndepolarization \n(without coming \nimpulses) due to \ninstability of \nmembrane potential \nin the myocytes\n• early after \ndepolarization: occur \nbefore repolarization \nhas finished \n(hypokalemia, \nhypothermia or \nhypoxia)\n• delayed after depolarization: occur \nafter repolarization (from raised \nintracellular Ca2+)\nExample: Torsade \nde pointes\nIn atrial and ventricular \nmyocytes repolarization due \nto K+ efflux: Non-pacemaker \naction potentials or fast \nresponse"}, {"page": 15, "text": "Torsade de pointes\n• A specific form of Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia: it has a \ncharacteristic morphology in which the QRS complexes “twist” around \nthe isoelectric line. R-on-T phenomenon: ventricular extrasystole stands \non descending part of T wave and trigger torsade de points\n• Hemodynamic changes, e.g. ↓ cardiac output due to too fast heart beat\n• Cause: QT prolongation (inborn defect) or long plateau phase, e.g. \nearly after depolarization\n"}, {"page": 16, "text": "17\n↑↓K+\nComparison of \nhypokalemia and \nhyperkalemia on ECG: \nthe first manifestation"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Hypernatremia implies a plasma sodium level above\n145 mEq/L (145 mmol/L) and characterized cellular dehydration.\n• Acute hypernatremia: it has been present for 48 hours or less.\nFast intervention or the exsiccation of the nerve cells\n• Chronic hypernatremia: it has been present for longer than 48 hours.\nSlow intervention or brain edema\nHyponatremia represents a plasma sodium concentration below 135 mEq/L \n(135 mmol/L) and characterized cellular hyperhydration.\n.\n• acute hyponatremia: it has developed over a period of less than 48 \nhours, \n• Fast intervention or fatal herniation (foramen magnum)\n• chronic hyponatremia: it has been present for more than 48 hours\n• Slow intervention or osmotic demyelination syndrome\nHypernatremia and hyponatremia I\n18\nNa+"}, {"page": 18, "text": "BRAIN ADAPTATION TO severe chronic hypernatremia\n• Endogenous/idiogenic osmoles (The brain can produce more particles \nfrom its cellular metabolism):  taurine, glycine, glutamine, sorbitol and \ninositol and urea.\n• Fast intervention: cerebral edema \nBRAIN ADAPTATION TO HYPONATREMIA:\n• increased intracranial pressure forces interstitial sodium and water out \nof the brain into the cerebrospinal fluid, ameliorating the increased \nbrain volume. In addition, astrocytes begin to lose intracellular solutes, \nprincipally potassium and organic osmolytes.\n• Fast intervention: osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS)\nHypernatremia and hyponatremia II\n19\nNa+"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Hypernatremia and hyponatremia: ECG\n20\nNa+\n• Increased (hypernatremia) and decreased (hyponatremia) sodium \nlevels do not have any effect on the ECG, nor cardiac rhythm or \nimpulse conduction (Non-significant changes on the ECG)"}, {"page": 20, "text": "• Most Ca2+ (99%): in bone as hydroxyapatite (Ca5(PO4)3(OH))\n• Small fraction of Ca2+ circulating within the intracellular (primarily in \nthe mitochondria) and extracellular (in the blood):\n• 50% is bound to plasma proteins, (primarily albumin) and\n• 40% is in the free or ionized form (5.5 to 5.6 mg/dl). \nCalcium\n22\nCa+"}, {"page": 21, "text": "• In states of acidosis, levels of ionized Ca2+ increase. \n• In states of alkalosis, the amount of protein-bound calcium increases \nand the physiologically active, ionized Ca2+ level decreases. The \ndecreased concentration of ionized Ca2+ may be great enough to \ncause symptoms of hypocalcemia, such as tetany.\nThe ionized calcium\n23\nCa+"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Hypocalcaemia (bellow 2.26 mmol/L): causes\n• Nutritional deficiencies\n• Excessive amounts of dietary phosphorus also bind with calcium, so \nneither mineral is absorbed when such an excess occurs. \n• Hypoparathyroidism (after removal of the parathyroid glands: loss of PTH) \nor Pseudohypoparathyroidism\n• Vitamin D deficiency\n• Blood transfusions: the citrate solution used in storing whole blood binds \nwith calcium and makes it unavailable to the tissues. \n• Renal insufficiency: loss of glomerular filtration with phosphate \naccumulation secondary decreases Ca2+ and secondary \nhyperparathyroidism\n• Pancreatitis causes release of lipases into soft tissue spaces, so the free \nfatty acids that are formed bind calcium, causing a decrease in the \nconcentration of ionized calcium. \n• Metabolic or respiratory alkalosis: ionized calcium.\n• Hypoalbuminemia: total serum calcium or bound calcium in the plasma.\n27\n↓Ca+"}, {"page": 23, "text": "• In the absence of PTH, resorption of calcium from bone and regulation \nof calcium reabsorption from the renal tubules are impaired. \n• ↓ serum calcium levels  \n• ↑ serum phosphate levels\nHypoparathyroidism \n28"}, {"page": 24, "text": "• Primary:\n• Damage to the parathyroid glands during thyroid surgery and occurs \nbecause of the anatomic proximity of the parathyroid glands to the \nthyroid or ionizing radiation\n• Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism  \n• Idiopathic hypoparathyroidism \n• Hemochromatosis: the accumulated iron damages the gland\n• Wilson's disease: the accumulated copper\n• Familial hypoparathyroidism \n• DiGeorge syndrome (velocardiofacial syndrome): thyroid gland \nmalformations\n• Secondary: \n• Hypomagnesemia (due to chronic alcoholism, malnutrition, \nmalabsorption) also can cause a decrease in PTH\nHypoparathyroidism \n29\n↓Ca+"}, {"page": 25, "text": "• an inherited condition: There is a resistance to PTH and its \nreceptor cannot produce cAMP in response to PTH\n• ↑ PTH\n• ↓ hypocalcemia\n• ↑  phosphate (hyperphosphatemia). \nPseudohypoparathyroidism\n30\n↓Ca+"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Hypocalcaemia (bellow 2.26 mmol/L): severe \nsymptoms\n• Long-term: Osteoporosis and fractures \n• Prolonged ST leads to long QT interval indicating prolonged ventricular \ndepolarization (plateau) and decreased cardiac contractility (inotropic effect), \ncardiac arrest\n• Tetany: continuous severe muscle spasm that can interfere with breathing \nand cause death.\n• Intestinal cramping and hyperactive bowel sounds: affected the smooth \nmuscles of the gastrointestinal tract\n• an increase in neuromuscular excitability: partial depolarization of nerves \nand muscle as the threshold potential becomes more negative and \napproaches the resting membrane potential (hypopolarization): a low \nthreshold for nerve and muscle excitation so that a nerve impulse may be \ninitiated by a slight stimulus\n• Relationship of irritability with cations and anions \n• Na+ + K+/Ca2+ + Mg2+ + H+\n31\n↓Ca+"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Hypercalcemia (exceeding 2.65 mmol/l or \nsevere 3.5mmol/L): causes\n• Primary hyperparathyroidism: ↑Ca2+ \n• Secondary hyperparathyroidism : slightly ↑Ca2+  \n• Tertiary hyperparathyroidism : ↑Ca2+ \n• Paraneoplastic syndrome: ectopic production of the hormones \nmimicking the effect of parathyroid hormone: many tumors produce \nhormones similar to PTH (hypoparathyroidism at parathyroid gland): \nbone metastases \n• sarcoidosis: increase vitamin D levels.\n• acidosis: increasing ionized calcium levels.\n34\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 28, "text": "• The calcium level in the blood increases because of increased bone \nresorption and gastrointestinal absorption of calcium. Normal feedback \nmechanisms, such as elevated serum levels of ionized calcium, fail to \nnormally inhibit PTH secretion by the parathyroid gland.\n• Parathyroid adenomas \n• Parathyroid hyperplasia\n• Parathyroid carcinoma\n• Multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes \n• MEN-1: Parathyroid adenomas + gastrinoma (neuroendocrine \ntumor secreting gastrin, pancreas or duodenum) + prolactinoma \n(benign tumor of pituitary gland), \n• MEN-2a: carcinoma of thyroid gland + feochromocytom (adrenal \nmedulla) and less hyperparathyroidism\n• Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH)\nPrimary Hyperparathyroidism\n35\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 29, "text": "• Benign autosomal dominant condition: It is caused by a mutation in the \ncalcium-sensing receptor in the parathyroid gland.\n• ↑ Ca2+ \n• ↓ phosphate (hypophosphatemia)\n• ↓ urine calcium excretion \n• It can be differentiated from primary hyperparathyroidism by measurement \nof 24-hour urine calcium excretion\nFamilial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) \n36\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 30, "text": "• Normocalcemic hyperparathyroidism:\n• Tissue resistance to PTH \n• An early phase of primary hyperparathyroidism\n• Secondary to a decrease in serum ionized calcium levels:\n• Chronic kidney disease: failure of glomerular filtration results in \nhyperphosphatemia and reduced levels of activated vitamin D. \nBoth leads to hypocalcemia, which stimulates PTH secretion. \n• chronic vitamin D deficiency\n• decreased intestinal absorption of vitamin D or calcium\nSecondary Hyperparathyroidism\n37\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 31, "text": "The etiology is unknown but represents autonomous secretion of PTH \nfrom persistent parathyroid stimulation.\nIt occurs: \n• after long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism causing \nhyperplasia\n• after renal transplantation\nTertiary Hyperparathyroidism\n38\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Hypercalcemia: severe symptoms\n• increased cardiac contractility: Short QT interval (short paleau) and \nventricular extrasystoles\n• decreased in neuromuscular excitability: threshold potential becomes \nmore positive (hyperpolarized) (e.g., moves from −60 to −50 millivolts) \nand the cell membrane becomes refractory to depolarization because \nthere is a greater difference between threshold potential and resting \nmembrane potential \n• Renal tubular function is decreased (polyuria and dehydration), kidney \nstones form as precipitates of calcium salts\n• Osteoporosis, fractures\n39\n↑Ca+"}, {"page": 33, "text": "• Phosphate (HPO2-\n4 ): primarily in bone (85%)\n• Smaller amounts: within the intracellular (main intracellular anions) \nand extracellular spaces. In the plasma, phosphate exists\n• phospholipids and phosphate esters\n• inorganic phosphate (ionized form). The normal serum levels of \ninorganic phosphate range from 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dl and may be as \nhigh as 6.0 to 7.0 mg/dl in infants and young children. \nPhosphate\n45\nP"}, {"page": 34, "text": "• Calcium and phosphate concentrations are rigidly controlled.\n• [Ca2+ × HPO2-\n4 =K] constant (K)\n• Thus, if the concentration of one ion increases or decreases, that of \nthe other increases or decreases. \n• Calcium and phosphate balance is regulated by three hormones:\n• parathyroid hormone (PTH):\n• PTH gene transcription is increased by hypocalcemia, \nglucocorticoids, and estrogen. PTH release is increased by \nhypocalcemia, adrenergic agonists, dopamine, and prostaglandin \nE2\n• PTH stimulates reabsorption of calcium along the distal tubule of \nthe nephron and inhibits phosphate reabsorption by the proximal \ntubule of the nephron.\n• The combination of low calcium level and increased PTH secretion \ncauses the renal activation of vitamin D.\n• vitamin D\n• When renal failure occurs, vitamin D is not activated; serum \ncalcium levels decrease; and phosphate levels increase. \nChronic kidney disease (decreased glomerular filtration leads \nto P accumulation that secondary decrease Ca2+)  \n• calcitonin.\nCalcium and phosphate\n46\nP and Ca2+"}, {"page": 35, "text": "hormone response \nto\nserum bone GIT kidney\nParathyroi\nd hormone\nparathyroid \ngland\n↓ Ca2+ hypercalcem\nia and \nhyperphosph\natemia\n↓ via calcitriol ↑ \nreabsorptio\nn of Ca2+\nbut \nexcretion \nof P and \nHCO3 \n-\nCalcitriol\n(from \ncholesterol \nin the skin, \nthan \nkidney and \nliver)\nparathyroid \nhormone\nDepends on \nconcentration\n↑absorption \nof Ca2+ and \nP in same \nratio\n↑ \nreabsorptio\nn of Ca2+\nand P\nCalcitonin \nthyroid \ngland\nAcute (not \nchronic) \nhypercalce\nmia\nhypocalcemi\na\nshort-\nterm \nbone \ncalciphic\nation\n↓absorption \nof Ca2+ and \nP in same \nratio\n↑excretion \nof both \nCa2+ and P\n47"}, {"page": 36, "text": "• loss of glomerular filtration: ↑P secondary decreases Ca2+ \nthat and secondary hyperparathyroidism\n• the cell destruction associated with treatment of metastatic \ntumors with chemotherapy: most phosphate is located in \ncells\n• Increased somatotropin (growth hormone): acromegaly \n(diseases of the Anterior Pituitary) and kids\n• Hypoparathyroidism can cause elevated phosphate levels \nby increasing renal tubular reabsorption of phosphate.\n• High D vitamin\nHyperphosphatemia (more than 1.46 mmol/L): \ncauses\n49\n↑P"}, {"page": 37, "text": "• are related primarily to low serum calcium levels and thus are \ncomparable to symptoms of hypocalcemia. \n• With prolonged hyperphosphatemia, calcification of soft tissues \noccurs in the lungs, kidneys, and joints because ↑ P with Ca2+ are \ndeposited in bone and soft tissues.\nHyperphosphatemia: Symptoms \n50\n↑P"}, {"page": 38, "text": "• decreased tubular reabsorption within the proximal tubule: leads \nto \n• Kids: rachitis (rickets) resistant to vitamin D\n• Adults: idiopathic osteomalacia (Milkman syndrome)\n• De Toni-Fanconi syndrome: decreased phosphate \nreabsorption plus glucose and amino acids\n• long-term alcohol abuse\n• malabsorption syndromes: decreased reabsorption of phosphate\n• Hyperparathyroidism\n• D vitamin deficiency\nHypophosphatemia (less than 0.81 mmol/L): \ncauses\n51\n↓P"}, {"page": 39, "text": "Hypophosphatemia: Symptoms\n• ATP levels\n• reduced capacity for oxygen transport:decreased 2,3-\ndiphosphoglycerate and the oxyhemoglobin curve shifts to the left, \nhypoxia can occur with bradycardia\n• bone resorption occurs\n52\n↓P"}, {"page": 40, "text": "• Cause: severe pancreatitis (The intestinal fat /steatorrhea can bind Mg2+ \nbeing similar to Ca2+ ), alcoholism, diuretics or diarrhea \n• Manifestation: \n• A loss of magnesium results in decreased parathyroid hormone and \nhypocalcemia\n• Similar to hypocalcemia, a low Mg (<0.5mmol/L) increases \nneuromuscular excitability and tetany are present. ECG: long QT \ninterval that increases the risk of Torsades de Pointes\n• A loss of magnesium results in movement of potassium out of the cell, \nwith renal excretion resulting in hypokalemia.\nHypomagnesemia <0.7mmol/L\n53\n↓Mg2+"}, {"page": 41, "text": "Hypermagnesemia > 1 mmol/L\n• It is rare except for renal failure or cellular destruction\n• ECG: long QT interval (same as low Mg2+) + long PQ \n54\n↑ Mg2+\n"}, {"page": 42, "text": "References \nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\nPorth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer \nHealth | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \nMcCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, \n2018. \nSilbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. Thieme, \n2016. "}]}, {"id": "hydration", "title": "E4 Hypertonic and hypotonic dehydration and hyperhydration ZN", "file": "/lectures/hydration.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Disorders of salt balance: content\n• Disturbance of volume and osmotic equilibrium\n• Body fluid components\n• Major electrolytes in body fluid\n• Cellular membrane permeability\n• Osmolality, effective osmolality and volume\n• Regulation: ANP, ADH and RAAS\n• Examples: \n• Hyperaldosteronism and hypoaldosteronism, \n• Diabetes insipidus and SIADH (Syndrome of \nInappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone)\n• Hypovolemic and hypervolemic states\n• Review: Structure and function of nephron\n1"}, {"page": 2, "text": "joint spaces, the pericardial sac, the peritoneal cavity,\nAnd the pleural cavity: it is often referred to as third-space fluid.\ntotal body water decrease with advancing age due to more adipose \ntissue and less muscle\n70kg adult male\ngender\n2"}, {"page": 3, "text": "3\n3Na+/2K+ ATPase pump\nExtracellular fluid: \nplasma\nIntracellular fluid\nElectrolytes mmol/L Electrolytes mmol/L\nCations Cations\nNa+ 135-145 K+ 150\nK+ 3.5-5.5 Mg+ 40\nCa+ 5 Na+ 10\nMg+ 2\nAnions Anions\nCl- 103 Phosphates and sulfates 150\nHCO3\n- 26 HCO3\n- 10\nHPO4\n2- 2 Proteinate 40\nHSO4\n- 1\nOrganic acids 5\nProteinate 17\nApproximate major electrolyte content in body fluid\n"}, {"page": 4, "text": "cellular membranecapillary\nWater\nUrea\nGlucose\nNa+\nProteins\nCellular membrane permeability\nThe high permeability of the small pore system to electrolytes (except in the \nbrain) quickly establishes an electrolyte equilibrium between plasma and\ninterstitium. Thus it is only the plasma proteins, that exert a sustained \nosmotic pressure across the capillary wall.\n4\n3Na+/2K+ ATPase \npump\nGLUT4"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Osmolality \nThe concentration of osmotically active particles in solution expressed \nin osmols or milliosmols per kilogram of solvent.\nNormal osmolality range 285 to 295 mOsm/kg\n– Hypoosmolality: serum osmolality < 285 mOsm/kg\n– Hyperosmolality: serum osmolality > 295 mOsm/kg\nOsmolality (equation): 2Na+ (+ K+) + glucose + urea (BUN: blood \nurea nitrogen)\nOsmotically active particles: Cl-, HCO3\n-, alcohol, aceton and \nmannitol, proteins\n5"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Osmometers\nOsmometers are used for measuring osmolality of biological \nmaterials.\nOsmotic gap: the difference between calculated and measured \nosmolality\n6"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Osmolarity\nThe concentration of osmotically active particles in solution \nexpressed in osmols or milliosmols per liter of solution.\n7"}, {"page": 8, "text": "Osmolality: Na+ as a key player \nNa+ affects osmolality to the larger extent than K+, because  Na+ is the \nmain extracellular cation (concentration)\nNormal osmolality range 285 to 295 mOsm/kg\n– Hypoosmolality: serum osmolality < 285 mOsm/kg\n– Hyperosmolality: serum osmolality > 295 mOsm/kg\nOsmolality (equation): 2Na+ + glucose + urea (BUN: blood urea \nnitrogen)\n– Na+: 135-145 mmol/L\n• Mild (146-149 mmol/L)\n• Moderate (150-169 mmol/L)\n• Severe (≥170 mmol/L)\n– K+: 3.5-5.5 mmol/L\n– Glucose: 3.3-5.5 mmol/l\n– Urea: 1.7–8.3 mmol/l\n– Albumine: 0.5-0.78 mmol/L (They constitute 55-65% of total plasma proteins)\n8"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Other osmolality equations\nCalculated osmolarity = 2 Na+ + Glucose + Urea (all in mmol/L).\nor\nCalculated osmolarity = 2 Na + + 2 K + + Glucose + Urea (all in mmol/L).\nor\nCalculated osmolarity = 2[Na + ] + [Glucose]/18 + [ BUN ]/2.8 where \n[Glucose] and [BUN] are measured in mg/dL and [Na + ]  in mmol/L. \n(typical in the US)\nor\nIf the patient has ingested ethanol, the ethanol level should be included \nin the calculated osmolality.\n9"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Na+=143 mEq/l BUN=4 mg/dL pH=7.30\nCl-=105 mEq/l GLU=104 mg/dL MO=356 mOsm/kg\nHCO3\n-=19 mEq/l KETO=Neg\nA sixty-seven year old white male was found pulseless and resuscitated; then \nbrought to the emergency room. He had been reported to be drinking in a bar \nall afternoon, and had then fallen from a ten foot balcony to snow covered \nground. He arrived in the emergency room with a fractured occiput and was \nunresponsive.\nAG = Na+ - Cl- + HCO3\n- = 143 - 105- 19 = 19\nNormal AG = 8-16 mEq/L\nCO = 2 x Na+ + 1.15 *GLU/18 + BUN/2.8 = 286+ 1.15 \n*104/18 + 4/32.8 = 294\n0G = MO - CO= 356 - 294 =62\nEXAMPLE CASE\n10"}, {"page": 11, "text": "EXAMPLE CASE: Interpretation\nAn osmolar gap value greater than 15 has traditionally been \nconsidered a critical value or cutoff. Approximately 97% of \nosmolar gaps in patients are between -10 and +10.\nProper interpretation of the OG also requires knowledge of the \nanion gap (AG = Na+ - HCO3\n- - Cl-), the blood pH, and \nqualitative testing of the plasma ketone bodies (KETO)\n11"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Plasma tonicity, effective osmolality\n2Na + + glucose \nplasma tonicity reflects the concentration of solutes that do not easily cross \ncell membranes\nThe most common example of this phenomenon is the rapid fall in plasma \nurea concentration produced by hemodialysis in a uremic patient. In this \nsetting, the removal of plasma urea occurs more quickly than urea can \nequilibrate across the hemo-cerebral barrier (delayed in the brain due to \nhemo-cerebral barrier), which promotes osmotic water movement into the \nbrain. In the brain, the water shift can result in cerebral edema and acute \nneurologic dysfunction, changes that partially explain the dialysis \ndisequilibrium syndrome\n12"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Effective volume\n• When the effective circulating blood volume is compromised, the \ncondition is often referred to as hypovolemia.\n• Effective volume — it is monitored by sensors in the renal afferent \nglomerular arterioles (for renin), carotid sinus (for antidiuretic \nhormone, ADH), and atria and ventricles (for natriuretic peptides) that \nrespond to changes in pressure (baroreceptors), not volume.\n• By contrast to the baroreceptors, osmoreceptor in hypothalamus are \nimportant for thirst sensation and to induce secretion of antidiuretic \nhormone\n14"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Renin-\nangiotensin-\naldosterone\nAntidiuretic \nhormone\nDiuresisAtrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) \nWater retention\nHormonal factors: diuresis vs. water retention\n16\n+"}, {"page": 15, "text": "• A family of hormone/paracrine factors that are structurally related: \nANP, BNP.\n• BNP (brain-type natriuretic peptide) is synthesized largely by the \nventricles as well as in the brain where it was first identified\n• ANP is synthesized and secreted by cardiac muscle cells in the walls \nof the atria in the heart. These cells contain volume receptors (low \npressure baroreceptors) which respond to increased stretching of the \natrial wall due to increased atrial blood volume. \n• Aims:\n• Suppress sympathetic outflow\n• Promote myocardial relaxation and inhibit hypotrophy and fibrosis\n• The main function of ANP is causing a reduction in \nexpanded extracellular fluid (ECF) volume by \n• inhibition of secretion of renin and aldosterone \n• vasodilation of the afferent arterioles with constriction of the \nefferent arterioles leading to increased GF and decreased  \nsodium and water absorption by kidney tubules.\nAtrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) or atrial natriuretic factor \n(ANF)\n17"}, {"page": 16, "text": "Vasoconstriction of vas afferent leads to ↓ glomerular filtration rate (*the \nincreased Na+ absorption) and ↓  renal perfusion\nThe model of glomerular filtration rate (GFR)\nand renal perfusion (RPF)\nVasoconstriction of vas efferent with vasodilation of vas afferent leads \nto ↑ glomerular filtration rate (*decreased Na+ absorption) and ↓ renal \nperfusion\n*When the GFR spontaneously decreases or increases, the renal tubules and, primarily the proximal tubules,\nautomatically adjust their rate of reabsorption of sodium andwater to balance the change in GFR. Thus a \nconstant fraction of filtered sodium and water is reabsorbed from the proximal tubule.This prevents wide \nfluctuations in sodium and water excretion into the urine and maintains sodium and water balance 18"}, {"page": 17, "text": "• Natriuretic peptide: Produced by atria and ventricles of the heart with \nhypertension and increased blood volume; causes vasodilation of \nafferent arteriole and vasoconstriction of efferent arteriole; modest \nincrease in GFR with little change in RBF\nANP: The model of glomerular filtration rate (GFR)\nand renal perfusion (RPF)\n↑ANP, ↑ adenosine, ↑ NO, ↑H+ , \n↑lactate and ↓ATP induce \nhyperpolarization of the cellular \nmembrane of the vascular smooth \nmuscle cells. It occurs via opening \nK+ channel that close Ca2+ channel \nand induce the vasodilation\n19"}, {"page": 18, "text": "• A hormone-inactive product of the proBNP molecule. It is \nconsidered to be a fragment that is incapable of linking to receptors \nand probably without biological significance. \n• It is a peptide with a high validity in cardiologic diagnostics, where it \nhas a clinically comparable statement as its own active hormone \nBNP. \n• BNP and NT-proBNP concentrations in plasma grow in people with \ndecompensated heart failure (distension of myocytes heart walls in \nheart failure as a pump), pulmonary hypertension, renal failure. \n• Compared to BNP, NT-proBNP has some advantages such as -\nstability of molecule, less biological variability, higher degree of \nstandardization).\nNT-proBNP\n20"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Water reabsorption\nRenin-\nangiotensin-\naldosterone\nAntidiuretic \nhormone\nEffective \nvolume\nEffective \nosmolality\nNa+ reabsorption\n(the principal cells of \nthe distal duct: \naldosterone and\nproximal Na + H + \nantiporter: \nangiotensin II)\nInsertion of \naquaporins \n(collecting duct)\nStimuli\nResponse\nWater \nreabsorption\n21"}, {"page": 20, "text": "α-globulin \n(angiotensinogen\nproduced by liver \nhepatocytes)\n• Vitamin D3 is a potent negative endocrine regulator of renin gene expression\n• ACE inhibitors are a class of drugs that reduce blood pressure by inhibiting the formation of angiotensin II and aldosterone\nAng II \nstimulates Na+/H+\nexchangers and ↓ \nwash out of vasa \nrecta \n• Less effect of  \nACTH\n22\nRenin-angiotensin-aldosterone system"}, {"page": 21, "text": "1 Renin production: its production is stimulate by\nJuxtaglomerular apparatus: 2 parts\n• Juxtaglomerular cells are located around the \nafferent arteriole.\n• Decreased blood pressure (renal perfusion) \nreduces stretch of the juxtaglomerular cells and \ntrigger renin release.\n• Portion of the distal convoluted tubule with \nspecialized Na+ and Cl-sensing cells known as the \nmacula densa: ↓Na at the start of distal tubules \ndetected by macula densa increases renin \nRenin is an enzyme formed and stored in granular cells of the afferent \narterioles of the JGA\n Innervated \nby SNS (the \nnext slide)\n23"}, {"page": 22, "text": "2 Renin production: its production is stimulate by\nsympathetic nerve system\nα1-adrenoreceptors\nvasoconstriction \nof the afferent \narterioles\nretention \nNa+\nand H2O\nDirect tubular \nreabsorption of Na+\n↑ renin \nsecretion\n↑ sympathetic NS\nthe kidney\nα2 1\n↓ glomerular \nfiltration\n↑ ang II and \n↑ \naldosterone\n• Sympathetic nerve via β1 receptors induces renin and via 1 \nvasoconstriction of vas afferens: low blood pressure detected by \nbaroreceptors in arterial circulation activates sympathicoadrenal system\n24"}, {"page": 23, "text": "• Aldosterone: \n• secretion of K+ and H+ \n• absorption of Na+ in \nprinciple cells\n• Angiotensin II stimulates Na+ \nH+ antiporter\n• Angiotensin II will ↓ perfusion through the vasa recta. This decreases the \nwashout of NaCl and urea in the kidney medullary space. It facilitate \nabsorption of tubular fluid that will increase passive reabsorption of sodium \nalong the thick ascending limb of the Loop of Henle.\nRole of angiotensin II and aldosterone\n25"}, {"page": 24, "text": "• Angiotensin II. Arteriolar vasoconstriction including the vas efferens (less \nthe vas afferens) will ↑glomerular filtration and decreases renal perfusion \nin peritubular capillaries. It maintains the GFR even under conditions of \nrenal hypoperfusion, e.g. bleeding. The GF forms primary urine from that \nNa+ and H2O is reabsorbed in the distal tubules. \n• In contrast, at higher concentrations or chronic one, angiotensin II induces \nglomerular remodeling  and vascular smooth muscle cells to hypertrophy \n(hypertension, atherosclerosis, renal disease, and cardiac hypertrophy) \n1 Role of angiotensin II: efferent arteriole \n26\nAngiotensin II: mesangial \ncell contraction"}, {"page": 25, "text": "• Physiology: a countercurrent in \nthe loop of Henle and vasa recta\n• Angiotensin II – mediated low \nperfusion through the vasa recta \ndecreases the washout of NaCl \nand urea from the \nkidney medullary space. It \nfacilitate absorption of tubular \nfluid that will increase passive \nreabsorption of sodium along the \nthick ascending limb of the Loop \nof Henle.\n2 Role of angiotensin II: vasa recta\n27"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Aldosterone: Adrenal gland hormone\nRenin, less \nACTH\nACTH"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Primary aldosteronism (primary hyperaldosteronism)\n• It is increased aldosterone originally\n• Causes: \n• Benign aldosterone-producing adrenal adenoma (Conn's syndrome)\n• Bilateral adrenal nodular hyperplasia: Because cortisol production is low, ACTH concentration increases and causes \nadrenal hyperplasia.\n• Adrenal carcinomas (rare)\nSecondary hyperaldosteronism\n• It is increased renin originally and secondarily  increased aldosterone and \nrenin\n• Causes: cirrhosis, ectopic production of renin, coarctation of aorta (narrow \naorta), stenosis of renal arteria, decreased cardiac output in cardiac failure \n(decreased circulating blood volume). During pregnancy, estrogen  \nstimulates the production of renin and angiotensin. In hyperthyroidism, \nthyroid hormones increase renin.\nPseudohyperaldosteronism \n• low aldosterone\n• activation of the aldosterone receptor without an increase in aldosterone.\n• Cushing’s syndrome (high cortisol stimulated receptor for \naldosterone)\n• High dose steroid therapy\n• Liddle syndrome, an autosomal dominant genetic disorder\nHyperaldosteronism\n29\n"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Conn's syndrome : \nHypertension (↑Na+ can be diluted and thus normonatremia)\nRenal excretion of potassium (hypokalemia with alkalosis may lead \nto hypocalcemia)\nNo edema: Sodium loss and water loss are maintained by \nhypervolemia-induced atrial natriuretic factor release, pressure \nnatriuresis, and aldosterone escape (spontaneous diuresis) leading \nto nocturia and polyuria X secondary hyperaldosteronism is \nconnected with edema.\nThe ANP-mediated polyuria can increase urinary losses of \npotassium (hypokalemic nephropathy with decreased urinary \nconcentration)\nLow renin\nMetabolic syndrome (hypertension, obesity, dyslipidemia, insulin\nresistance, and hyperglycemia) is associated with primary \naldosteronism: Aldosterone reduces insulin sensitivity in skeletal muscle \nand adipocytes\nClinical manifestation of primary \nhyperaldosteronism\n30\n"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Pressure-diuresis or pressure-natriuresis\nRelatively small increases in glomerular filtration that follow from \nincreases in renal arterial pressure cause relatively large increases in \nurine and sodium output.\n31"}, {"page": 30, "text": "Clinical manifestation of secondary \nhyperaldosteronism\n Increased renin (stimulated by ↓circulating blood volume) \n• During pregnancy, ↑estrogen  stimulates the production of renin and \nangiotensin.\n• In hyperthyroidism, thyroid hormones increase renin.\n Hypertension and hypokalemia is less common compared to primary \nhyperaldosteronism\n Edema are common\n"}, {"page": 31, "text": "• Congenital hypoaldosteronism:\n• Hyperreninemic hypoaldosteronism: genetic defect in \nenzymes that are important for synthesis of aldosterone\n• Congenital form of Addison's disease (congenital adrenal \nhypoplasia) or pseudohypoaldosteronism\nHypoaldosteronism\n33\n• Acquired hypoaldosteronism:\n• Primary hypoaldosteronism: Addison's disease \n(primary adrenal insufficiency, autoimmune): \n↓aldosterone, ↓cortisol (primary hypocortisolism) \nand secondarily ↑ increased adrenocorticotropic \nhormone (ACTH) and ↑ renin\n• Note: secondary adrenal insufficiency (secondary hypocortisolism, e.g. \nexogenous glucocorticoids) due to ↓ ACTH does not affect aldosterone but \nsimilar to SIADH with ↑ADH as cortisol inhibit ADH\n• Secondary hypoaldosteronism: Acquired \nhypoaldosteronism: due to low renin: \nhyporeninemic hypoaldosterinism\n• Pseudohypoaldosteronism: high aldosterone, \ntubular cells do not response to aldosterone due \nto renal hypoxia or urine stagnation"}, {"page": 32, "text": "• Autoimmune disease: rare, more common in women\n• 21-Hydroxylase autoantibodies and autoreactive T cells specific to \nadrenal cortical cells\n• Others: infections (tuberculosis, fungal infections, human \nimmunodeficiency virus), infiltrative diseases (amyloidosis, metastatic \ncarcinoma),bilateral adrenal hemorrhage.\n• AD often is associated with other autoimmune diseases: autoimmune \npolyendocrine syndrome (APS): \n• Type I: autosomal recessive (Addison disease, \nhypoparathyroidism, mucocutaneous candidiasis)\n• Type II: Addison disease, immune thyroid disease, diabetes \nmellitus, celiac disease, and hypogonadism.\nAddison disease: causes\n↓\n ↓"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Addison disease: manifestation I\n• ↓ corticosteroid and ↓ mineralocorticoid synthesis and ↑ ACTH \n• Serum glucose concentration can be low: decreased gluconeogenesis \n• Manifestation of primary hypocortisolism dominate \n↓\n ↓"}, {"page": 34, "text": "Addison disease: clinical manifestation II\n↓\n ↓\n• Weakness and easy fatigability and later anorexia, \nnausea, vomiting, and diarrhea\n• Disturbances in mood and motivation are common\n• Hypotension:↓ Na+ leads to hypovolemia and ↓ glomerular filtration, \nweakness\n• GIT disturbances: celiac disease electrolyte\n• Water poisoning: hypovolemia induces ADH that leads to H2O retention. \nWater poisoning manifest with headache, nausea, vomiting and glucose \nfever due to edema in hypothalamus, mainly after administration of glucose.\n• Hyperkalemia: both decreased ↓aldosterone-mediated K+ excretion and ↓ \nglomerular filtration contribute to ↑K + leading to arrhythmia, mild alkalosis or \nhypochloremic acidosis "}, {"page": 35, "text": "Addison disease: clinical manifestation III\n↓\n ↓\n• Hyperpigmentation: ACTH stimulate melanocytes\n• Vitiligo (white patchy areas of depigmented skin): autoimmune destruction of \nmelanocytes\n• ↓ adrenal androgen\n•In men: the adrenals not a major source of male androgens. \n•In women: a loss of some secondary sexual characteristics such as \npubic and axillary hair, normally maintained by the adrenal androgens\n• Acute Addisonian crisis\n"}, {"page": 36, "text": "• Sever hypotension and vascular collapse that can lead to circulatory \nshock\n• Combined effect of hypocortisolism, hypoaldosteronism, volume \ndepletion and precipitating factors: infection or ↓ vasomotor tone due \nto ↓ cortisol\n• Acute renal failure: ↑ creatinine and ↑urea\n• Eosinophil and lymphocyte counts are often elevated, neutropenia\n• ↑fever, confusion, vomiting\nAcute Addisonian crisis \n↓"}, {"page": 37, "text": "The secretion of ADH is stimulated by\nInduced primarily by the osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus.\nInduced less by hypovolemia: e.g. In circulatory shock, an arterial \nhypotension induces ADH that inactivate K+ channel leading to \nvasoconstriction but later ADH is run out and vasodilation \nInduces primarily water retention\nThe secretion of ADH is inhibited by\nAntidiuretic Hormone (ADH) or vasopressor\npain\nglucocorticoids\nhypoxiastress\nnicotine\nsex\n39\nLow temperature"}, {"page": 38, "text": "ACTH\nAntidiuretic Hormone (ADH) or vasopressor\nadenohypophysis\naquaporins\nV1 needs 10x \n↑concentration \nof ADH than V2 \nV1A receptor\nV1B receptor\nV2 receptor"}, {"page": 39, "text": "• Low function of antidiuretic hormone: Diabetes Insipidus \n• Increased function of antidiuretic hormone: Syndrome of \nInappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH or Schwartz-Bartter\nsyndrome)\nAntidiuretic Hormone (ADH): conditions\n41"}, {"page": 40, "text": "• Increased function of antidiuretic hormone\n• Causes: \n• CNS disorders, e.g. stroke, hemorrhage, infection or tumor\n• Ectopic production of ADH by a tumor\n• Pulmonary disorders (unknown mechanism)\n• Mechanism: ↑ADH induces water retention leading to dilutional \nhyponatremia \n• Absolute urine osmolarity is low but ratio between urine and \nplasma osmolarity is ↑. (Urinal Na+ is concentrated due to poor \nwater retention but not too much due to hyponatremia.)\n• edema↑ does not have to be present due to compensatory ANP \nand diuresis.\n• Manifestation: lethargy, myoclonus, asterixis (flapping tremor , \ntemporary loss of motor tone) and confusion or coma\n• https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sEnp2ss8VoA\nSyndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone \n(SIADH or Schwartz-Bartter syndrome)\n42"}, {"page": 41, "text": "43\nSyndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone \n(SIADH or Schwartz-Bartter syndrome): mechanism\nSigns and symptoms depend upon the rate (chronic vs acute: tolerated \nbetter/less) and severity of hyponatremia: \n• Acute or fast decline of Na+: cerebral edema\n• Chronic: adaptive responses counteracting swelling in brain cells and is \ntraditionally defined as an asymptomatic"}, {"page": 42, "text": "• Low function of antidiuretic hormone and loss of hypotonic water \n(increased urination) leads to hypernatremia that induces thirst\n• Central (neurogenic) (low level of ADH)\n• causes: pituitary dysfunction due to ischemia or hamorrhage, \npregnancy (placenta can produce the enzymes that degrade \nADH)\n• Synthetic ADH will help\n• Peripheral (nephrogenic) (normal level of ADH)\n• causes: receptor dysfunction due to genetic mutation in V2\nreceptor \n• Synthetic ADH will not help. Vasoconstriction (V1A receptor) is \nnot affected as V2 receptor has defect.\nDiabetes Insipidus \n44"}, {"page": 43, "text": "45\nDiabetes Insipidus: mechanism \nSigns and symptoms depend \nupon the rate (chronic vs acute: \ntolerated better/less) and severity \nof hypernatremia: \nhypernatremia can suck out \nwater from brain and cause \nbleeding"}, {"page": 44, "text": "Hypovolemic and hypervolemic states\n46"}, {"page": 45, "text": "Hypovolemic and hypervolemic states: content\n• Dehydration\n• Symptoms\n• Examples: isotonic, hypotonic and hypertonic\n• Hyperhydration: symptoms\n• Symptoms\n• Examples: isotonic, hypotonic and hypertonic\n47"}, {"page": 46, "text": "• Headache and thirst \n• Weight loss \n• Decreased urine output (oliguria), and concentrated urine (with \nthe exception of diabetes insipidus and others).\n• Skin turgor may be normal or decreased. Dry skin and mucous \nmembranes. \n• Symptoms of hypovolemia: tachycardia, weak pulses and \n(postural) hypotension (peripheral vasoconstriction)\n• Others: Elevated levels of hematocrit (with the exception of \nbleeding),serum sodium concentration, urine and serum \nosmolality, and urine specific gravity are associated with \nmoderate water loss.\nDehydration: symptoms\n48"}, {"page": 47, "text": "• The hematocrit (Ht or HCT), the volume percentage (vol%) \nof red blood cells (RBC) in blood.\nDiagram of packed cell volume\n49\nDehydration: hematocrit"}, {"page": 48, "text": "Poor skin turgor\n50"}, {"page": 49, "text": "Dehydration Increase hematocrit in isotonic and hypotonic  (not in plasma loss)\nIsotonic\n(Normal Na+)\nHemorrhage, profuse bleeding, burn Loss of isotonic fluid: hypovolemia with peripheral \nvasoconstriction, tachycardia and RAAS, ADH activation. \nNote Despite high aldosterone, ↑K+ excretion does not have \nto be present due to low urine in the distal tubules that will \nconcentrate K+. By contrast, loop diuretics will increase \nurine in the distal tubules and K+ excretion)\nPuncture of ascites, sever wound drainage after \noperation. \nFluid leakage to the 3th space: paralytic ileus, \npancreatitis\nHypotonic\n(Decreased \nNa+)\nLow aldosterone Loss of water is less than Na + loss: cellular edema\nnephropathia loosing salt: if primary renal disease \naffected the renal marrow osmolarity e.g. \nPyelonephritis (bacterial infection of renal pelvis)\nHypotonic saline or pure water replacement after \nendurance exercise (No issue as healthy kidney \nwill compensate: reabsorbed Na+)\nBartter syndrome\nOverdose of loop diuretics\nHypertonic\n(Increased \nNa+)\nProfuse sweating, diarrhea (sweat, stomach fluid\nand stool is hypoosmotic, sever diarrhea has \nisoosmotic fluid)\nLoss of water is higher than loss of Na+: cellular exsiccation\nPolyuria at acute renal failure (the remaining \nnephrons cannot reabsorb Na+ and ↑ water loss \ndue to osmotic diuresis with hypoosmotic urine)\nlow antidiuretic hormone (diabetes insipidus, \nthirst due to ↑ osmolarity)\nosmotic diuresis in diabetes mellitus 51\nDehydration: forms"}, {"page": 50, "text": "mutations involving the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick \nascending limb of the loop of Henle. Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport one \nsodium, one potassium, and two chloride ions across the apical \nmembrane of the tubule.\nBartter syndrome (BS) \n• Primary hypereninism and secondary \nhyperaldosteronism\n• Hypokalemia\n• Hypochloremia \n• Metabolic alkalosis\n• Tendency to hypovolemia\n52"}, {"page": 51, "text": "Bartter syndrome (BS) \nAbolishment of the \nhypertonicity of the \nrenal medulla, \nseverely impairing \nthe ability to \nreabsorb water \nlater in the distal \nnephron \nand collecting duct \nsystem, leading to \nnormal extracellular \nvolume with \ntendency to \nhypovolemia.\n↓ Na+, K+, 2Cl-\nreabsorption \nin the Henle loop ↑ urinary Na+ load to \nthe distal nephron\n↑ Na+\nreabsorption\nChronic stimulation \nof macula densa\n(hyperplasia of \nJuxtaglomerular \napparatus ) and renin\n↑ urinary Cl- load to the \ndistal nephron and their \nexcretion\n↑ aldosterone\nHypokalemia\nH+ ATPase \nstimulation and \nH+ excretion\nNa+ combines with HCO3\n-\nas relative ↓ Cl- in urine. \nHCO3\n- are nor reabsorbed \nand abolish the lumen-\npositive voltage, resulting \nin decreased calcium and \nmagnesium reabsorption\nIntra and extracellular \nexchange between K+\nand H+Metabolic alkalosis\n↑ PGE2\n53"}, {"page": 52, "text": "Hyperhydration decrease hematocrit in isotonic and hypertonic \nIsotonic\n(Normal Na+)\nAccumulation of isotonic fluid due to↑ \nhydrostatic or ↓ oncotic pressure=edema with \nisotonic fluid accumulation (secondary not \nprimary ↑aldosterone)\nsevere congestive heart failure\ncirrhosis or hepatic failure\nnephrotic syndrome, >3,5 g/24 hours\nOverdose by anti-inflammatory drugs that ↓ \nPGE2 \nPGE acts via vasodilation in vas afferent\nHypotonic\n(Decreased Na+)\nWater is accumulated more than salt: \nIncreased mean erythrocyte volume (cellular \nedema)\n↑antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) (hypervolemia, \nno edema) hyponatremia and hypokalemia \npsychogenic disease (compulsive water \ndrinking)\nAcute renal failure with oliguria/anuria \n(catabolism)\nHypertonic\n(Increased Na+)\nSalt is accumulated more than water: \nDecreased mean erythrocyte volume (cellular \nexsiccation)\n↑aldosterone (Conn‘s syndrome) \n(hypervolemia, no edema)~\nsalt water\nAcute renal failure\nPrimary effect on aquaporins: accumulation of water and diluted Na+. Euvolemic hyponatremia due to secondary induced  ANP. \n~Due to water retention, hypernatremia does not have to be present. No edema: hypervolemia induces atrial natriuretic factor and \ndiuresis\n54\nHyperhydration: forms"}, {"page": 53, "text": "Acute renal failure with anuria or oliguria\n• Decreased glomerular filtration\n• ↑ serum urea to creatinine ratio: \n• Normally urea is reabsorbed: The low filtration of urea due to low \nGF will increase urea reabsorption. \n• Normally creatinine is secreted by tubular cells if serum creatinine \nis high. The low filtration of creatinine will increase serum \ncreatinine but serum creatinine is secreted by tubular cells.\n• Renal tubules are not damaged: \n• ↑ urinal osmolarity due to urea (Na+ is low)\n• Na+ is reabsorbed by RAAS leading to water retention: life \nthreatening edema (pulmonary edema), heart failure, cell swollen, \nhigh intracranial pressure\n• Catabolic state of metabolisms is additional source of water that \nis not excreted: hypoosmolar hyperhydration (hypervolemia)\n• Oliguria\n• Metabolic acidosis: accumulation of waste product of metabolism and low \nexcretion of H+\n• Hyperkalemia: in the last stage, in early stage: K+ usually excreted by \ntubules (arrhythmia) 55"}, {"page": 54, "text": "Normovolemia\nIsotonic\n(Normal Na+)\nnormal\nHypotonic\n(Decreased Na+)\nhypotonic saline or pure \nwater replacement after \nendurance exercise\nLoss of water is less than \nNa + loss: cellular edema\nHypertonic\n(Increased osmotically \nactive molecules )\nhyperglycemia Loss of water is higher \nthan loss of osmotically \nactive molecules: cellular \nexsiccation\nAccumulation of ketone \nbodies in diabetes \n56\nNormovolemia: forms"}, {"page": 55, "text": "References \nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\nPorth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters \nKluwer Health | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \nMcCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. \nMosby, 2018. \nSilbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. \nhttps://pfyziol.upol.cz/ - selected text passages (according to \nteachers' recommendations) "}]}, {"id": "nutrition", "title": "P4 Nutritional status and Malassimilation syndromes ZN", "file": "/lectures/nutrition.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Pathophysiology of nutritional \nstatus and malassimilation \nsyndrome"}, {"page": 2, "text": "• Regulation of appetite: orexins and anorexins\n• Central in hypothalamus\n• Peripheral: short-term and long-term effect\n• Pathophysiology of regulation of food intake and body mass: bulimia \nand others\n• Basel metabolic rate\n• Obesity: How we assess obesity (BMI, Tanita or Waist-to-hip ratio). \nInflammation in obesity.\n• Glycemic index\n• Catabolic and anabolic state: BUN and creatinine, azotemia and uremia\n• Starvation: \n• Short term\n• Long term: marasmus, kwashiorkor X Kwashiorkor-like syndrome, \ncachexia (e.g. anorexia  Il-1 beta), sarcopenia, malabsorption (primary \nand secondary).\nContent\ncentral peripheral\norexins neuropeptide Y ghrelin\nanorexins POMC producing peptide Insulin, leptin"}, {"page": 3, "text": "Appetite and satiety\n• Regulation of appetite and satiety occurs from the periphery acting \non central controls including the brainstem, hypothalamus, and \nautonomic nervous system. "}, {"page": 4, "text": "Central orexins and anorexins: Hypothalamus\n• Orexins: which promotes appetite, \nand decreases metabolism (anabolic)\n– neuropeptide Y (NPY) \n– agouti-related protein (AGRP)\n• Anorexins: which suppress appetite, \nand increase metabolism (catabolic).\n– cocaine-and-amphetamine–\nregulated transcript (CART) \n– pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC)–\nproducing peptide  \n• Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) is the pituitary \nprecursor for\n– circulating melanocyte stimulating hormone \n(α-MSH)\n– adrenocorticotropin hormone (ACTH)\n– β-endorphin.\n"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Peripheral orexin: Ghrelin from stomach\n• Ghrelin is produced in the response to hunger (low sugar) in the stomach \nand stimulate appetite by activating neuropeptide Y-expressing neurons in \nthe hypothalamus.\n• Ghrelin induces hyperglycemia to keep \nhomeostasis and antagonize lipolysis \n• Ghrelin also stimulates release of \ngrowth hormone (GH) from anterior \npituitary cells, release of gastric acid, \ngastrointestinal motility, and pancreatic \nfunctions.\n• There is expected level in obesity (↓) \nand anorexia nervosa (↑). \n• ↑ in some obese individuals\n• ↓ associated with insulin resistance \nand hyperleptinemia in type 2 \ndiabetes.\n"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Orexin: Ghrelin from stomach\n• Prader–Willi syndrome (PWS) is a genetic disorder caused by \nchromosome 15. It is associated with high ghrelin level and high appetite."}, {"page": 7, "text": "Anorexins\n– Leptin and adiponectin from adipocytes \n– glucagon-like peptide 1, intestinal peptide YY (PYY) and cholecystokinin \n(CCK) from the intestines \n– insulin from pancreatic beta cells\n– endocannabinoids from brain and peripheral nerves"}, {"page": 8, "text": "Anorexins: leptin in long-term regulation\n• High levels act via POMC \nproducing peptide and suppress \nappetite and increase expenditure\n• Low level induces neuropeptide Y \nand increase appetite\n• In obesity, high level and leptin \nresistance (insulin resistance)\n• In cachexia, low leptin does not \ninduce neuropeptide Y\n• Another adipokines can contribute, \ne.g. adiponectin\n• Stimulation of glucose uptake, \ninsulin sensitivity and lipid \ncatabolism (-oxidation)\n• Protection from endothelial \ndysfunction (prevention of \natherosclerotic formation)\n• In obesity, decreased\n• In anorexia nervosa, increased \nbut not from adipose tissue)\n"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Incretin: Glucagon-like peptide 1\n• It is produced and secreted by intestinal enteroendocrine L-cells and \ncertain neurons in the brainstem upon food consumption.\n• Incretin: decrease blood sugar levels in a glucose-dependent \nmanner by enhancing the secretion of insulin.\n• inhibits gastric emptying, acid secretion and motility collectively \ndecreasing appetite."}, {"page": 10, "text": "Short term-anorexin cholecystokinin (CCK, \npancreozymin)\n• a hormone secreted especially by the duodenal mucosa in response \nto dietary products and nervous system (e.g. acetylcholine)\n• improves digestion by \n– slowing down the emptying of food from the stomach \n– stimulating the production of bile in the liver as well as its release \nfrom the gall bladder (digestion of fats and proteins)\n• a neuropeptide in the brain\n• an anorexin: The mechanism for hunger suppression is thought to \nbe a decrease in the rate of gastric emptying"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Peptide YY\n• released from cells in the ileum and colon in response to feeding.\n• it slows the gastric emptying and inhibits gastric motility\n• increases water and electrolyte absorption in the colon but \nsuppress pancreatic secretion. \n• obese people secrete less PYY than non-obese people"}, {"page": 12, "text": "• Anorexia nervosa\n• Bulimia\n• Binge eating\n• Orthorexia refers to an unhealthy obsession with eating “pure” \nfood. Food considered “pure” or “impure” can vary from person to \nperson\nPathophysiology of regulation of food intake and \nbody mass."}, {"page": 13, "text": "Basal metabolic rate \n• refers to the amount of calories that your body needs to perform \nbasic, life-sustaining functions. \n• It is the amount of energy expended at rest and neutral temperature. \nIt is also necessary for the digestive system to be inactive, which \nrequires at least a 12-hour fast. This number tells how much energy \nyou need to sustain your vital organs like your brain, kidney, liver, \nheart, lungs, skin, muscles and your nervous system.\n• It tells you how many calories your body would burn in a day if you \ndo absolutely nothing and lie in bed. "}, {"page": 14, "text": "Obesity is defined as a body mass index (BMI) that exceeds 30 kg/m2 and \ngenerally develops when caloric intake exceeds caloric expenditure in \ngenetically susceptible individuals.\nWaist-to-hip ratios is useful in visceral and peripheral obesity\nObesity is a risk factor for hypertension, stroke, hyperlipidemia, gallstones, \nnonalcoholic steatohepatitis, gastroesophageal reflux, osteoarthritis, \ninfectious disease, asthma, and obstructive sleep apnea.\nBody mass index (BMI) and waist-to-hip ratios (waist \ncircumference)\nSkinfold caliper"}, {"page": 15, "text": "The bioimpedance scale is a diagnostic device that determine the \npercentage composition of the human body based on different tissue \nimpedances. The method is based on supplying a weak alternating current \nto the organism. Thanks to the various ions, water behaves as a very good \nconductor. On the other hand, fat tissue has properties more like an \ninsulator. We can therefore measure different impedances in different \ntissues and derive the relative or total values ​​of the body composition from \nthis.\nTanita scale or InBODY"}, {"page": 16, "text": "• The glycemic index is a value assigned to foods based on how quickly those foods \ncause increases in blood glucose levels. Foods low on the glycemic index (GI) scale \ntend to release glucose slowly and steadily. Foods with high glycemic index increase \nblood glucose rapidly. For instance, we would need to consume more beets than rice \nto cause the same spike in blood sugar levels.\n• In particular, we measure the incremental area under the two-hour blood glucose \nresponse curve (AUC) following ingestion of a food with 50g of carbohydrate. The \nAUC of the test food is divided by the AUC of glucose (standard) and multiplied by \n100the.\n• Foods with a high GI are not necessarily unhealthy, and not all foods with a low GI \nare healthy. E.g.  Fat and protein in a specific food can slow down carbohydrate \nabsorption.\nGlycemic index \n"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Nitrogen balance\n• Nitrogen Balance = Nitrogen intake - Nitrogen loss (urinal urea and \ncreatinine)\n• Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) level and creatinine can be used in \nestimating nitrogen balance and can be used to study protein \nmetabolism.\n– Positive nitrogen balance: growth, hypothyroidism, tissue repair, and \npregnancy.\n– Negative nitrogen balance: burns, serious tissue injuries, \nfevers, hyperthyroidism, wasting diseases, during periods of \nfasting. A negative nitrogen balance can be used as part of a clinical \nevaluation of malnutrition."}, {"page": 18, "text": "• Creatinine is a breakdown product of creatine phosphate from muscle and \nprotein metabolism. It is released at a constant rate by the body (depending on \nmuscle mass). \n• Sarcopenia is associated with ↓ creatinine in blood and urine.\n• Creatine in meat is converted to creatinine on cooking, which is \nabsorbed, causing  increased serum creatinine.\n• ↓BUN levels indicate malnutrition and ↑ BUN indicates increased protein \nreleased from muscle tissue breakdown (autolysis) that occurs during \nstarvation to provide an energy source.\n• The BUN and creatinine is important for assessment of kidney function: ↑blood \ncreatinine and ↑ BUN\nNitrogen: Creatinine and urea"}, {"page": 19, "text": "• Azotemia: less severe condition with increased levels of urea but is not yet \nso severe as to produce symptoms \n• Uremia or Uremic syndrome: high levels of urea in the blood with \nsymptoms.\n• The terminal clinical manifestation of kidney failure (also called renal \nfailure)\n• Cause: Chronic renal insufficiency: Decreased glomerular filtration \nthat leads to tubular compensation with altered phenotype of tubular \ncells and decreased number of glomeruli in chronic kidney\nCatabolic state: Uremia and Uremic syndrome"}, {"page": 20, "text": "• Sort-term starvation: glucose, hepatic glycogen (glycogenolysis for 12-24 \nhours) and after app. 8 hours gluconeogenesis from pyruvate, lactate, \namino acids, glycerol portion of triglyceride (adrenaline, noradrenaline)\n• Long-term starvation (3 days): fatty \nacids provide energy to cardiac and \nskeletal muscle cells and ketone \nbodies sustain brain tissue. Some \nglucose is still needed as fuel for brain \ntissue.\n1. Simple starvation: Poor nutritional supplementation (e.g. poverty): \nmarasmus without hepatomegaly (e.g. anorexia nervosa) and \nmalnutrition such as kwashiorkor with hepatomegaly \n2. Non-simple starvation:\n1. Kwashiorkor-like syndrome: stress (disease)\n2. chronic diseases leading to malabsorption\n3. chronic diseases leading to cachexia (e.g. anorexia  Il-1 beta)\n4. aging or immobility leading to sarcopenia\nStarvation"}, {"page": 21, "text": "• The short-term normal starvation: Initially, the level ofinsulin in circulation \ndrops and the levels of glucagon, epinephrine, norepinephrine and GC \nrise: glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis.\n• Long-term normal starvation (simple): decreased circulating thyroid \nhormones, catecholamines and GC\n• Metabolic adaptation during stress: Catecholamines act transiently during \nthe initial exposure to stress and at later stages would be accompanied by \nthe secretion of glucocorticoids. GC promote hepatic gluconeogenesis and \nreduce glucose uptake and utilization in skeletal muscle and white adipose \ntissue. Chronic GC exposure results in hyperglycemia and insulin \nresistance.\nStarvation: hormones"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Simple starvation: Marasmus X Kwashiorkor "}, {"page": 23, "text": "Refeeding syndrome\n• Hypophosphatemia (a biochemical hallmark), abnormal sodium and fluid balance; \nchanges in glucose, protein, and fat metabolism; thiamine deficiency; \nhypokalemia; and hypomagnesemia.\n• Oral feeding resulted in fatal diarrhea, vomiting, heart failure and neurological \ncomplication (coma).\n• Diarrhea may occur during the early stages of refeeding, due to atrophy of the intestinal mucosa and pancreatic \nimpairment. The diarrhea generally resolves within the first few weeks of refeeding as the villous surface is \nreconstituted\n• To prevent refeeding syndrome, initial fluid and electrolyte balance should be \nachieved prior to starting caloric intake. Once calories are started, the goal should \nbe daily caloric requirements for age and weight divided into small frequent meals \nfor the first one to two weeks."}, {"page": 24, "text": "• Kwashiorkor-like syndrome is low circulating visceral proteins (albumin, \nretinol-binding protein, and transferrin) and a suppressed immune response \ncaused by stress starvation that accompany advanced diseases, e.g. \nalcohol associated liver disease\nStress starvation: Kwashiorkor-like syndrome: stress \n(disease): hormones"}, {"page": 25, "text": "• A complex syndrome associated with an underlying illness causing \nongoing muscle loss that is not entirely reversed with nutritional \nsupplementation.\n• Causes: end-stage cancer ( upper gastrointestinal and pancreatic \ncancers ), congestive heart failure, chronic obstructive pulmonary \ndisease, chronic kidney disease and AIDS.\n• Multifactorial pathophysiology: \n1. The inflammation: TNF (which is also nicknamed 'cachexin' or 'cachectin') \ninduces proteolysis and breakdown of myofibrillar proteins\n2. Tumor-derived molecules (e.g. proteolysis-inducing factor) induce protein \ndegradation\n3. Alteration in feeding control loops in cachexia: high levels of leptin, a hormone \nsecreted by adipocytes, block the release of neuropeptide Y leading to \ndecreased appetite  despite the high metabolic demand for nutrients\nCachexia "}, {"page": 26, "text": "All nutrients Proteins Hormones\nPoor diet Marasmus Kwashiorkor ↓GC, ↓thyroid \nhormones, ↓ \ncatecholamine\nStress starvation \nin advanced \ndiseases\nCachexia • Visceral proteins:\nKwashiorkor-like \nsyndrome\n• Muscles: \nsarcopenia\n↑GC \nNormal acute\nstress\n↑ epinephrine \nnorepinephrine\nSummary"}, {"page": 27, "text": "• A type of skeletal muscle loss that occurs with aging and/or immobility \n(loss of muscle mass and strength)\n• the replacement of muscle fibers with fat and fibrosis \n• changes in muscle metabolism,\n• oxidative stress \n• degeneration of the neuromuscular junction\nSarcopenia\nHandgrip"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Malassimilation syndrome\n• Malassimilation is the decreased ability of the GI tract to incorporate \nnutrients into the body, either due to maldigestion or malabsorption.\n• Primary: Ciliac disease, Tropical sprue, Whipple's disease, \nLactase deficiency or lactose intolerance \n• Secondary: Pancreatic insufficiency and Bile salt insufficiency"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Lactase deficiency or lactose intolerance\n• Congenital lactase deficiency (blacks)\n• Acquired lactase deficiency: \n• Risk factors: gluten-sensitive enteropathy, enteritis or bacterial\novergrowth\n• Undigested lactose leads to\n• Bacterial fermentation with gas production leads to bloating and \nflatulence\n• Osmotic diarrhea\n• Irritation and crampy pain "}, {"page": 30, "text": "Ciliac disease, Celiac sprue, nontropical sprue, \nendemic sprue, gluten enteropathy\n• Cause: abnormal reaction to gluten (gliadins and glutenins in corns or \nwheat) in genetically predisposed individuals (HLA-DQ)\n• Diagnosis: anti-transglutaminase antibodies and biopsy (villous atrophy)\n• Symptoms: \n• pale (anemia: iron, folic acid and vitamin B12 malabsorption )\n• calcium and vitamin D malabsorption (and compensatory \nsecondary hyperparathyroidism) leading to osteopenia or osteoporosis\n• decreased vitamin K leading to petechia (hemostasis) \n• selenium malabsorption and copper and zinc deficiencies\n• loose (large volume), abnormally bad smelling and greasy stool \n(steatorrhoea) leading to weight loss or failure to gain weight\n• abdominal pain, cramping, bloating with abdominal distension (due to \nfermentative production of bowel gas)\n• later mouth ulcers, lactose intolerance and \nadenocarcinoma and lymphoma"}, {"page": 31, "text": "2. HLA-DQ2 allele or less HLA-DQ8 allele:\nHLA-DQ is part of the MHC class II antigen-presenting \nreceptor. HLA-DQ bind to gliadin peptides more tightly \nthan other forms of the antigen-presenting receptor.\n1. Tissue \ntransglutaminase\nCiliac disease: pathophysiology\n1. Anti-tissue \ntransglutaminase \nantibodies (anti-tTG) : \ntTG catalize deamidation\nand transamidation of \ngluten. Transamidation\nforms the cross-linking of \na glutamine residue from \nthe gliadin peptide to a \nsecond protein with a \nlysine residue resulting \nin   permanently \ncovalently linked \ncomplex. This new \nepitopes is believed to \ntrigger the primary \nimmune response by \nwhich the autoantibodies \nagainst tTg develop\n3. Microbes"}, {"page": 32, "text": "• A malabsorption disease commonly found in tropical regions, marked \nwith abnormal flattening of the villi and inflammation of the lining of \nthe small intestine.\n• Signs and symptoms: diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, fever, malaise, weight \nloss, anorexia\n• Causes: \n• not known\n• persistent bacterial, viral, amoebal, or parasitic infections.\n• folic acid deficiency\n• persistent small intestinal bacterial overgrowth\nTropical sprue"}, {"page": 33, "text": "• A rare systemic infectious disease caused by   \nthe bacterium Tropheryma whipplei\n• Signs and symptoms: diarrhea, abdominal pain, weight loss, and joint \npains.\nWhipple's disease"}, {"page": 34, "text": "Pancreatic insufficiency\n• Causes of pancreatic insufficiency include chronic pancreatitis, \npancreatic carcinoma, pancreatic resection and cystic fibrosis.\n• Although pancreatic insufficiency causes poor digestion of all \nnutrients, fat maldigestion is the chief problem. A large amount of fat \nin the stool (steatorrhea) and weight loss are the most common \nsigns."}, {"page": 35, "text": "Bile salt insufficiency\n• Causes of bile salt insufficiency include \n– advanced liver disease, which decreases production of bile salts \n– obstruction of the common bile duct, which decreases flow of \nbile into the duodenum; \n– intestinal stasis (lack of motility), which permits overgrowth of \nintestinal bacteria that deconjugate bile salts; \n– diseases of the ileum, which prevent the reabsorption and \nrecycling of bile salts (enterohepatic circulation)\n• Sign: poor intestinal absorption of fat (steatorrhea) and fat-soluble \nvitamins (A, D, E, and K)."}, {"page": 36, "text": "Other courses of malassimilation: Crohn disease\n• CD affects any part of the gastrointestinal tract from the mouth to the anus. \nThe distal small intestine (malabsorption) and proximal large intestine are \nmost commonly affected (patchy distribution in the colon or ileum).\n• The typical chronic lesion is a skip granuloma having cobblestone \nprojections of inflamed tissue surrounded by areas of ulceration. It starts in \nthe intestinal submucosa and spreads across the intestinal wall to involve \nthe mucosa and serosa in areas overlying lymphoid tissue. Complications \ninclude obstruction, fistulae, abscess formation, and chronic blood loss.\n• Th1 immune response and IgG associated with severity\n• Its causes are unknown: aggressive response to normal flora bacteria in \ngenetically predisposed individuals (e.g. TLR)"}, {"page": 37, "text": "• The distal small intestine (malabsorption) and \nproximal large intestine are most commonly affected \nby patchy (local) distribution of lesions\nCrohn disease"}, {"page": 38, "text": "• It starts in the intestinal submucosa and spreads across the intestinal wall to \ninvolve the mucosa and serosa in areas overlying lymphoid tissue. \n• Complications include obstruction, fistulae, abscess formation, and chronic blood \nloss.\n• The affected serosis leads to adhesions in which fistulas are formed.\n• The fibrosis leads to stenosis and colocky pain (subileus state)\nCrohn disease: complications"}, {"page": 39, "text": "Crohn disease x Ulcer colitis\nContinuous \ninflammation in \nlarge colon and \nloss of haustra\n(taeniae relax)"}, {"page": 40, "text": "Other courses of malassimilation: Ulcerative colitis \n(UC)\n• UC is a chronic inflammatory disease that causes ulceration of the \ncolonic mucosa and extends proximally from the rectum into the \ncolon: continuous lesions, are limited to the mucosa, and are not \ntransmural. The mucous layer is thinner than normal bud edema and \nthickening of the muscular mucosae may narrow the lumen of the \ninvolved colon. \n• In chronic disease, inflammatory polyps (pseudopolyps) develop in \nthe colon from rapidly regenerating epithelium(also in CD). Mucosal \ndestruction causes bloody diarrhea with passage of purulent mucus \nbut severe hemorrhage is rare.\n• T cells, plasma cells in the inflamed colon and colonic epithelial \nantibodies IgG in the circulation\n• Its causes are unknown: bacterial, genetic, immunologic or dietary\nfactors"}, {"page": 41, "text": "References \nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\n• Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer \nHealth | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \n• McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, \n2018. \n• Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. "}]}, {"id": "vitamins-lipids-gout", "title": "P4 Disturbances of vitamin uptake, Pathophysiology of lipid and purine metabolism and Gout ZN", "file": "/lectures/vitamins-lipids-gout.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Disturbances of vitamin uptake. \nPathophysiology of lipid and \npurine metabolism. Gout. "}, {"page": 2, "text": "• Nutrients: macronutrients, micronutrients (essential trace elements, \nminerals and vitamins)\n• Lipoprotein metabolism disorders: hyperlipoproteinemia\n• Deficiencies in amino acid metabolism: nitrogen excretion disorders, \nphenylketonuria, homocystinuria\n• Disorders of the metabolism of purines, pyrimidines and sugars -\nhyperuricemia (gout), glycogenoses, galactosemia, fructosemia\nContent"}, {"page": 3, "text": "Nutrients\n1. Macronutrient (fat, protein, carbohydrate)\n2. Micronutrients\n– Essential trace elements: zinc (Zn), copper (Cu), selenium (Se), \nchromium (Cr), cobalt (Co), iodine (I), manganese (Mn), and \nmolybdenum (Mo).\n– Minerals: fluor (F), magnesium (Mg), iron (Fe), potassium (K), \nchlorine (CL), sodium (Na), calcium (Ca), phosphorus (P)\n– Vitamins"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Essential trace elements (a metal cofactor in \nenzymes)\nIodine Required for the synthesis of thyroid hormones deficiency: goiter, cretinism delayed normal \ngrowth and development (in childhood)\nExcess state: hyperthyroidism\nCobalt Required for the synthesis of vitamin B12 Deficiency: Pernicious anemia\nExcess state: cobalt poisoning\nZinc Zn-bound liver alcohol dehydrogenase,\ncarbonic anhydrase, SOD1 in cytoplasm and \nextracellular SOD3 (Both SODs have both Zn \nand Cu) and storage, secretion, and action \nof insulin\nHypothesis:  reduced reproductive function\nCopper Superoxide dismutase (SOD1) in cytoplasm \nand cytochrome C oxidase (or complex IV \ncontains both Cu and hemes with Fe)\n↓ inactivation of ROS\n, Wilson disease\nManganese Superoxide dismutase (SOD2) in mitochondria ↓ inactivation of ROS\nMolybdenum The active site of xanthine oxidize with Mo. \nThe enzyme can catalize xanthine to uric acid.\nHypothesis: hyperuricemia in ↑ Mo, gout\nSelenium Se-containing glutathione peroxidase which \ncan protect the organism from oxidative \ndamage\nHypothesis: ↓Se in hypothyroidism\nEssential trace elements"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Wilson disease\n• An inherited disorder (autosomal recessive pattern) in which excessive \namounts of copper accumulate in the body: the liver (1st) than brain and \neyes.\n– diminished biliary excretion of copper\n– failure to insert copper into ceruloplasmin (a glycoprotein that transports copper in the \nblood)\n• Excesses of copper generate free radicals that disrupt cellular organelles\n– clumsiness, tremors, difficulty walking, speech problems, impaired \nthinking ability, depression, anxiety, and mood swings. \n– mild liver disease: yellowing of the skin or of the eyes (jaundice), \ncirrhosis\n– green-to-brownish ring, called the Kayser-Fleischer ring\ndark rings that appear to \nencircle the iris of the eye"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Minerals"}, {"page": 7, "text": "• An excessive iron is stored as hemosiderin in the cells of many organs and \ntissues\n• Causes:\n• repeated blood transfusions\n• hemolytic anemia\n• excessive alcohol ingestion\n• Hereditary hemochromatosis: a genetic disorder of iron metabolism \nand the most severe example of iron overload caused by excessive \nintestinal absorption of elemental iron. \n• Manifestation:\n• liver (cirrhosis) \n• pancreatic cell (bronze diabetes)\n• bronze pigmentation of the skin\nHemosiderosis"}, {"page": 8, "text": "• Fat-soluble (DEKAF): They are stored (mainly D and A vitamins) and \ntheir shortage lead to late signs. Their excess leads to toxic \nhypervitaminoses (e.g. vitamin A: hepatomegaly, vomiting, bone and \njoint pain)\n• Water-soluble vitamins  (8 B vitamins and C  vitamin ): Except for B12 \nvitamin, they are not stored and their shortage lead to yearly signs.\nVitamins"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency\n• Severe and chronic deficiency lead to beriberi\n• Causes\n– alcoholics (vitamin B1 is stored in the liver)\n– polished rice"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Two forms of beriberi\n• Wet beriberi affects the cardiovascular system resulting in \ncardiomyopathy (dilated cardiomegaly, biventricular heart failure): \nhyperdynamic circulation (systemic vasodilation that is compensated \nby high cardiac output and fast heart rate, shortness of breath), \nand leg swelling.\n• Dry beriberi affects the nervous system by demyelination resulting \nin peripheral neuropathy and than muscle wasting\n– Tingling or numbness of the hands and feet and partial paralysis\n– Symmetrical and neuropathy: difficulty rising from squatting position\n– Confusion and pain\n– Impaired sensory perception and motor weakness, poor reflexes"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Dry beriberi: Wernicke encephalopathy \nWernicke encephalopathy (CNS) is characterized by the triad\n• ophthalmoplegia: refers to weakness (-paresis) or paralysis (-plegia) \nof one or more extraocular muscles which are responsible for eye \nmovements and later expanded to other eye movement disorders \n(nystagmus)\n• ataxia: lack of voluntary coordination of muscle movements\n• confusion"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Case (video)\n• Description\n• A 34-year-old man was presented to our emergency department in \nalcohol withdrawal.\n• Despite initial treatment with high-dose intravenous thiamine therapy \nhe went on to develop nystagmus , ataxia, pass pointing, intention \ntremor and worsening confusion. He was diagnosed with Wernickes\nencephalopathy, an acute neuropsychiatric syndrome resulting from \nthiamine deficiency\n• His serum magnesium levels were found to be low at 0.41 mmol/L \n(normal range 0.66–1.02 mmol/L). He was started on high-dose \nintravenous magnesium in addition to thiamine replacement and his \nneurological symptoms resolved once his serum magnesium levels \nhad normalised\n• https://casereports.bmj.com/content/2016/bcr-2016-218046"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Dry beriberi: Korsakoff syndrome\n• is often accompanied by Wernicke encephalopathy \n• an amnestic disorder: deficit in memory caused by brain damage. \n– confabulation, that is, invented or fabricated memories which are then taken by \nthe patient as true due to gaps in memory, with such gaps sometimes associated \nwith blackouts\n• hallucination\n• minimal content in conversation\n• apathy"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency\n• It is stored in the liver (storage for several years, not very common in \nyoung adults)\n– older people are more prone to this condition leading to peripheral \nneuropathy, neurodegenerative disorder or psychiatric problems). \n– in fetal and early life cause poor brain development and cognitive \nfunctions\n• Causes: \n– malabsorption due to intrinsic facto (IF) deficiency \n• atrophic gastritis (in the elderly)\n• surgical resection\n• intrinsic facto antibodies (pernicious anemia) often combination \nwith Graves' disease (hyperthyroidism) and other autoimmune \ndisorders\n– Inadequate dietary intake in vegans\n– Genetic diseases: common mutations in methylenetetrahydrofolate \nreductase (MTHFR) gene "}, {"page": 15, "text": "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin): biochemistry\n• conversion of homocysteine  to methionine \n• conversion of methylmalonal-CaA to succinyl-CoA \n• conversion of 5-methyltetrahydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate\n• Neurological \nmanifestation \ndue to low \nmethionine for \nmyelin sheaths\nLimited DNA synthesis leading to megaloblastic \nanemia and others resulting from the cells with  \nhigh turnover (GIT)\nHyperhomocysteinemia is \nregarded as a marker of \ncardiovascular disease (blood \nclots, heart attacks, and \nstrokes), likely working \nthrough atherogenesis\n• A lack for \nneurotrans\nmitter \nsynthesis"}, {"page": 16, "text": "• Pellagra (also known as “the three Ds”) is a disease marked \nby dementia, diarrhea and dermatitis.\n• If left untreated, pellagra can be fatal.\nseborrheic dermatitis: is a common skin \ncondition that causes scaly patches, red \nskin and stubborn dandruff.\nNiacin (vitamin B3) deficiency"}, {"page": 17, "text": "• Signs: \n• peripheral neuropathy and a pellagra-like syndrome with seborrheic \ndermatitis \n• glossitis and cheilosis\n• depression, confusion, seizures\n• Causes: \n• Alcoholism\n• Hemodialysis\n• pyridoxine-inactivating drugs (e.g. penicillamine)\nVitamin B6 deficiency (pyridoxine)\nCracked corners of the mouth in cheilosis and \nglossitis (inflammation with loss of the lingual \npapillae)\n"}, {"page": 18, "text": "• an antioxidant\n• facilitates the absorption of iron\n• collagen formation and scurvy in advanced deficiency (bleeding sores, \ntooth loss, anemia, and a reduced rate of healing for injuries). \nVitamin C deficiency"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Vitamin D deficiency \n• caused by \n– inadequate nutritional intake of vitamin D \n– disorders limiting vitamin D absorption \n• pancreatic insufficiency, \n• celiac disease\n• Cholestasis( failure of bile flow)\n– disorders impairing vitamin D conversion into \nactive metabolites—including certain liver, kidney\n– inadequate sunlight exposure (UV)"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Rachitic beads\n• In childhood: rachitis\n– bone deformities \n• craniotabes (softening or thinning of the skull), \n• outward bowed legs and hips \n• rosary or beading of the ribs\n• weak or soft bones leads to often fractures\n• In adult: osteomalacia\n– weak and soft bones \n– muscle weakness \n– joint and bone pain (secondary hyperparathyroidism)\nVitamin D deficiency "}, {"page": 21, "text": "Vitamin D deficiency \n• Biochemical findings: \n– low serum and urinary calcium  \n– low serum phosphate, except chronic kidney disease (decreased \nglomerular filtration leads to P accumulation that secondary \ndecrease Ca2+)  \n– elevated serum alkaline phosphatase due to an increase in \ncompensatory osteoblast activity (Note: High levels of ALP is \nalso indicate liver disease) \n– elevated parathyroid hormone (due to low calcium)\nOsteocalcin is vitamin \nK-dependent and a \nmarker of bone \nformation (note: it \ncorrelates with \nincreases in bone \nmineral density) that \nalso increases insulin \nsensitivity in many \ntissues"}, {"page": 22, "text": "hormone Response to serum bone GIT kidney\nParathyroid hormone\nparathyroid gland\n↓ Ca2+ hypercalcemia ↓ via calcitriol ↑ reabsorption \nof Ca2+ but \nexcretion of P\nand HCO3 \n-\nCalcitriol\n(from cholesterol in the skin, \nthan kidney and liver)\nparathyroid \nhormone\nDepends on concentration of\ncalcitriol\n↑absorption of Ca2+\nand P in same ratio\n(independent on \nparathyroid hormone)\n↑ reabsorption \nof Ca2+ and P\nCalcitonin \nthyroid gland\nAcute (not \nchronic) \nhypercalcemia\nhypocalcemia short-term \nbone \ncalciphicati\non\n↓absorption of Ca2+\nand P in same ratio\n↑excretion of \nboth Ca2+ and P\nVitamin D \n• The overall effect of PTH secretion is to increase serum calcium \nconcentration and decrease serum phosphate level.\nRickets: Rachitic \nbeads\n"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Vitamin E deficiency\n• Causes: Fat malabsorption\n• Vitamin E is an antioxidant.\n• Sign:\n• neurological and neuromuscular problems: poor conduction of \nelectrical impulses along nerves due to changes in nerve \nmembrane structure and function ( the damaging effects of free \nradicals ).\n• hemolytic anemia – due to oxidative damage to red blood cells. "}, {"page": 24, "text": "Vitamin K deficiency\n• Causes:\n• hepatic failure \n• vitamin K1-antagonist such as warfarin\n• disruptions of colonic bacteria (antibiotics and newborns)\n• Colonic bacteria synthesize a significant portion of the Vitamin K required \nfor human needs and only small amount in food is needed. Vitamin K is \nchanged to its active form in the live.\n• Clotting factors II, VII, IX, X, and anti-coagulant proteins (protein \nC and protein S) and osteocalcin. \n• Signs: bruising, petechiae, hematomas and massive or chronic \nuncontrolled bleeding (nose ,GIT, brain)\n• Prothrombin time (PT or quick test) is elevated, but the partial \nthromboplastin time (PTT) is normal or only mildly prolonged because \nfactor VII has the shortest half-life."}, {"page": 25, "text": "Vitamin A deficiency\n• Dark blindness\n• Squamous epithelium due to keratinization (abnormal quantity of keratin): \n• Skin, upper respiratory passage and urinary tract\n"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Disorders of the metabolism of purines, pyrimidines \n• Hyperuricemia and gout: DNA and uric acid\n• Porphyria: heme synthesis"}, {"page": 27, "text": "• Uric acid is a breakdown product of purine nucleotides (DNA)\n• Cause (hyperuricemia):\n• Overproduction of uric acid: \nAccelerated rate of purine synthesis accompanied by an \noverproduction of uric acid or some patients break down \npurine nucleotides \n• Underexcretion of uric acid:\n• Pathophysiology: In gout, undigested uric acid accumulates within \nlysosomes, damaging the lysosomal membrane. Subsequent enzyme \nleakage results in cell death and tissue injury (the inflammation).\nGouty tophus\nGout: deposits of urate crystals"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Tophaceous deposits\n• Decreasing temperatures cause both urate \nand uric acid solubility to fall. \n• Lead poisoning can interfere with excretion \nof urate and predispose to gout.\nGout: typical manifestation \n"}, {"page": 29, "text": "• Porphyrias are metabolic disorders of the heme biosynthesis\n• Porphyrins build up in the body and affect the liver or erythropoiesis \n• Two types\n• hepatic porphyria: accumulation of porphirins in the liver\n• acute intermittent porphyria\n• porphyria cutanea tarda\n• erythropoietic porphyria: accumulation of porphirins in the bone marrow \nand erythrocytes\n• congenital erythropoietic porphyria = Gunther disease extremely \nrare: severe photosensitivity with erythema, swelling, blistering,\nhemolytic anemia, splenomegaly\nPorphyrias\nHeme synthesis pathway"}, {"page": 30, "text": "• An autosomal dominant porphyria resulting in about 50% normal activity \nof the affected enzyme. \n• Intermittent: The triggers for acute attacks can be medications.\n• Its manifestation is an acute attack: The exact mechanism of acute \nattacks is not clear. One hypothesis is a toxic effects on neurons. The \nmajor symptom of an acute attack is abdominal pain, often accompanied \nby vomiting, hypertension, and tachycardia. Severe attack: motor \nneuropathy: quadriplegia (paralysis of all four limbs) central nervous \nsystem: seizures and coma.\n• 90% of affected individuals never experience an acute attack and are \nasymptomatic\n• The absence of photosensitive\nAcute porphyrias: \nacute intermittent porphyria (AIP)"}, {"page": 31, "text": "• the most common type of porphyria\n• PCT is due to underlying liver \ndisease (excessive alcohol consumption hepatitis B and C) or \ninherited in an autosomal dominant pattern (only 20%)\n• A primary cause: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency (UROD: \none of the enzyme responsible for heme production. ).\n• primary manifestation is with skin disease (cutaneous porphyrias)\n• Porphyrins are photoactive molecules: exposure to light results in \npromotion of electrons to higher energy levels. When these return to \nthe resting energy level or ground state, energy is released. This \ncauses local skin damage.\n• fluid or blood-filled blisters, \nostinflammatory pigmentation and cysts (milia)\nCutaneous/chronic porphyrias: \nporphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)"}, {"page": 32, "text": "• Abnormal concentrations of serum lipoproteins\n• Causes:\n• Primary dyslipoproteinemias result from:\n• genetic defects (familial dyslipoproteinemias)\n• dietary factors \n• Secondary dyslipoproteinemias result from diabetes, hypothyroidism, \npancreatitis, renal nephrosis and medications such as certain \ndiuretics, beta-blockers or glucocorticoids.\nDyslipidemia (or dyslipoproteinemia)"}, {"page": 33, "text": "• autosomal dominant disorders with various genetic defects in LDL receptor\n• accelerated atherosclerosis, coronary disease and fatal myocardial \ninfarction\n• cholesterol deposits in skin and tendons (xanthomas)\n• plasma cholesterol levels are approximately twice as high as normal\nFamilial hypercholesterolemia (FH) \n"}, {"page": 34, "text": "• Hyperlipidemia is classified according to which types of lipids are \nelevated:\n• hypercholesterolemia: The UK National Health Service \nrecommends upper limits of total cholesterol of 5 mmol/L, and \nlow-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL) of 3 mmol/L.\n• hypertriglyceridemia\n• combined hyperlipidemia.\nDyslipidemia (or dyslipoproteinemia): classification"}, {"page": 35, "text": "Lipoprotein metabolism disorders\n"}, {"page": 36, "text": "• A lipoprotein is an assembly whose primary purpose is to \ntransport hydrophobic lipid molecules in water (blood).\n• A lipoprotein consists of \n• lipid center (triglycerides and cholesteryl esters = a dietary lipid, is \nan ester of cholesterol)\n• outer shell, with the hydrophilic portions oriented outward toward the \nplasma and lipophilic portions oriented inward toward the lipid \ncenter. (phospholipids, cholesterol and embedded apolipoproteins that \ndetermines its fate).\nLipoprotein"}, {"page": 37, "text": "• Chylomicrons transport exogenous (dietary) products from the intestines to \nother locations in the body. \n• Nascent chylomicron: The enterocytes use fatty acids \nand monoacylglycerides from the chime and transform again into \ntriglycerides and add apolipoprotein B-48\n• Mature chylomicrons: Nascent chylomicrons interact with HDL particles, \nresulting in HDL donation of apolipoprotein C-II and apolipoprotein E to the \nnascent chylomicron. \n• Via apolipoprotein C-II, mature chylomicrons activate lipoprotein \nlipase (LPL), an enzyme on endothelial cells lining the blood vessels in \nthe adipose (mainly) and muscle (less) tissues. LPL catalyzes \nthe hydrolysis of triglycerides that releases glycerol and fatty acids. \n• They are absorbed in peripheral tissues (adipose and muscle).\n• Chylomicron remnants: When a large portion of the triglyceride core has \nbeen hydrolyzed, chylomicron remnants are formed and are taken up by \nthe liver (apolipoprotein E).\nExogenous pathway of Lipoproteins"}, {"page": 38, "text": "• Very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL): is a type of lipoprotein made by \nthe liver (e.g. after the excessive incomes of sugar or ethanol). VLDL \ntransports endogenous triglycerides and circulates in blood. Lipoprotein \nlipase (LPL) removes the triglycerides  from VLDL for storage or energy \nproduction. \n• Intermediate-density lipoprotein (IDL) or VLDL remnants: As more and more \ntriglycerides are removed from the VLDL, it becomes IDL. Via an interaction \nbetween apolipoprotein E and the remnant receptor, IDL are absorbed by \nthe liver or escape to produce LDL. \n• IDL remnants called low-density lipoproteins (LDL): If their cholesterol \ncontent becomes greater than the content of triglyceride, IDL becomes LDL. \nThe majority of LDL content (cholesterol) is either stored, secreted in bile \n(free cholesterol) used for cell membrane structure (cholesterol in the \ncellular memrane), or converted into other products such as steroid \nhormones or bile acids. Macrophages remover small part of the circulating \nLDL. \nEndogenous pathway of Lipoproteins"}, {"page": 39, "text": "• HDL is produced by the liver. It is the densest because it contains the \nhighest proportion of protein to lipids.\n• HDL remove  the tissue cholesterol from e.g. dead cells and transfer \nit to VLDL and then LDL .\n• HDL is able to prevent the process of oxidation of LDL and the \nsubsequent inflammatory processes seen after oxidation.\n• HDL has the ability to bind LPS, creating HDL-LPS complexes to \nneutralize the harmful effects in the body and clear the LPS from the \nbody.\n• LDL contains apolipoprotein B (apoB), which allows LDL to bind to \ndifferent tissues, such as the artery wall \n• if they have been damaged by high blood sugar levels.\n• if oxidised, the LDL can become trapped in the proteoglycans, \npreventing its removal by HDL cholesterol efflux. Normal functioning.\nEndogenous pathway II"}, {"page": 40, "text": "Defects in Amino Acid Metabolism\n• An enzymatic deficiency are associated with increased blood \nconcentrations of the amino acid whose degradation pathway is impaired \nand with the appearance of the amino acid in the urine.\n• Examples:\n• Phenylketonuria (PKU)\n• Homocystinuria (HCU)"}, {"page": 41, "text": "• Genetic defect in enzymes that convert homocysteine to methionine or other \nof the amino acids (an inherited autosomal recessive trait) \n• Pathophysiology: an accumulation of the amino acid homocysteine in \nthe serum\n• Signs and symptoms:\n• Intellectual disability\n• Musculoskeletal diseases: osteoporosis, abnormally-shaped chest and \nbone and joint problems\n• Vascular diseases: Homocysteine binds to the endothelium of the \nblood vessels and activate signaling pathways leading to the release \nof pro-inflammatory molecules. This may cause the following :\n• Extensive atheroma formation at a young age which affects many \narteries but not the coronary arteries\n• Intravascular thrombosis\n• Symptoms of homocystinuria can also be caused by a deficiency of vitamins \nB6, B12, or folate\nHomocystinuria (HCU)"}, {"page": 42, "text": "Phenylketonuria (PKU):\n• Mutations of the phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) gene results in \nphenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency and the inability of the body to \nconvert the essential amino acid phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to the \naccumulation of phenylalanine in the serum.\n• The accumulation of phenylalanine: brain growth slow, intellectual \ndisability, mental retardation, jerking movements, seizures, skin \nrashes and attention problems.\n• The lack of tyrosine : blond hair, blue eyes, and fair skin (lack of \nmelanin)\n"}, {"page": 43, "text": "Disorders of the metabolism of sugars: hypoglycemia\n• Hypoglycemia: 3 mmol/L (more dangerous than hyperglycemia, first \nmanifestation is activation of sympathetic NS and advanced hypoglycemia leads to \ncerebral edema and coma)\n• Postprandial hypoglycemia (after food)\n• Alimentary hyperinsulinism:  After GIT operation, some people with rapid \nstomach emptying leading to fast release of insulin\n• Galactosemia: a rare genetic metabolic disorder preventing  glucose release \n• Fructosemia: fructose intolerance, genetic disease an accumulation of \nfructose-1-phosphate which, over time, results in the death of liver cells, \njaundice + hypoglycemia (fructosa-1phosphate inhibits gluconeogenesis)\n• Hypoglycemia with low production of glucose\n• Enzymopathy: glycogenosis\n• Endocrinopathy: hypopituitarism or hypocorticalism (Addison disease)\n• Sever malnutrition\n• Alcohol and drugs\n• Excessive glucose consumption\n• Excessive energetic demand: normoinsulinism\n• Excessive effect of insulin: hyperinsulinism\n• Insulinoma: a rare tumor of endocrine cells in pancreas\n• Treatment of Type I and II diabetes mellitus"}, {"page": 44, "text": "• Hyperglycemia:  7mmol/L \n• Manifestation:\n• Glycosurie if serum glucose 10mmol/L, dehydration\n• Acute: Hyperglycemic and hyperosmolar coma in Type II diabetes mellitus\n• Chronic: AGE advanced glycation endproducts damage vessel wall, hemoglobin \nglycation, polyol pathway increasing sorbitol in some organs, ROS production\n• The 1st step in the sorbitol pathway is the conversion of glucose to sorbitol. \nThe 2nd step in this pathway is the conversion of sorbitol into fructose via \nthe enzyme sorbitol dehydrogenase. A point of concern is that not all tissues \nhave the enzyme sorbitol dehydrogenase, and these include the retina, \nkidneys, and Schwann cells. As a result, diabetic patients with ↑ glucose \nlevels accumulate sorbitol to toxic levels within these organs.\n• Causes of hyperglycemia:\n• Diabetes mellitus Type I\n• Diabetes mellitus Type II\n• Insulin resistance that accompany many diseases, e.g. infection\n• Increased corticoids: Cushing‘s syndrome or drugs\n• Adenoma of pituitary gland: acromegaly (STH)\n• Glucagonoma: a very rare tumor of the pancreatic alpha cells that results in the \noverproduction of the hormone, glucagon.\nDisorders of the metabolism of sugars: hyperglycemia"}, {"page": 45, "text": "References \nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\n• Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer \nHealth | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \n• McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, \n2018. \n• Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. "}]}, {"id": "nutrition-2025", "title": "KS_GM_nutrition_2025 (1)", "file": "/lectures/nutrition-2025.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Nutritional status disorders"}, {"page": 2, "text": " Basal metabolic rate\n Nutrition\n Carbohydrates\n Proteins\n Lipids\n Vitamins\n Nutritional status assessment\n Malnutrition\n Marasmus\n Kwashiorkor (kwashirkor-like syndrome)\n Malabsorption"}, {"page": 3, "text": "Basal metabolic rate\nThe lowest amount of energy expended to maintain vital processes in a \nwaking state\nphysical and mental calm\nfasting state (12-14 hours)\nthermoneutral environment\n10% HIGHER RESTING METABOLISM\nhttps://www.calculator.net/bmr-calculator.html\nsleep -10%\nlong-term starvation - 40% \nGrowth\neffort – up to 10x\nregeneration\nJOJO \nefect\nDAILY ENERGY OUTPUT\n7 000-10 000KJ/day\n"}, {"page": 4, "text": "REGULATION OF METABOLIC PROCESSES\n Nervous system – autonomic nervous system.For example, stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system leads to\nglycogenolysis.\n Liver, blood sugar regulation (glucose storage, glycogen formation and breakdown, gluconeogenesis), protein synthesis\n(albumin, coagulation factors, plasma transport proteins, acute phase proteins, etc.), urea, lipids are stored in it, lipoproteins\nare formed there, cholesterol is excreted or synthesized into bile acids, it is a storage site for many vitamins, etc. Their\ndetoxification function through hydroxylation reactions mediated by cytochromes P450 is significant, but it can be a source of\ntoxic metabolites.\n Digestive system – intake of water and food, its digestion and utilization, and excretion of certain substances. Lipoproteins\ncalled chylomicrons are formed in the intestine. The intestinal microbiota also has a significant influence on metabolic\nprocesses.\n Muscle mass is an important site of energy consumption (glucose, fatty acids, ketone bodies) and a reservoir of proteins in case\nthey are needed for the synthesis of other proteins (e.g., during inflammatory reactions or starvation). The main amino acid\nreleased by muscles is glutamine.\n Adipose tissue is an important energy store in the form of fat droplets. In adipose tissue, androgens are converted to\nestrogens, and many cytokines – adipokines – are produced that interfere with food intake (e.g., leptin) or metabolism\n(adiponectin, resistin).\n Bones (storage of substances, calcium reserves, buffering capacity for hydrogen ions).\n Skin – thermoregulation, storage of subcutaneous fat, metabolism of certain substances (e.g., production of vitamin D).\n The kidneys help maintain water and mineral balance and activate vitamin D through hydroxylation. Their excretory\n(\"cleansing\") ability is important, as is their ability to reabsorb important substances that are filtered into primary urine\n(glucose, amino acids).\n The respiratory and cardiovascular systems ensure an adequate supply of blood with oxygen and nutrients necessary for\nmetabolism, as well as the removal of metabolic waste products."}, {"page": 5, "text": "Hormones\n Insulin increases glucose uptake into muscle and adipose tissue cells by affecting\nthe membrane transporters for glucose GLUT4 and GLUT2 in hepatocytes. This\nreduces blood glucose levels, stimulates protein synthesis, glycogenogenesis, and\nlipogenesis, and inhibits lipolysis. Insulin suppresses appetite and induces the\nproduction of its antagonists in terms of their effect on blood glucose levels.\n Glucagon increases blood glucose levels by increasing glycogenolysis and\ngluconeogenesis. Its concentration rises physiologically during fasting and is\npathologically elevated in glucagonoma tumors.\n Growth hormone (STH; somatotropic hormone) has a proteoanabolic effect. It\nincreases glycemia by stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver\n(causingthe development of insulin resistance) and also has a lipolytic effect.\n Glucocorticoids mobilize energy resources for vital organs through catabolismof\nproteins in muscles (and also in the skin) and lipolysis in subcutaneous fat. In the\nliver, they have an anabolic effecton glycogen synthesis. They increase blood sugar\nlevels primarily by reducing glucose utilization(causing insulin resistance) and\nstimulating glucogenesis in the liver. They are stress hormonesand are partly\nresponsible for metabolic changes that occur in response to stress, including insulin\nresistance."}, {"page": 6, "text": " Thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which is several times more effective, cause an\noverall increase in metabolism,increase oxygen consumption and heat production in\nmost organs, sensitize tissuesto the effects of catecholamines (thus promoting lipolysis),\nand reduce blood cholesterol concentrations. Physiological concentrations have rather\nproteoanabolic effects, but excessive amounts of thesehormones have a catabolic effect.\n Sex hormones have a predominantly anabolic effect, especially androgens (increased\nmuscle mass,positive nitrogen balance).\n Estrogens inhibit bone resorption, affect the subcutaneous distribution ofadipose tissue,\nlead to sodium and water retention, and interfere with cholesterol metabolism\n(protectiveeffect against atherosclerosis).\n Progesterone has a thermogenic effect (increase in body temperatureduring the luteal\nphase of the ovarian cycle).\n Cytokines, tissue hormones, and neurotransmitters interfere with metabolism at many\nlevels.These include appetite regulators (e.g., leptin, neuropeptide Y),\ngastrointestinalhormones (cholecystokinin, etc.), adipokines (cytokines produced in\nadipose tissue), and inflammatorycytokines (e.g., IL-1, IL-6, TNF).\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/IgB-6l4_UJg"}, {"page": 7, "text": "1. carbohydrates\n● 1g – 17kJ\n● In the diet 60% (50-80%)\n● 3-5g/kg/day\nsimple\ncarbohydrates\n1/6\n• monosaccharides – glucose, fructose, galactose\n• disaccharides – sucrose, lactose, maltose\n• fruit, honey, sugar cane and beet, milk, confectionery, sweetened\nbeverages\nComplex\ncarbohydrates\n5/6 \n• polysaccharides – glycogen, starch, fiber\n• potatoes, grains, legumes, vegetables, whole grain products\nPolysaccharides representing fiber (cellulose, hemicellulose, mucin, pectins, and others), which is not \nabsorbed (and therefore is not a source of energy), but has a number of beneficial effects on intestinal \nactivity (e.g., slows down the absorption of carbohydrates, reduces cholesterol absorption, and \nprevents constipation)."}, {"page": 8, "text": "Functions of carbohydrates\n● IMMEDIATE ENERGY SOURCE (MAINLY BRAIN AND \nERYTHROCYTES) (glucose, fructose)\n● STORAGE SUBSTANCES (glycogen)\n● STRUCTURAL FUNCTIONS (glycoproteins, glycolipids)\n● COMPONENTS OF SOME MORE COMPLEX SUBSTANCES \n(nucleic acids, hormones, coenzymes)\n● de novo synthesis from glycerol and AMK"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Indigestible complex sugars - fiber\n• absorbs water, swells – feeling of satiety\n• ferments\n• nutrient for microbial flora – prebiotic\n• Formation of usable MK = source of energy \n• regulates the digestion of fats and carbohydrates\n• Pectin\nSoluble\n• does not ferment, is not a source of energy\n• increases volume in the intestines -> accelerates the passage of \nwaste and toxic substances, slows down the absorption of \ncholesterol\n• lignin, cellulose\nInsoluble\n20-35g daylly\n1:3"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Glycemic index\n● the rate at which glucose is utilized by the body from a given food\n● the area under the glycemic curve during two hours after ingestion \nof a given food, as PERCENTAGE OF PURE GLUCOSE INTAKE –\nGLUCOSE =100\n● Influenced by:\n– fiber content, food preparation, amount of chyme in the stomach\nLow GI less than 55 \nfruit, vegetables, \nnuts, whole wheat\nbread ...\nLow GI less than 55 High GI – more than 70 \ncornflakes, boiled \npotatoes, honey, \nchips, beer ...not \nsuitable for diabetics\nDM type 2, \nobesity\nbetter\nutilization of\nglucose\n"}, {"page": 11, "text": "2. proteins\n● 1g = 17kJ (23kJ - urea)\n● 10-15%, 0,8g/kg/day\nFunction\n VESSEL FILLING albumin \n BUILDING collagen, elastin, \nkeratin \n TRANSPORT AND STORAGE\nhemoglobin, transferrin\n ENSURING MOVEMENT \nactin, myosin\n CATALYTIC, CONTROLLING, \nREGULATING enzymes, \nhormones, receptors… \n PROTECTIVE, DEFENSIVE \nimmunoglobulin, fibrin, \nfibrinogen\nAA\nESSENTIAL    \nSEMI-ESSENTIAL \nNON-ESSENTIAL\nOligopeptides\n(2-10AA)\nPolypeptides\n(11-50/100AA) \nVlastní \nbílkoviny (up \nto 50/100AA)\nThe quality of proteins depends\non their amino acid composition.\nA complete protein diet is one\nthat contains all essential amino\nacids (lysine, leucine, isoleucine,\nvaline, methionine,\nphenylalanine, tryptophan,\nthreonine, and conditionally\nessential histidine and arginine).\nHigh-quality proteins must be\neasily digested and amino acids\nabsorbed by the body. Amino\nacids consumed in excess are\nnot stored in the body, but are\ndegraded by metabolic\nprocesses (formation of urea),\nthe carbon skeleton is used for\nthe synthesis of glycides\n(gluconeogenesis) and fats, and\noxidized to produce energy."}, {"page": 12, "text": "Proteins – Dietary Reference Intake (DRI) 15%\nChildern\n• 0,9-2,7 \ng/kg/day\nAdult\n• 0,8 g/kg/day\nSeniors\n• 1,0-1,2 \ng/kg/day\nNursing\nmothers\n• 1,5 g/kg/day\nAthletes\n• 1,6-2,2 \ng/kg/day (on \nanabolic \nsteroids even \nmore)\nLiver and kidney disease - individually depending on health condition\n"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Proteins - ureasynthetic cycle\n urea \n uremia (normal blood level 1.7-7.3mmol/l) \n increased protein intake \n catabolism \n kidney failure\nAA\npurines and pyrimidines \nheme synthesis \ncolon bacteria\nAMONIAK, \nNH4\nLIVER\nresorption of water and ions\nUREA\nURINE\nSWEAT"}, {"page": 14, "text": "https://books.google.cz/books?id=XUkhYLAC3HwC&pg=PA170&lpg=PA170&dq=synt%C3%A9za+hemu+a+produkce+amoniaku&source=bl&ots=xlfusD_egN&sig=hYJrVOYr4_keIgAVcalQzLhy3VA&hl=cs&sa=X&ved\n=2ahUKEwi6n86E--7dAhWDh6YKHZmOAe0Q6AEwAnoECAcQAQ#v=onepage&q&f=false"}, {"page": 15, "text": "3. lipids\n● 1g = 38kJ\n● 0,7–1,2g/kg/day\n● < 30%\n Simple fats and oils, \nwaxes\nComposite\nPhospholipids, \nglycolipids, \nlipoproteins\nFunction:\nCELL MEMBRANES\nSOLVENT OF VITAMINS\nCOMPONENT OF HORMONES\ncholesterol\nFORMATION OF GIT\nTHERMAL INSULATION\nENERGY RESERVE more than 50,000 kJ\nPotrava – FA from:\ntriacylglyceroles (90%)\nphospholipides\nCholesterol esters\nLipids are the most energy-rich nutrient, a\ncarrier of fat-soluble vitamins and a source\nof essential fatty acids.\nAll cells in the body, with the exception of\ncells of the central nervous system and\nerythrocytes, can directly use fatty acids as\na source of energy.\nThe composition of lipids in food,\nespecially the content of saturated or\nunsaturated fatty acids, plays an important\nrole."}, {"page": 16, "text": "4. Minerals\nCalcium, Ca Formation of bones and teeth, participates in nervous activity\nMagnesium, Mg Helps in bone formation, participates in nerve activity\nDraslik, K Maintains ion balance, participates in nervous activity\nSodium, NaMaintains ion balance, participates in nervous aktivity\n5. MICRONUTRIENTS AND TRACE ELEMENTS(Fe, I, Cu, Se, Cr, Mn, Mo, Co, F..)\nFe - An essential part of hemoglobin\nI - Essential for thyroxine (thyroid hormone)\nCu – collagen, elastin\nSe – antioxidant, THYROID hormone metabolism, deficiency – cardiomyopathy\nCr – stimulatory effect of insulin\nZn – forms the core of the insulin molecule\nMn – oxidative phosphorylation (metabolism of fats)\nMo – metabolism of purines\nCo – B12, leafy vegetables"}, {"page": 17, "text": "6. Vitamins\n The organism is dependent on the intake of vitamins or their precursors through food. Only small amounts of\nsome vitamins can be formed endogenously: vitamin D from steroid precursors, vitamin K and biotin by\nintestinal microflora.\n For fat-soluble (A, D, E, K), maldigestion or malabsorption of fats leads to combined hypovitaminosis;\nreserves in body fat (especially in the liver) prevent the development of deficiency symptoms for a long time\n(this applies especially to vitamins A and D); with excessive intake, their accumulation occurs and toxic\nmanifestations of hypervitaminosis may occur.\n Water-soluble vitamins (B, C) are in minimal reserves in the organism and with excessive intake, they are\nrapidly excreted by the kidneys and do not accumulate in the organism, on the contrary, hypovitaminosis\ndevelops faster for the same reasons. The exception is vitamin B12, whose reserves in the liver are sufficient\nfor several years.\n Hypovitaminosis\n insufficient intake of the vitamin through food; e.g. vitamin D deficiency in breast milk, B1 deficiency in areas where\nthe main food is peeled rice, vitamin B12 deficiency\n insufficient endogenous vitamin production; e.g. intestinal dysmicrobia, lack of UV radiation\n impaired resorption (impaired emulsification of bile fats, malabsorption syndrome of various etiologies, selective\nimpaired resorption of vitamin B12 in pernicious anemia)\n congenital or acquired disorder of vitamin metabolism at the level of its storage, transport or conversion into the active\nform (e.g. vitamin D-resistant rickets type I with a defect in the formation of 1,25-(OH)2-vitamin D3 in the kidneys)\n resistance to the vitamin (e.g. vitamin D-resistant rickets type II in the absence of the receptor for 1,25-(OH)2-vitamin\nD3)\n increased need for the vitamin (during growth, pregnancy, hyperthyroidism, malignancies, etc.)"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Deficiency of water-soluble vitamins\n Vitamin B1 (thiamin) \n is lost during processing (peeled rice, white sugar)\n Vitamin B1 is active in the form of thiamine pyrophosphate and acts as a cofactor in the oxidative \ndecarboxylation of γ-keto acids. maintains normal function of peripheral nerves.\n• polyneuropathy (dry form) caused by degradation of the myelin sheaths of motor and sensory \nnerve fibers, especially in the lower extremities; it is manifested by paresthesias, muscle weakness \nand hyporeflexia\n• cardiovascular syndrome (wet form) with hyperkinetic circulation caused by vasodilation of \narterioles; edema may be present, the heart may (but may not) be extremely dilated\n• Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is an encephalopathy with ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus, ataxia, \napathy, disorientation and even psychotic disorders. There are small hemorrhages and degenerative \nchanges of neurons in some parts of the brain.\nBERI-BERI Korsakoff's\npsychosis\nSE ASIA ALCOHOLICDRY FORM \ndemyelinisation\nWET FORM\nhyperkinetic circulation\n"}, {"page": 19, "text": " Vitamin B2 (riboflavin)\n Riboflavin, as a part of the coenzymes flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, participates in a\nnumber of oxidation-reduction reactions\n Vitamin B2 deficiency is associated with a deficiency of other vitamins and proteins-> developing countries\n In alcoholics and in patients with chronic infections or advanced malignancies.\n Deficiency is manifested by cheilosis (rhagades of the corners of the mouth, secondarily infected), atrophic\nglossitis, keratitis and dermatitis.\n source: meat, milk, eggs, yeast, vegetables\n Vitamin B6 (pyridoxin)\n This coenzyme is a cofactor for a number of transamination, carboxylation and deamination enzymes in amino\nacid metabolism and glycogen, necessary for the production of serotonin\n Vitamin B6 is present in practically all foods, but it can be lost during their processing.\n Secondary hypovitaminosis occurs with long-term use of drugs that act as pyridoxine antagonists\n(antituberculosis drug isoniazid, estrogens, penicillamine).\n Alcoholics suffer from vitamin B6 deficiency because acetaldehyde, potentiates the degradation of pyridoxine.\n The need for vitamin B6 is increased during pregnancy.\n The symptoms of vitamin B6 deficiency are similar to hypovitaminosis B2 – seborrheic dermatitis, cheilosis,\nglossitis, peripheral neuropathy."}, {"page": 20, "text": "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) \n= complex of organic compounds\ncontaining a cobalt atom inside a\ncircular molecule\nAbsorption:\n In the stomach, binding to the GP\ngroup complex – R-binder\n in the duodenum, cleaved and\nbound to IF (intrinsic factor)\n in the distal ileum, resorbed after\nbinding to specific receptors –\ncubilin -> by endocytosis\nTotal reserves of 4mg (1/2 in the\nliver, the rest in the bone marrow\nand elsewhere) last for 3-6 years\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1503/cmaj.1031155"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Deficiency – causes + symptoms\n Vitamin B12 deficiency is primarily a consequence\nof malabsorption\n In our conditions, the main cause is a deficiency of\nintrinsic factor (IF) due to an autoimmune reaction\nagainst the parietal cells of the stomach ->\natrophic gastritis with achlorhydria-> pernicious\nanemia\n IF deficiency occurs after gastrectomy and some\nbariatric operations and with reduced HCl\nproduction in the stomach\n anatomical intestinal lesions (strictures and\nfistulas)\n pseudo-obstructive intestinal conditions with\nsignificantly reduced intestinal motility\n(neuropathy in diabetes mellitus, scleroderma) ->\nmassive colonization of the small intestine by\nbacteria that consume vitamin B12\n resection of the terminal ileum (Crohn's disease)\n MTHFR polymorphism-> enzyme with reduced\nactivity. This may predispose to higher levels of\nhomocysteine, which is one of the risk factors for\natherosclerosis.\nimpaired DNA synthesis and cell division \n MEGALOBLASTIC ANEMIA\n leukopenia, thrombocytopenia\n glossitis, cheilosis, atrophy\n anorexia, diarrhea, dyspepsia\n Deficiency of methionine -> DEMYELINIZATION \n paresthesias in the extremities, later weakness,\nataxia, sphincter disorders, slow reflexes\n(peripheral neuropathy and spinal cord\ndegeneration) to severe dementia or psychosis\n These neurological disorders can also occur in \npatients without hematological symptoms\nneuroanemic syndrome\n"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Vit. C (Ascorbic acid ) \n in milk, animal products (liver, fish), fruits and vegetables\n Antioxidant, hydroxylation of procollagen\n elderly people living alone or in alcoholics, peritoneal dialysis or\nhemodialysis\n Def: collagen formation, Fe absorption\n Wound healing is impaired and scar strength is reduced\n Vitamin C deficiency in its advanced form is referred to as scurvy and\nis manifested by bone disorders in growing individuals (osteoid matrix\nformation disorder, Möller-Barlow disease), subperiosteal\nhematomas, skin hemorrhages, gingivitis, and recurrent bacterial\ninfections\nhttp://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-37320399\nFolic acid B9\n mainly found in vegetables and fruit\n only small folate stores, so a constant and sufficient intake through food is necessary\n increased need – pregnancy (cleft lip, cleft palate, neural tube), growth, hemolytic anemia, cancer\n most common causes of hypovitaminosis include insufficient intake, especially in alcoholics\n Hypovitaminosis due to malabsorption is part of malabsorption syndromes (gluten enteropathy, tropical\nsprue).\n Folate from the diet must first be converted into its active form - tetrahydrofolate (THF). This reaction is\ncatalyzed by the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase, which can be inhibited by some drugs (e.g., methotrexate),\nand its blockade can induce symptoms of folic acid hypovitaminosis\n megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, cheilosis, dyspeptic disorders, neurological symptoms are not present"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins\nVitamin A (retinol, retinal, retinoic acid and carotenoids – provitamins A)\n food of animal origin (liver, fish, eggs, milk), vegetables (spinach, carrots) contain carotenoids (carotenes)\n digestion and absorption of carotenes requires the presence of bile and pancreatic enzymes\n 90% of vitamin A in the liver (6 months)\n part of the pigments rhodopsin and iodopsin in the retina -> vision adaptation to lack of light\n EPITHELIAL DIFFERENTIATION – MAINLY DURING EMBRYOGENESIS\n ANTIOXIDANT\nLack:\n night blindness\n Keratinization of the corneal and conjunctival epithelium is manifested by their dryness (xerophthalmia), \nthe formation of erosions and even destruction of the cornea (keratomalacia) with loss of vision\n squamous metaplasia of the epithelium and its keratinization \n respiratory tract epithelium loses its protective antibacterial properties ->relapses of bronchitis and \nbronchopneumonia\n urinary tract epithelium ->higher frequency of urolithiasis\nExcess:\n toxic manifestations \n Acute -headaches, vomiting, stupor\n Chronic -weight loss, vomiting, bone and joint pain, hyperostosis, and hepatomegaly\n pregnancy – teratogen in smokers: risk of tumor formation (bronchogenic cancer)\n"}, {"page": 24, "text": "Vitamin D\n skin exposed to ultraviolet radiation (vitamin D3) 80%, sea fish and\nvegetables\n In the plasma bound to D-binding protein\n-> liver->conversion to 25-hydroxyvitamin D\n->kidneys-> active form 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (1,25(OH)2D(steroid\nagent)\n->binds to specific intracellular receptors (intestine, bones, skin and\nkidneys)-> maintain normal plasma calcium levels (neuromuscular activity,\nphosphate levels)\n 1,25(OH)2D stimulates the intestinal absorption of calcium and\nphosphates, together with parathyroid hormone mobilize bone calcium->\nparathyroid hormone-dependent reabsorption of calcium in the distal\ntubules of the kidneys\n"}, {"page": 25, "text": "Hypovitaminosis:\n combination of vitamin D deficiency in the diet and lack of UV radiation, peripheral resistance\nis rare\n impaired resorption in the intestine (cholestasis, pancreatic insufficiency, celiac disease)\n insufficient hydroxylation (hepatic, renal insufficiency, congenital vitamin D-dependent rickets\ntype I)\n impaired ossification of newly formed osteoid and an excess of unmineralized matrix\n During grow-skeletal deformations (skull deformation, rachitic rosary of ribs, saber-shaped\ntibia, triangular shape of pelvic bones) RACHITIS: COMPLETE DISORDER OF SKELETAL\nMATURATIONAND FORMATION\n Adult- insufficient mineralization interferes with the normal course of bone remodeling ->\nbone is weak, risk of fractures (osteomalacia).\n Laboratory hypocalcemia- increased bone turnover\n reduced resistance to infectious diseases -> metabolic syndrome and autoimmune diseases,\nincluding type 1 diabetes.\nHypervitaminosis\n hypercalcemia\n prolonged, causes excessive bone resorption\n Endogenous vitamin D production is increased in some granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis)\nhttps://www.dreamstime.com/stock-illustration-causes-rachitis-most-common-\nimage69147405"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Vitamin E\n a group of eight similar lipophilic compounds\n abundantly present in food (vegetables, meat)\n absorption is dependent on bile and pancreatic enzymes->resorbed vitamin E is\ntransported into the blood in chylomicrons and becomes part of lipoproteins, especially\nLDL->is stored in a number of tissues, especially in fat, but also in muscles and the liver\n Hypovitaminosis due to malnutrition is not known in developed countries\n antioxidants -scavengers of free oxygen radicals.\n prevention of atherogenesis ? (pro and con)\n gamete formation\nHypovitaminosis:\n in lipid malabsorption (cholestasis, pancreatic insufficiency, primary diseases of the small\nintestine)\n in the rare abetalipoproteinemia\n nervous system disorders (reflex disorders, position and vibration sensation, pain\nperception, muscle weakness, vision disorders).\n decreased immunity, infertility\n in newborns anemia\n"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Vitamin K \n synthesized by the intestinal flora, small exogenous intake through food is \nnecessary \n cofactor of hepatic microsomal gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which converts \nglutamyl residues to γ-carboxyglutamate\n creates a binding site for calcium\n VIT. K DEPENDENT PROTEINS\n essential for coagulation (anticoagulants ) factors (II, VII, IX, X, proteins C and S) \n Needed for carboxylation of osteocalcin present in osteoid\n In the absence of vitamin K, coagulation factors are still produced in the liver, \nbut in a non-functional form\nLack:\n deficiency due impaired fat resorption, liver failure or drug-induced \nhypovitaminosis\n Iatrogenic induced by dicumarol preparations (warfarin)\n Long-term treatment with dicumarol -> higher risk of developing osteoporosis\n BLEEDING\ncarboxylation\nCa2+ binding"}, {"page": 28, "text": "MALNUTRITION\n OBESITY\nNUTRITION\nQUALITATIVELY UNBALANCED FOOD"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Malnutrition diagnostics\nSGA = subjective global assessment\n patient eats a not healthy diet in which one or more nutrients are either not enough or are too much \n The aim is to identify malnourished patients or patients at risk of malnutrition/ 6 months\nwell-fed weight loss 10 %\nwithout somatic \nand functional \nsigns of \nmalnutrition\nslightly\nmalnourished\n 10 % somatic signs of \nmalnutrition\nseverely\nmalnourished\n 15 % somatic signs of \nmalnutrition\nfunctional signs \nof malnutrition\nSomatic: Loss of muscle mass: temporomandibular region, limb muscles, Loss of body fat, Skin\nchanges: loss of skin turgor, peeling skin, hair color changes, Swelling, hepatomegaly (steatosis)\nFunctional: immunodeficiency, atrophy with reduced function – bradycardia, hypotension"}, {"page": 30, "text": "Malnutrition\nreduced appetite\nInfertility\nlack of interest in food and drink\nhypothyroidsm\nfeeling tired all the time\nfeeling weaker\ngetting ill often and taking a long time to \nrecover\nanemia\nwounds taking a long time to heal\npoor concentration\nfeeling cold most of the time\nlow mood, sadness and depression"}, {"page": 31, "text": "1. Assessment of fat stores\nAnthropometry\nBMI\nwaist circumference\nmid-arm\ncircumference\nskinfold\nBioimpedance\nlow intensity, \nhigh frequency \ncurrent\nUltrasound\nvisceral fat"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Body mass index (BMI)\nObesity is defined as a body mass index (BMI) that exceeds 30 kg/m2 and generally\ndevelops when caloric intake exceeds caloric expenditure in genetically susceptible\nindividuals.\nWaist-to-hip ratios is useful in visceral and peripheral obesity\nObesity is a risk factor for hypertension, stroke, hyperlipidemia, gallstones, nonalcoholic\nsteatohepatitis, gastroesophageal reflux, osteoarthritis, infectious disease, asthma, and\nobstructive sleep apnea."}, {"page": 33, "text": "2. Assessment of protein reserves\n Body protein\n muscle (30%)\n visceral (70%) prealbumin, albumin, transferrin, …\n I. MEASUREMENT OF ARM CIRCUMFERENCE\n II. CREATININE\n is formed from creatine only in muscle\n is not reused\n is excreted only in urine\n increased excretion during physical exertion, acute illness, meat intake\n NITROGEN BALANCE"}, {"page": 34, "text": "Nitrogen balance\n= nitrogen intake - nitrogen excretion\n nitrogen intake\n food (enteral, parenteral nutrition)\n nitrogen excretion\n URINE - urea, creatinine-> laboratory measurement\n STOOL - exfoliated epithelia, digestive enzymes, undigested dietary proteins\n SKIN - exfoliated epithelia\nbalanced\n• adult healthy person\npositive\n• growth in childhood and \npuberty, pregnancy, \nrecovery from illness\nnegative\n• injury, serious illness, \nmalnutrition (lack of \nenergy and/or protein)\nINTAKE\nEXPENDITURE catabolism"}, {"page": 35, "text": "hunger center (VL)    and    satiety center (VM)\nOREXIGENIC SUBSTANCES ANOREXIGENIC SUBSTANCES\nGhrelin\nPeripheral organs, hypothalamus\nCholecystokinin (CCK)\nGIT\nNeuropeptide Y(NPY)\nhypothalamus\nGlucagon-like peptide\nGIT\nOrexin A/orexin B\nHypothalamus/ GIT\nPeptide YY(PYY3-36)\nGIT\nEndocannabinoid substances\nHypothalamus, periphery\nLeptin\nAdipose tissue, hypothalamus, brainstem\nSomatoliberin Adiponectin, \n• complex interaction of neurotransmitters with either orexigenic (resulting in obesitogenic) or anorexigenic \n(leptogenic, i.e. slimming) effects \n• The satiety center is stimulated immediately after a meal by stomach distension-> satiety center is also \ninfluenced by hormones released from the stomach wall, intestine and pancreas\n• Suppression of appetite by the postprandial rise in blood glucose\n• satiety regulation by protein intake\n• sugars have only a short-term effect \n• long-term regulation -the total body fat mass is used as a hormonally active tissue-> humoral signal sent by \na fat cell is the hormone leptin. Leptin production is directly proportional to the amount of body fat. \n• pain and some medications can decrease appetite\nInsulin"}, {"page": 36, "text": "SIMPLE starvation\n SHORT-TERM FASTING\n Within 72 hours\n glycogen depletion (<48h)\n Catecholamines and GC\n glycogenolysis, \ngluconeogenesis\n LONG-TERM FASTING\n Adult patients can usually survive more \nthan 3 weeks, consuming only water \n More than three days \n energy expenditure decreases by 40% \n DECREASE IN THYROID HORMONES\n DECREASE IN GC AND CA \n lipolysis increases\n ketone bodies in the liver (source for \nCNS) – decrease in pH\n proteins are saved \n 40th day proteocatabolism\n"}, {"page": 37, "text": "ketogenesis"}, {"page": 38, "text": "Malnutrition\n• = tumor emaciation, inability of tissues to \nadequately utilize nutrientsCACHEXIA\n• simple starvation – energy malnutrition in \nchildrenMARASMUS\n• protein deficiency –– protein malnutritionKWASHIORKOR \n• stress starvationKWASHIORKOR LIKE \nSY \n• Mixed, in adultsPROTEINO – ENERGY \nMALNUTRITION (PEM)\n• micronutrient deficiency (hypovitaminosis,…)DEFICIENCY\n"}, {"page": 39, "text": "MARASMUS\n Below 60% of weight for age\n malnutrition - balanced deficiency of essential nutrients and energy, i.e. \nproteins and fats \n uniform loss of subcutaneous fat and skeletal muscle\n simple starvation\n anorexia nervosa\n overall decreased metabolism\n TISSUE ATROPHY:\n BRADYCARDIA, HYPOTENSION\n dry thin skin, thin, falling hair\n MUCOSA ATROPHY:\n Chronic diarrhea\n SECONDARY MALABSORBATION – T and B immunodeficiencies, \nanemia, osteopathy\n patient dies from organ failure or infection\n NORMAL VISCERAL PROTEINS\n"}, {"page": 40, "text": "KWASHIORKOR\n● diet poor in complete proteins-> deficiency of one or more essential amino acids\n->DECREASE IN VISCERAL PROTEINS, LYMPHOCYTES\n● Energy intake may be adequate-energy source is predominantly carbohydrates\n● Normal appearance and weight, preserved fat stores\n● children in developing countries (predominantly corn or rice diet lacks the amino acid\ntryptophan)\n● Hypalbuminemia causes edema and ascites with general emaciation- Pitting edema with\nswelling of the ankles and feet\n● Protruding belly- Enlarged liver with fatty infiltrates\n● INFECTION, secondary immune disorders\n● MUSCLE ATROPHY\n● loss of appetite, vomiting, persistent diarrhea\n● growth disorders, change in the quality of skin, hair, nails, teeth\n"}, {"page": 41, "text": "kwashiorkor x   marasmus\nhttp://www.imgrum.org/tag/kwashiorkor/J0HV4d7kAAAAF0HVztaAwAAAFiYA\n"}, {"page": 42, "text": "kwashiorkorlike sy – stress starvation\n develops rapidly\n Stress: GC\n IMMUNOSUPRESSION\n Intensive protein katabolism- LOSS OF MUSCLE MASS + VISCERAL \nPROTEINS\n HYPOALBUMINEMIA\n ECT expansion, fluid retention\n false weight gain\n hepatomegaly\n hyperglycemia, hyperinsulinemia - insulin resistance\n skin and hair changes\n Therapy: amk supply to reduce negative nitrogen balance"}, {"page": 43, "text": "death"}, {"page": 44, "text": "P\nMg2+\nK+\nRefeedingsyndrom\n a set of metabolic abnormalities arising as a result of the restoration of food \nintake\n glucose + insulin into cells, restoration of ATP pump function\n decrease in serum levels of\n PHOSPHATES\n MAGNESIUM\n POTASSIUM\n Manifestations:\n muscle weakness\n water and sodium retention\n psychiatric changes and paresthesia\n up to malignant arrhythmia and heart failure\nATP\nGLU"}, {"page": 45, "text": "Anorexia nervosa\n psychogenic disorders of body image\n purposeful reduction in body weight by restricting food intake (sometimes associated with vomiting,\nabuse of diuretics, laxatives or thyroid hormones)\n chronic disease that leads to insufficient nutrient intake, secondary endocrine and metabolic\nchanges and disruption of bodily functions.\n Physical manifestations correspond to malnutrition, but a striking difference is voluntary physical\nhyperactivity as an additional tool for maintaining low body weight.\n Body weight is reduced by more than 15% compared to normal, BMI>18.5\n All components of the sex hormone axis are suppressed\n Reduced estrogen production in women is manifested by amenorrhea and the long-term\ndevelopment of osteoporosis. Hypercortisolemia and insufficient calcium intake also contribute to\nosteoporosis.\n The suppression of the sexual axis in men leads to infertility and loss of libido. The production of\nthyroliberin (TRH), thyrotropin hormone (TSH), thyroxine (T4) and especially triiodothyronine (T3) is\nreduced, along with a corresponding increase in rT3 (the so-called low T3 syndrome). However,\nclinical manifestations typical of hypothyroidism are absent and the thyroid gland responds normally\nto a functional stimulation test.\n delayed gastric emptying, dilatation of the jejunum, and constipation.\n Higher death rate than bulimia\n"}, {"page": 46, "text": "Orthorexia nervosa\n mental illness that can be translated as the right taste (from the\nGreek orthos = correct, orexis = taste). It is a pathological obsession\nwith healthy nutrition and biologically pure foods, which is why it is\nalso classified as an eating disorder.\nConsequences\n the body is deprived of a large number of important substances and\nvitamins\n significant weight loss occurs\n they constantly think about food in their thoughts\n over time, they only consume food that they have prepared\nthemselves\n they often convince those around them of the harmfulness of\ncommonly available foods"}, {"page": 47, "text": "bulimia nervosa\n Mental illness, WITHOUT WEIGHT LOSS\n episodes of overeating (bing eating), guilt\n VOMITING, LAXATIVES, DIURETICS ->LOSS OF K+\n Esophageal irritation – inflammation, erosion to bleeding\n Effect of NEUROPEPTIDES (cholecystokinin, endorphins, …)\n Teeth, nails tooth loss, brittle nails\n Internal environment\n METABOLICAL ALKALOSIS WITH HYPOKALEMIA, loss of Cl- and H+ arrhythmia\n Kidneys KALIOPENIC NEPHROPATHY\n GIT Degeneration of the myenteric plexus (laxatives), Mallory-Weiss\nSyndrome"}, {"page": 48, "text": "malabsorption\nCeliac disease\nLactose intolerance"}, {"page": 49, "text": "Malabsorption syndrome (malasimilatory)\n● MALDIGESCENCE\n● Disorder of intraluminal DIGESTION\n● MALABSORPTION\n● Impaired ABSORPTION of nutrients\n● Abnormal secretion of digestive juices\n● Severe disturbance of motility of the digestive \ntract\n● Insufficient food intake\n● decreased resorption of both water and ions\n● insufficient lipolysis\n● Steatorrhea (impaired pancreatic secretion, \naccompanied by a lack of fat soluble vitamins)\n"}, {"page": 50, "text": "Malabsorption\n Primary\n at the enterocyte level\n COELIA DISEASE\n DISACCHARIDASE DEFICIENCY \n(lactase deficiency)\n TROPICAL SPRUE\n CARBOHYDRATE TRANSPORT \nDISORDERS (glucose and galactose\nmalabsorption, fructose\nmalabsorption)\n AMINO ACIDS TRANSPORT \nDISORDERS (cystinuria)\n Secondary\n PANCREATIC MALDIGESTION AND \nMALABSORPTION (disturbance in the\npancreatic secretion)\n WHIPPLE'S DISEASE\n blind loop syndrome\n SHORT BOWEL SYNDROME\n POSTRADIATION ENTERITIS AND \nCOLITIS\n disturbance in the bile secretion"}, {"page": 51, "text": "Celiac disease, celiac sprue, gluten enteropathy\n ↑permeability of the\nintestinal mucosa to gliadin\n TISSUE TRANSGLUTAMINASE\n transfer of glutamyl residue, \nhigh specificity for gliadin\n+ deamination: ->NEW \nPEPTIDES = antigen -> binding\nto HLA DQ2/8 (macrophage)\n T,B-LYMPHOCYTES\n proinflammatory cytokines, \nantibodies\n inflammatory reaction\n DEVASTION OF \nJEJUNUM AND ILEUM\n↑PERMEABILITY\nDEAMINATION"}, {"page": 52, "text": "DIARRHEA, FAILURE TO BRING, GROWTH ARRESTED\n In the 6th-18th month of life\nAtypical:\n more than 50% may not have intestinal problems or diarrhea\n? DERMATITIS HERPETIFORMIS DUHRING\n bullous skin disease (IgA)\n iron deficiency anemia, growth retardation\n Hepatopathy, osteoporosis, intestinal findings consistent with celiac disease\nASYMPTOMATIC FORM\n no intestinal or extraintestinal symptoms\n lower physical performance,\n easier fatigue, tendency to depression, \n iron deficiency without anemia\n Bloating as a side effect of gluten\n GLUTEN-FREE DIET\n"}, {"page": 53, "text": "LACTOSE INTOLERANCE \n LACTOSE = GALACTOSE + GLUCOSE \n selective malabsorption disorder\n Congenital deficiency X Acquired\ndefect\n❖ PRIMARY \ngradual decrease in gene \nexpression\n❖ SECONDARY \ndecreased activity in \nintestinal diseases, \nADMINISTRATION OF ATB\nPLANT MILK\nDISACCHARIDASE DEFECT –\nLACTASE \n• inducible enzyme \nactivity increases after\nconsumption of lactose\nUNABSORBED LACTOSE \nLACTIC ACID, CO2 , CH4 \n• osmotic diarrhea, flatulence \n• abdominal pain, sour-smelling stool\nBACTERIAL MICROFLORA"}, {"page": 54, "text": "Clinically\n DIARRHEA\n WEIGHT LOSS\n WEAKNESS AND INEFFICIENCY\n ANEMIA\n EDUMS, MUSCLE ATROPHY\n HAEMORRHYMATIC DISORDERS\n SKIN, HAIR, NAIL DISORDERS\n MENSTURAL DISORDERS, INFERTILITY\n OSTEOPOROSIS, PARESTHESIA/TETANIA"}, {"page": 55, "text": "http://pfyziolklin.upol.cz/?p=4356\n"}, {"page": 56, "text": " Obesity is a syndrome characterized by excessive accumulation of energy reserves in the form \nof body fat. \n excess of fat reserves stored outside of physiological adipose tissue, which increases health \nrisks and shortens life expectancy\n social, neuropsychic, metabolic and endocrine factors\n genetic predispositions 40% \n social and psychological (family tradition, preference for fatty and sweet foods, psychological \nstress)\n metabolic (various resting and postprandial energy expenditure, spontaneous physical activity, \nlipoprotein lipase activity, expression of β3-catecholamine receptors)\n endocrine (insulin production, insulin sensitivity, cortisol, growth hormone, sex hormones).\nPrimary-Risk periods -periods of\nhormonal changes –puberty,\npregnancy and postpartum,\nmenopause, andropause.\nObesity= hormonal imbalance\nINSULIN\nLEPTIN\nADIPONECTIN\n"}, {"page": 57, "text": "Secondary obesity \nonly 3–5%\n accompanies treatment with some drugs that interfere with lipid and energy metabolism and central\nregulation of food intake\n hormones with lipo-anabolic effect consists of cortisol, insulin and estrogens. Their pathological excess is\ntherefore often associated with obesity. Lipo-catabolic hormones include glucagon, STH and catecholamines\n(acting via β2 and β3 receptors).\n Cushing's syndrome – hypercortisolism manifests itself both by increasing appetite and increasing visceral fat\ndeposition-moon-shaped face, fat deposition on the neck, supraclavicular and abdominally.\n Hypothyroidism – obesity is associated with reduced energy consumption; however, hypothyroidism does not\nlead to severe obesity, the weight gain does not exceed 5–10 kg.\n Insulinoma – hyperinsulinemia stimulates food intake with recurrent hypoglycemia (however, most patients\nwith islet tumors of Langerhans are not obese).\n Hypogonadism.\n Hypothalamic obesity is a rare type of obesity in which the function of the ventromedial centers of the\nhypothalamus is genetically determined or acquired (tumors, radiation, surgery, inflammation) is altered.\n Obesity develops in childhood and is the result of a combination of dysfunction of the hypothalamic centers of\nfood intake and hormonal disorders promoting obesity (hypogonadism, STH deficiency)\n There are also monogenic types of obesity resulting from a genetically determined disorder of the formation of\na mediator or its receptor, leptin, leptin receptor, POMC, etc."}, {"page": 58, "text": "obesity in children \n Predisposing factors and lifestyle \n Intrauterine development and \n prepuberty and puberty, especially in girls.\n early onset of its metabolic complications. \n Hyperlipidemia\n arterial hypertension\n Hepatic steatosis\n impaired glucose metabolism"}, {"page": 59, "text": "Endocrine activity of adipose tissue and systemic \ninflammation\n fat cells -> the target organ of cortisol, insulin, STH, sex hormones, thyroid hormones, \netc.\n In men and postmenopausal women, is adipose tissue the main source of estrogens, \nwhich block osteoclastic bone resorption and slow down the development of \nosteoporosis \n In obese women, excess estrogen can lead to ovarian cycle disorders. \n Until menopause, progesterone largely eliminates the carcinogenic effect of estrogen \non estrogen-dependent organs, especially the uterus -> obese women: persistent \nestrogen production in adipose tissue increases the risk of breast and uterine cancer\n The production of adipokines with insulin-resistant effects and pro-inflammatory \ncytokines increases when the physiological capacity of adipose tissue to store lipids is \nexceeded. \n Hypertrophied adipocytes die to an increased extent and provoke an inflammatory \nresponse including increased accumulation of inflammatory cells in adipose tissue. \n Pro-inflammatory cytokines released from adipose tissue cause low-intensity systemic \ninflammation, but with long-term effects on the body, including the acceleration of \natherosclerosis. "}, {"page": 60, "text": "metabolic syndrome\n obesity (abdominal);\n insulin resistance (type 2 diabetes mellitus);\n arterial hypertension;\n hyperlipoproteinemia;\n hypercoagulable state\n The central part of the metabolic syndrome is insulin resistance\n Visceral fat is more metabolically active\n Insufficient glucose uptake by muscle cells due to a reduced amount of GLUT4 on their membrane and increased\ngluconeogenesis in the liver cause hyperglycemia.\n Hyperglycemia increases insulin secretion-> hyperinsulinemia\n Insulin inhibits lipolysis in adipose tissue and insulin resistance is thus also linked to android-type obesity and its\nmetabolic and cardiovascular complications.\n When the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans are unable to compensate for insulin resistance with sufficient\nhyperinsulinemia, type 2 diabetes mellitus develops.\n Hyperinsulinemia predisposes to arterial hypertension by increasing sodium retention in the kidneys, stimulating\nthe formation of vasoconstricting endothelin and, conversely, reducing the production of (vasodilating) nitric\noxide in the endothelium, and by increasing the tone of the sympathetic nervous system.\n The main complications of metabolic syndrome therefore include cardiovascular disease due to accelerated\natherosclerosis and a procoagulant state, as well as type 2 diabetes mellitus and some malignant tumors due to\nhyperinsulinemia."}, {"page": 61, "text": "Disorders of purine, \npyrimidine and sugar \nmetabolism"}, {"page": 62, "text": "Purine metabolism\n– adenine, guanine\nXanthine oxidase (XO) \nadenine → hypoxanthine → \nxanthine\nguanine → xanthine → uric acid \npart of hypoxanthine and xanthine is\nconverted back to nucleotides and \nutilized\nHypoxanthine-guanine-\nphosphoribosyltransferase HGPRT\n"}, {"page": 63, "text": "Hyperuricemia\n= increased concentration of uric acid in the blood\n from food \n purine metabolism\ncongenital\n ENZYMATIC DEFECT HGPRT (Lesch-Nyhan syndrome – X-linked on.)\n Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase\n ↑phosphoribosyl diphosphate PRDP: does not enter nucleotide synthesis\n Encephalopathy, spastic paralysis, self-harm\nacquired\n ↑ NUCLEOTIDE CATABOLISM \n cytostatics, more severe catabolic states, fructose\n ↑ DIETARY SUPPLY \n ↓URIC ACID EXCLUDING \n renal insufficiency, drugs – thiazide diuretics, alcohol\n influence of urine pH – better excreted in alkaline X lactate"}, {"page": 64, "text": "GOUT\n more common in men and in people with a high meat intake \n combination of various factors (increased synthesis, selective transport \ndisorders at the level of the renal tubules)\n heterogeneous group of disease states whose common feature is hyperuricemia \nand formation + deposition of urate crystals in tissues, especially in joints and \ntheir surroundings primary – idiopathic x secondary \n Microcrystals are phagocytosed by leukocytes and thus trigger a cascade of local \ninflammatory processes\n Most affected: \n JOINTS \n KIDNEYS \n urate nephrolithiasis, tubulointerstitial nephritis sometimes leading to renal insufficiency\n CIRCULATORY SYSTEM \n acceleration of atherosclerosis, more frequent thrombosis \n eyes, skin, more frequent DM2"}, {"page": 65, "text": "Acute gout attack\nCrystals falling out into the joint \ncavity (reduced temperature \nand reduced pH at night)\nsubsequently coated with\nIgG\nINFLAMMATION\nphagocytosis neutrophils + \nneutrophil damage crystals\nrelease of enzymes TISSUE DAMAGE\n90% monoarticular -> 70% 1st MTP joint\n• sudden onset (often at night), severe \npain,\njoint red, hot, hypersensitive,\ngeneral symptoms\n• average duration of attack 1 – 2 weeks\n"}, {"page": 66, "text": "Clinical stages of gout\n• ↑ S-KM\n• 90% men, 5th-6th decade\n• postmenopausal women (uricosuric effect of estrogens)\n1. Asymptomatic\nhyperuricemia\n• provoking moment: dietary excess, stress\n• ACUTE GOUTY ARTHRITIS\n2. Acute gout\nattack\n• rest intervals between attacks ½ - 2 years\n3. Intercritical\nperiod\n• TOPHOSIC DEPOSIT: cartilage, synovium, tendons, skin\n• urate nephropathy\n4. Chronic\ntophic gout"}, {"page": 67, "text": "Secondary hyperuricemia\n Increased turnover or breakdown of nuclear elements\n Myelo- and lymphoproliferative diseases, increased production of\nerythroblasts (hemolytic and macrocytic anemias), cytostatics, crush\nsyndrome \n Drugs\n Cytostatics, corticosteroids in high doses (catabolism), blocking urate\nsecretion – diuretics (furosemide) \n Renal insufficiency\n Reduced glomerular filtration\n Chronic alcoholism\n Increased purine intake, ↓pH, lactate reduces excretion by the kidneys\n Chronic lead poisoning"}, {"page": 68, "text": "Urate nephropathy\n 1. URATE UROLITHIASIS \n formation and presence of urate stones in the urinary tract\n 10-25% of patients with gout\n risk of precipitation: ↓urine pH, dehydration\n→ RENAL COLICS \n 2. INTERSTITIAL GOUTY NEPHRITIS \n deposition of Na-urate crystals in the renal interstitium with subsequent inflammatory\nchanges\n→ albuminuria, ↓kidney concentration capacity, renal hypertension\n→ CHRONIC RENAL INSUFFICIENCY \n 3. ACUTE URATE TUBULOPATHY \n during radio or chemotherapy hematomalignancy\n ->URINARY TRACT OBSTRUCTION \n→ acute tubular necrosis (distal tubules and collecting ducts) \n→ ACUTE RENAL FAILURE"}, {"page": 69, "text": "Disorders of pyrimidine metabolism\nPyrimidine synthesis disorders\n UMP synthase deficiency (Orotic\naciduria) \n AR heredity \n Orate accumulation\n Crystalluria\nPyrimidine deficiency\n megaloblastic anemia\n growth disorders, psychomotor\nretardation, leukopenia, malaise\nPyrimidine degradation disorders\n Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase\n(DPD) deficiency\n AR inheritance \n Uracil and thymine accumulation\nComplete deficiency in children\n epilepsy, mental retardation, autism, \nmicrocephaly\nPartial deficiency\n manifestations of 5- flurouracil\ntherapy (tumors) \n neutropenia, stomatitis, neurological\nsymptoms"}, {"page": 70, "text": "Glycogenosis\n Storage polysaccharide\nstored in the cytoplasm of\nhealthy cells.\n Stored in the liver and\nmuscles.\n Can only be released into the circulation from the liver\n(glucose-6-phosphatase).\n Glycogenoses\n = genetically determined\ndisorders of\nglycogen metabolism\nGlycogen accumulates in the cells in the form of\ngranules.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LuVcPNF5Slg"}, {"page": 71, "text": "Glycogenosis\n Hepatic: GSD I, III, IV, VI \n Hepatomegaly\n Hypoglycemia (liver = main source of glucose! has glucose-6-\nphosphatase)\n↑ glucose-6-phosphate = ↑ lactate lipolysis → hyperlipidemia and ketogenesis → KAC\n• Myopathic: GSD V, VII\n• Glycogen accumulation in muscle\n• Muscle weakness\n• Muscle cramps\n• Necrosis (myoglobinuria)\nOther: GSD II\nInvolvement of other organs"}, {"page": 72, "text": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yvaqtsSceww"}, {"page": 73, "text": "Von Gierke's disease (GSD I)\n deficiency of the enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase → hypoglycemia (→ \ncramps) and lactic acidosis\n AR, incidence of GSD I in the general population is 1/100,000, Ashkenazi\nJewish population has 5 times higher prevalence\n G6Pase enzyme is primarily expressed in liver, kidneys and intestines\n→accumulation of glycogen and fat in liver, kidneys and intestinal mucosa\n hepatomegaly\n nephromegaly\n ↑ uric acid\n ↑ lipids"}, {"page": 74, "text": "Pompe disease (GSD II)\n↓ Lysosomal α1,4 glucosidase\n AR inheritance\n 1:40,000\n Deficiency of lysosomal enzymes + glycogen storage in skeletal and cardiac\nmuscle\n❖ Infantile type\nskeletal muscle → hypotonia, failure to thrive, respiratory muscle weakness → \nsleep apnea; myocardium → hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; vascular\nendothelium, astrocytes, liver → hepatomegaly, kidneys\n❖ Late type (juvenile and adult)\nmuscle weakness of the pelvic ring and proximal muscle groups of the limbs, \nexertional dyspnea, apnoeic pauses, feeding disorders (swallowing)\n→ MUSCLE HYPOTONY (at any time during life)"}, {"page": 75, "text": "Classical galactosemia\n AR heredity\n incidence is 1:35,000–1:50,000\n galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase deficiency\nbetween 4th–9th day after milk intake:\n❖ vomiting, diarrhea, milk refusal\n❖ hepatic insufficiency with jaundice and hepatomegaly\n❖ hemorrhagic diathesis\n❖ lethargy, convulsions\n❖ pseudotumor cerebri, brain edema\n❖ bilateral cataract (↑ galacticoll)\n PICTURE OF SEPSIS WITH LIVER AND RENAL FAILURE\n speech disorder\n in girls hypergonadotropic hypogonadism\nEvidence of increased galacticoll concentration in urine\nhttps://www.mountsinai.org/health-library/diseases-conditions/galactosemia"}, {"page": 76, "text": "Disorders of protein and \namino acid metabolism"}, {"page": 77, "text": ""}, {"page": 78, "text": "Phenylketonuria (PKU)\n 377 / 5 000\n AR, in the Czech Republic 1:6500, world 1:13000\n absence or reduced activity (600 gene mutations on chromosome 12 \ndescribed) of phenylalanine hydroxylase = hydroxylation of Phe to Tyr.\n alternative degradation of Phe – phenylpyruvate (transamination), \nphenyllactate, phenylacetate or phenylethylamine is formed\n -> mouse-like urine odor\n Tyr deficiency -> mental retardation, epilepsy, reduced pigmentation\n newborn screening"}, {"page": 79, "text": ""}, {"page": 80, "text": "Homocystinuria\n AR\n a metabolic disease that is caused most often by a disorder of the \nactivity of cystathione beta-synthase (CBS deficiency), an enzyme\ninvolved in the body in the conversion of the amino acid methionine \nto cysteine.\n In addition to the amino acid METHIONINE, the very toxic\nHOMOCYSTEINE also accumulates\n 1:83,000 to 1:15,000\nhttps://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/artic\nles/10.3389/fnut.2021.690359/full"}, {"page": 81, "text": "Homocystinuria\n Eye disorders: \n lens dislocation, myopia, glaucoma, retinal detachment, optic nerve atrophy, \ncataracts\n Bone disorders: \n metabolic osteopathy, osteoporosis, scoliosis, pathological fractures and \nvertebral collapse\n Central nervous system disorders: \n developmental delay and mental retardation (60%), seizures (50%), EEG \nabnormalities and psychiatric disorders\n Vascular disorders: \n thromboembolic complications (deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, \nstroke)"}, {"page": 82, "text": "DISORDERS OF LIPID AND \nLIPOPROTEIN METABOLISM"}, {"page": 83, "text": ""}, {"page": 84, "text": "Dyslipidemia (or dyslipoproteinemia)\n Abnormal concentrations of serum lipoproteins\nCauses:\n Primary dyslipoproteinemias result from:\n genetic defects (familial dyslipoproteinemias)\n dietary factors\n Secondary dyslipoproteinemias result from diabetes, hypothyroidism, \npancreatitis, renal nephrosis and medications such as certain\ndiuretics, beta-blockers or glucocorticoids."}, {"page": 85, "text": "Lipoproteins\n Chylomicrons (CM) -formed in the intestine and transport absorbed exogenous\ntriacylglycerols. The main apoprotein is B48.\n VLDL (Very Low Density LP ,pre-ß-LP) - transport endogenous triacylglycerols synthesized in\nthe liver.\n IDL (Intermediate-Density LP) are formed from VLDL by the action of lipoprotein lipase and\nhave a lower content of triacylglycerols with a relatively higher proportion of cholesterol\nesters.\n LDL (Low Density LP ,ß-LP) contain mainly cholesterol esters and a single apoprotein (B 100).\n HDL (High Density LP ,α-LP) reverse cholesterol transport. The main apoprotein is apoprotein\nA. HDL cholesterol (HDL-C) is an important protective factor in atherosclerosis; its low\nconcentrations are one of the components of the metabolic syndrome.\n Lipoprotein(a), Lp(a) is a lipoprotein particle of LDL with a molecule very similar to\nplasminogen attached. It is a significant risk factor for atherosclerosis. The liver is the key\norgan for LP metabolism. Lipoproteins are produced in it (“intrinsic pathways”) and it is the\nonly organ capable of eliminating cholesterol from the body, either directly into bile or after\nits conversion to bile acids. These are also part of bile, but undergo enterohepatic circulation.\nIn addition to the liver, lipoproteins are also produced in the intestine (“extrinsicpathways”).\n Molecules important for LP metabolism are apolipoproteins, enzymes and cell receptors."}, {"page": 86, "text": "Dyslipidemia \n increase in the concentration of some lipoproteins in plasma\n pathogenesis of atherosclerosis and its complications.\nThe cause is disorders of:\n enzymes\n receptors\n apolipoproteins\n excessive synthesis of own lipids\n Dyslipidemias are genetically determined-> complex genetic influences combine with external factors\n hyperlipidemia -increased concentration of lipids (triacylglycerols and cholesterol) in plasma.\n Hypercholesterolemia -increased concentration of cholesterol in the blood.\n Blood cholesterol is made up of up to 75% cholesterol contained in LDL -atherogenic.\n HDL also contain cholesterol, but from the point of view of the development of atherosclerosis, it acts\nas a protector with its function of reverse cholesterol transport.\n Hypertriacylglycerolemia -increase in the concentration of triacylglycerols in the blood.\n risk factor for pancreatitis and an independent factor for the development of atherosclerosis"}, {"page": 87, "text": "Primary dyslipidemia\n• monogenic or polygenic\ninheritance\n• referred to as \"familial\". \n• Familial combined\nhyperlipidemia - elevated\ncholesterol and \ntriglycerides. \n• Familial\nhypercholesterolemia\nLDL receptor mutation. . \n• Familial apo B100 defect\nFamilial\nhypertriacylglycerolemia\nSecondary dyslipidemia\n• diabetes mellitus;\n• nephrotic syndrome and \nchronic renal failure;\n• hypothyroidism;\n• primary biliary cirrhosis;\n• alcoholism (not to be \nconfused with the effect \nof small doses of \nalcohol);\n• the effect of medications \n(e.g. oral contraceptives)."}, {"page": 88, "text": "Hypolipoproteinemia\nFamilial hypo-β-lipoproteinemia\n rare genetic abnormality, probably with autosomal dominant inheritance.\n ↓LDL cholesterol in plasma\n longevity, probably due to the low incidence of myocardial infarction\nAbetalipoproteinemia\n rare autosomal recessive disease in which neither Apo-B48 is synthesized in enterocytes\nnor apo-B100 in the liver.\n chylomicrons are absent in the circulation and the transport of endogenous cholesterol\nto peripheral cells via LDL is impaired, concentration of cholesterol in plasma is reduced.\n Impaired absorption of lipids from the intestine causes their excessive loss in the feces\nand thus a deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins.\n delayed development and reduced intelligence, polyneuropathy, and muscle weakness.\nHypoalphalipoproteinemia\n Conditions with reduced HDL-cholesterol have an increased risk of atherosclerosis and\nthe resulting cardiovascular disease. The familial form appears to have dominant\ninheritance.\n The result is a relative lack of apoC-II and increased VLDL levels.\nAnalphaloproteinemia (Tangier disease)\n It is a rare disease with autosomal recessive inheritance, characterized by a complete\nabsence of HDL in plasma."}, {"page": 89, "text": "Recommended resources\n Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th Edition) \nby Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters Kluwer Health | \nLippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. Chapter 10. \n McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. Mosby, 2018. \nChapter 16. \n Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. Thieme, \n2016.\n Nečas a spolupracovníci, Obecná patologická fyziologie. Karolinum, 2000"}]}, {"id": "digestive", "title": "Pathophysiology of the digestive system ZN", "file": "/lectures/digestive.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Pathophysiology of the \ndigestive system\nDoc. M.Sc. Zdenka Navratilova, Ph.D."}, {"page": 2, "text": "• Symptoms \n• Indigestion or dyspepsia or an upset stomach is a symptom (e.g. abdominal pain and a feeling of fullness soon after \nyou start eating) of various digestive diseases, rather than a specific disease. \n• Dysphagia is the swallowing problems\n• Odynophagia is the painful swallowing\n• Pyrosis or heartburn is a burning sensation in the upper abdomen\n• Vomiting\n• Constipation\n• Diarrhea\n• Bleeding\n• Abdominal pain\n• The diverticula: types\n• The pharynx\n• Zenker's diverticulum or systemic diseases: Sjögren syndrome\n• The esophagus\n• Achalasia and epiphrenic / supradiafragmal diverticula \n• Parabronchial diverticula\n• The stomach\n• Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease\n• Hiatal hernia\n• Pyloric obstruction\n• Gastritis\n• Peptic Ulcer Disease: Duodenal ulcers and Gastric ulcers\n• Stress-Related Mucosal Disease and Dumping Syndrome\n• The small Intestinal Obstruction and Ileus\n• The Large intestine\n• Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Ulcerative Colitis (distal small intestine) and Crohn Disease\n• Diverticular Disease of the Colon\n• Irritable Bowel Syndrome\n• Megacolon\nGIT: Content \n"}, {"page": 3, "text": "Signs and symptoms: content\n• Indigestion or dyspepsia or an upset stomach is a symptom (e.g. abdominal \npain and a feeling of fullness soon after you start eating) of various \ndigestive diseases, rather than a specific disease. \n• Dysphagia is the swallowing problems\n• Odynophagia is the painful swallowing\n• Pyrosis or heartburn is a burning sensation in the upper abdomen\n• Vomiting\n• Constipation\n• Diarrhea\n• Bleeding\n• Abdominal pain\n"}, {"page": 4, "text": "• the duodenum and antrum of the stomach produce retrograde \nperistalsis while the body of the stomach and the esophagus relax \n• deep inspiration and the glottis closes\n• the diaphragm is forced high into the thoracic cavity by strong \ncontractions of the abdominal muscles \n• the higher intrathoracic pressure forces the upper esophageal \nsphincter to open, and chyme is expelled from the mouth \n• the stomach relaxes and the upper part of the esophagus contracts, \nforcing the remaining chyme back into the stomach\nA sympathetic discharge causes the tachycardia, tachypnea, and\nsweating.\nThe parasympathetic system mediates copious salivation, increased \ngastric motility, and relaxation of the upper and lower esophageal \nsphincters.\nVomiting "}, {"page": 5, "text": "Vomiting with/without nausea\n• Nausea and retching usually precede vomiting  \n• Nausea is a subjective experience \n• Retching is the muscular event of vomiting but the contents of the \nesophagus drop back into the stomach as the upper esophageal sphincter is \nclosed\n• Projectile vomiting is spontaneous vomiting that is not preceded by nausea or \nretching. Projectile vomiting is caused by direct stimulation of the vomiting center \nby neurologic lesions"}, {"page": 6, "text": "• The vomiting center lies in the medulla \noblongata and includes the reticular \nformation. The stimuli arising from: \n• the nucleus tractus solitarius\n• the cortex and thalamus (anxiety \nand pain)\n• The nucleus tractus solitarius\nintegrates inputs noxious stimuli \narising from\n• the vestibular system through \nthe vestibulocochlear nerve \n(VIII, motion sickness) \n• the chemoreceptor trigger \nzone\nchemorece\nptor trigger \nzone\nThe vomiting center "}, {"page": 7, "text": "Vomiting: chemoreceptor trigger zone\n• The chemoreceptor trigger zone (the area postrema = a \nsensory circumventricular organ without a blood–brain \nbarrier) has the receptors for dopamine, serotonin, \nhistamine, substance P, opioid and acetylcholine \nreceptors.\n• These receptors monitor blood content and signal to \nthe vomiting center. \n• They monitor intestinal, vagal, sympathetic input, \nincluding: pharyngeal stimulation, myocardial \nischemia, gastric irritation or distension.\n• Serotonin is produced by the intestinal \nenterochromaffin cells which can be stimulated by \nluminal distension or changed osmotic concentrations \nin intestinal contents.\n• Serotonin antagonists are effective antiemetics\n(drugs preventing or relieving nausea and \nvomiting) and have been used to treat nausea \nand vomiting associated with postoperative \nvomiting and cancer chemotherapy"}, {"page": 8, "text": "difficult or infrequent defecation\n• Primary constipation\n• Normal transit (functional) constipation: a normal stool passage \nbut difficulty with stool evacuation. Causes: low-residue, low fluid \nintake,  low physical activity\n• Slow-transit constipation: impaired colonic motor activity with \ninfrequent bowel movements, straining to defecate, mild \nabdominal distention, and palpable stool in the sigmoid colon. \n• Pelvic floor dysfunction: difficulty expelling stool because of \nfailure of the pelvic floor muscles or anal sphincter to relax with \ndefecation.\n• Secondary constipation\nNeurogenic disorders: stroke, Parkinson disease, spinal cord injury, multiple \nsclerosis\nEndocrine or metabolic disorders: hypothyroidism, diabetes mellitus, \nhypokalemia, and hypercalcemia.\nPelvic hiatal hernia (herniation of the bowel through the floor of the pelvis), \ndiverticuli, irritable bowel syndrome– constipation predominant, \nDrugs: codeine, antacids containing calcium carbonate\nPregnancy \nAging due to degeneration of neurons in the myenteric plexus\nConstipation"}, {"page": 9, "text": "• Large-volume diarrhea:\n• Osmotic diarrhea: a nonabsorbable substance in the intestine \ndraws water into the lumen by osmosis. Causes: oral doses of \nmagnesium, sulfate, and phosphate, synthetic sugars: sorbitol) or \nlactase deficiency, pancreatic enzyme or bile salt deficiency, small \nintestine bacterial overgrowth, and celiac disease.\n• Secretory diarrhea caused by excessive mucosal secretion of \nchloride- or bicarbonate-rich fluid or inhibition of net sodium \nabsorption. Infectious causes include viruses (rotavirus), bacterial \nenterotoxins (Escherichia coli and Vibrio cholerae)\n• Small-volume diarrhea usually is caused by an inflammatory disorder of \nthe intestine, such as ulcerative colitis, Crohn disease, or microscopic \ncolitis\n• Motility diarrhea: excessive motility caused by resection of the small \nintestine (short bowel syndrome), surgical bypass of an area of the \nintestine, fistula formation between loops of intestine, irritable bowel \nsyndrome–diarrhea predominant, diabetic neuropathy, hyperthyroidism, \nand laxative abuse. \nDiarrhea:"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Abdominal pain\n• Parietal (somatic) pain arises from the parietal peritoneum. \n• More intense\n• More localized : Nerve fibers from the parietal peritoneum travel \nwith peripheral nerves to the spinal cord, and the sensation of \npain corresponds to skin dermatomes\n• Visceral pain arises from a stimulus (distention, inflammation, ischemia) \nacting on abdominal organ\n• An dull (vague) rather than sharp, and is difficult to describe\n• Poorly localized (diffuse):Pain is usually felt near the midline in \nthe epigastrium, midabdomen, or lower abdomen. \n• Referred pain is visceral pain felt at some distance from a diseased \nor an affected organ. It is felt in the skin dermatomes or deeper \ntissues that share a central afferent pathway with the affected organ. \nGallbladder pain is, for example, referred to the right shoulder or \nscapulae. "}, {"page": 11, "text": "• Upper GI bleeding is bleeding in the esophagus, stomach, or duodenum and \nis characterized by \n• Hematemesis: is the vomiting of blood, which may be obviously red or \nhave an appearance similar to coffee grounds.  \n• Melena: dark, grainy digested blood (“coffeegrounds”) in stool.\n• Lower GI bleeding: bleeding from the small intestine (jejunum or ileum), \ncolon, or rectum (polyps, inflammatory bowel disease, diverticulosis, cancer, or \nhemorrhoids)\n• Haematochezia (enterorrhagia): fresh blood in stool (ulcerative colitis, in \nthe ileum and transverse colon or hemorrhoids)\n• Haematochezia from an upper gastrointestinal source is an ominous \nsign, as it suggests a very significant bleed which is more likely to be \nlife-threatening\n• Rectorrhagia: rectal bleeding without defecation; expulsion of fresh bright \nred blood\n• Occult bleeding is usually caused by slow, chronic blood loss that is not \nobvious and results in iron deficiency anemia as iron stores in the bone \nmarrow are slowly depleted. \n• Acute, severe GI bleeding is life threatening depending on the volume and \nrate of blood loss, associated disease, age, and effectiveness of treatment.\nGastrointestinal (GI) bleeding "}, {"page": 12, "text": "Dysphagia: oropharyngeal  \n• It is difficulty swallowing. Disorders that affect the striated muscles of the \nupper esophagus interfere with the oropharyngeal (voluntary) phase of \nswallowing.\n• Mechanical obstructions:\n• Intrinsic: originate in the wall of the esophageal lumen(tumors, \nstrictures, and diverticular herniation, see diverticula)\n• Extrinsic: originate outside the esophageal lumen and narrow the \nesophagus by pressing inward on the esophageal wall.(tumor)\n• Functional dysphagia:\n• Systemic disorders: \n• Sjogren syndrome\n• Dermatomyositis (an idiopathic inflammatory symmetric, \nproximal skeletal muscle disease) \n• Neurologic impairments: caused by \n• Stroke \n• Multiple sclerosis\n• Parkinson disease\n• Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis\n• Myasthenia gravis\n• Poliomyelitis (poliovirus)"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Dysphagia: Sjögren syndrome or Sjögren's syndrome\n• A long-term autoimmune disease leading to loss of exocrine glands: \nsalivary glands and often seriously affects other organ systems, such \nas the lungs, kidneys, and nervous system\n• The hallmark symptom of Sjögren syndrome is dry \nmouth (xerostomia) and xerophthalmia (dry eyes) with \nkeratoconjunctivitis. Sicca syndrome refers to dry eyes and mouths.\n• It is the second-most common rheumatic autoimmune disorder, \nbehind rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus"}, {"page": 14, "text": "The esophagus: content   \n• Achalasia and diverticula (types and examples): affected motility\n• Gastroesophageal reflux: lesion\n• Hiatal hernia: types\n• Mallory–Weiss syndrome\n• Systemic diseases: e.g. sclerodermia"}, {"page": 15, "text": "• Traction diverticula: resulting from a pulling force on the outside of the \nalimentary tract, are more commonly seen in the mid- to distal \nesophagus and often result from inflammatory or neoplastic processes \nadjacent to the muscularis.\n• Pulsion diverticula: result from an internal pushing force (intraluminal \npressure ) causing the mucosa and submucosa to herniate through the \nmuscular wall. These are pseudodiverticula: mucosa and submucosa \nthrough the muscularis propria.\nTypes of diverticula\nContain only \nmucosa and \nsubmucosa"}, {"page": 16, "text": "• Diverticulum of the mucosa of the human pharynx, just above \nthe cricopharyngeal muscle (above the upper sphincter of the esophagus). \n• Pseudo diverticulum: not involving all layers of the esophageal wall\n• Pulsion diverticula: result from an internal pushing\nPharynx: Zenker's diverticulum or pharyngeal pouch\nClinical symptoms:\n• difficulty swallowing (dysphagia)\n• spontaneous return of undigested \nfood (regurgitation of undigested \nfood)\n• chronic aspiration and frequent \nrespiratory infections (aspiration \npneumonia)\n• bad breath (fetor ex-ore) due to the \nexistence of decomposed food \ningredients in a diverticulum."}, {"page": 17, "text": "The esophagus: Achalasia   \n• a rare disorder related to loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus \nwith smooth muscle atrophy in the middle and lower portions of the \nesophagus. This leads to altered esophageal peristalsis and failure of the \nlower esophageal sphincter  to relax.\nUnknown etiology: viral or \nautoimmune mechanisms. This \nleads to altered esophageal \nperistalsis and failure of the \nlower esophageal sphincter  to \nrelax"}, {"page": 18, "text": "• on the body of the esophagus and above the diaphragm\n• The predominant motor disorder is diffuse spasm or functional obstruction \nusually done on patients primarily suffering from achalasia.\n• Pseudo diverticulum: not involving all layers of the esophageal wall\n• Pulsion diverticula\nEpiphrenic or supradiafragmal diverticula \nClinical manifestation:\nDysphagia\nRegurgitation\nRetrosternal pain mostly \ndue to the joint nutcracker esophagus\n"}, {"page": 19, "text": "• Traction diverticula are usually localized in the thoracic esophagus \n(tracheal bifurcation).\n• Results from inflammation of lymph nodes with subsequent scarring and \nadhesion of matted nodes to the esophagus. (tuberculosis or \nhistoplasmosis).\n• Traction diverticula\n• True diverticula\nParabronchial diverticulum: in the middle of the chest\n"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Gastroesophageal reflux leading to\nGastroesophageal Reflux Disease\nGastroesophageal Reflux Disease: the regurgitation of acid and pepsin from \nthe stomach to the esophagus that causes esophagitis.\n• Physiologic reflux: Gastroesophageal reflux that does not cause \nsymptoms\n• Nonerosive reflux disease: symptoms of reflux disease but no visible \nesophageal mucosal injury\nCauses and risk factors: weaken low esophageal sphincter (LES) mediated by \n• Hiatal hernia\n• Drugs or chemicals that relax the LES: anticholinergics, nitrates, calcium \nchannel blockers, nicotine\n• Increases abdominal pressure: vomiting, coughing, lifting, bending, or \nobesity or asthma or chronic cough\nLater the inflammation can further damage vagus innervation that lead to delay \ngastric emptying worsening motility: lengthened period during which reflux is \npossible or increasing the acid content of chyme, e.g. gastric or duodenal \nulcers, which can cause pyloric edema"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Pathophysiology:\ntransient relaxation or weakness or low resting tone of the low esophageal sphincter\nInflammatory responses in the esophageal wall resulting in: hyperemia, edema, \ntissue fragility, erosion, and ulcerations, fibrosis (strictures or abnormal narrowing ), \nbasal cell hyperplasia, and elongation of papillae. \nBarrett esophagus: precancerous lesions can progress to adenocarcinoma \nThe clinical manifestations: \n• Heartburn from acid regurgitation: Heartburn also may be experienced as chest \npain, which requires ruling out cardiac ischemia.\n• Chronic cough, asthma attacks \n• Laryngitis\n• Dysphagia.\nUpper abdominal pain usually occurs within 1 hour of eating and can\nbe relapsing and remitting. Symptoms can worsen if the individual\nlies down.\nGastroesophageal reflux leading to\nGastroesophageal Reflux Disease"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Barrett esophagus\nchange in the mucosal cells lining the lower portion of \nthe esophagus, from stratified squamous epithelium to simple \ncolumnar epithelium with interspersed goblet cells that normally \nsecrete mucins only in the small intestine and large intestine.\nA premalignant condition because it can lead to transition to esophageal \nadenocarcinoma."}, {"page": 23, "text": ""}, {"page": 24, "text": "• The protrusion (herniation) of the upper part of the stomach through the \ndiaphragm into the thorax.\n• Four types: \n• Sliding (type I):\n• Paraesophageal (type II):\n• Mixed (type III): which include elements of types I and II\n• Type IV: the entire stomach and other abdominal organs slide into \nthe thorax.\nHiatal hernia "}, {"page": 25, "text": "• the most common type\n• the proximal portion of the stomach moves into the thoracic cavity through the \nesophageal hiatus, an opening in the diaphragm for the esophagus and vagus\nnerves.\n• Supine position:, the lower esophagus and stomach are pulled into the \nthorax.\n• Standing causes the organs to “slide” back into the abdomen. Sliding hiatal \nhernia is by factors that\n• Symptoms are exacerbated by intra-abdominal pressure: coughing, \nbending, wearing tight clothing, ascites, obesity, or pregnancy \n(progesterone and estrogen may lower the resting pressure of the LES \nfurther). \n• This type of hernia is associated with gastroesophageal reflux because the\nhernia diminishes the resting pressure of the LES. \nHiatal hernia:\nType I or sliding hiatal hernia"}, {"page": 26, "text": "• herniation of the greater curvature of the stomach is through a \nsecondary opening in the diaphragm. As the stomach \nprotrudes through the opening into the thorax, it lies \nalongside the esophagus. \n• The gastroesophageal junction remains below the diaphragm. \n• The position of a portion of the stomach above the \ndiaphragm, however, causes congestion of mucosal blood \nflow and can lead to gastritis and ulcer formation. A \nmechanical strangulation of the hernia is a major \ncomplication, and surgical correction is required. \nStrangulation occludes blood vessels and causes vascular \nengorgement,edema, ischemia, and hemorrhage.\nHiatal hernia:\nType II, paraesophageal hiatal hernia (rolling \nhiatal hernia)"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Type I, sliding hiatal hernia\nvs\nType II, paraesophageal hiatal hernia (rolling \nhiatal hernia)\n"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Type I: Absent angle of His (esophagogastric angle)\nType II: Present angle of His (esophagogastric angle)\nType I, sliding hiatal hernia\nvs\nType II, paraesophageal hiatal hernia (rolling \nhiatal hernia)"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Mallory–Weiss syndrome or gastro-esophageal \nlaceration syndrome\n• It refers to bleeding from a laceration in the mucosa at the junction of\nthe stomach and esophagus. This is usually caused by\nsevere vomiting because of alcoholism or bulimia, but can be caused by\nany condition which causes violent vomiting and retching such as food\npoisoning. The syndrome presents with hematemesis. The laceration is\nsometimes referred to as a Mallory–Weiss tear.\n"}, {"page": 30, "text": "• The narrowing or blocking of the opening between the stomach and \nthe duodenum. \n• Congenital: infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis\n• Acquired: peptic ulcer disease or carcinoma near the pylorus. \nDuodenal ulcers are more likely than gastric ulcers to obstruct the \npylorus. Ulceration causes obstruction resulting from \ninflammation, edema, spasm, fibrosis, or scarring. Tumors cause \nobstruction by growing into the pylorus\n• Symptoms: \n• Less severe obstruction causes vague epigastric fullness\n• Severe obstruction causes gastric distention and atony (lack of \nmuscle tone and gastric motility). Cardinal signs: vomiting and \nrolling or jarring of the abdomen produces a sloshing sound \ncalled the succussion or gastric splash.\nPyloric obstruction (gastric outlet\nobstruction)"}, {"page": 31, "text": "The Stomach\n• Introduction: mucosa of stomach\n• Zollinger-Ellison syndrome\n• Gastritis \n• Acute and chronic (type A and B)\n• Peptic Ulcer Disease\n• Gastric and duodenal ulcers\n• Defensive factors and aggressive factors\n• Helicobacter Pylori\n• Ischemic and curling ulcers\n• Postprandial Dumping syndrome"}, {"page": 32, "text": "• Chief cells secrete:\n• pepsinogen which is readily converted \nto pepsin (proteolytic enzyme ) at a pH \nof 2. \n• Lipase\n• HCO3\n-\n• Parietal cells secrete\n• hydrochloric acid \n• intrinsic factor (pernicious anemia) \n• gastroferrin (It facilitates small intestinal \nabsorption of iron) \n• G cells secrete: gastrin which stimulates \nHCl and gastric motility (mainly pyloric \ngland mucosa in the antrum) and \npancreatic juice.\n• Enterochromaffin-like cells secrete histamine. Histamine receptors in the gastric \nmucosa are H2 receptors (unlike those in the bronchial mucosa, which are H1 \nreceptors). They stimulates gastric acid secretion.\n• D cells secrete somatostatin which inhibits acid secretion.\nMucosa of stomach"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Zollinger-Ellison syndrome\n• A gastrinoma (benign or malignant tumors) of the pancreas or \nduodenum (a gastrin-secreting\nneuroendocrine tumor or multiple tumors) stimulates a proliferation of \ngastric parietal cells and chronic secretion of gastric acid.\n• ↑gastrin, which causes ↑ gastric acid.\n• It causes gastric and duodenal ulcers,  gastroesophageal reflux\n"}, {"page": 34, "text": "Gastritis\nan inflammatory disorder of the gastric mucosa. The erosions are \nusually superficial.\n"}, {"page": 35, "text": "caused by injury of the protective mucosal barrier \n• Drugs: Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs NSAIDs (ibuprofen or \naspirin)\ninhibit cyclooxygenase-1, and thus inhibit prostaglandins, which \nnormally stimulate the secretion of mucus and suppress inflammation.\n• Helicobacter pylori: causes \n• Inflammation\n• Down-regulation of antral somatostatin leading to increased \ngastrin, increased acid, impaired mucosal bicarbonate \nproduction, and increased mucosal exposure to acid and pepsin.\n• Secretion of urease that produces ammonia and carbon dioxide, \nresulting in a more alkaline environment\n• Others: alcohol, histamine and metabolic disorders such as uremia \nare contributing factors.\nAcute gastritis\n"}, {"page": 36, "text": "occur in older adults and causes chronic inflammation, mucosal \natrophy, and epithelial metaplasia.\n• type A, or immune (fundal): rare and severe type\n• type B, nonimmune (antral): common\natrophic gastritis: antiparietal cell antibody and elevated plasma ghrelin \nlevel\nH. pylori gastritis : H. pylori infection\nChronic gastritis \n"}, {"page": 37, "text": "Chronic fundal gastritis: type A\nloss of T-cell tolerance and development of autoantibodies to \ngastric H+-K+ ATPase.\nH. pylori can trigger the immune response through molecular\nMimicry\n• Loss of parietal cells \n• diminishes secretion of hydrochloric acid (achlorhydria, \ndiminished secretion of hydrochloric acid) causing \nelevated plasma levels of gastrin\n• decreased intrinsic factor causing pernicious anemia\n"}, {"page": 38, "text": "It is caused by \n• H. pylori bacteria\n• chronic use of alcohol, tobacco, and nonsteroidal antiinflammatory\ndrugs. \nThere are high levels of hydrochloric acid\nsecretion with an increased risk of duodenal ulcers. H. pylori can also \nprogress to autoimmune atrophic gastritis and involve\nthe fundus, thus becoming pangastritis. In these cases there is greater \nrisk for the development of gastric cancer\nChronic antral gastritis: type B\n"}, {"page": 39, "text": "• a stomach inflammation caused by reflux of bile and alkaline \npancreatic secretions that contain proteolytic enzymes and disrupt \nthe mucosal barrier in the remnant stomach.\n• Clinical manifestations include nausea, bilious vomiting (vomiting \nin which the vomitus contains bile), and sustained epigastric pain \nthat worsens after eating and is not relieved by antacids.\nAlkaline reflux gastritis \n"}, {"page": 40, "text": "• Duodenal ulcers: \n• greater frequency \nthan other types of \npeptic ulcers, \n• hyperacidity \n• possible overweight\nPeptic Ulcer Disease\n• Gastric ulcers: \n• absent hyperacidity,\n• loss of weight, \n• 1:1 man : women and \nolder age\n• Erosions: superficial ulcerations \n• True ulcers: extend through the muscularis mucosae and damage \nblood vessels, causing hemorrhage or perforating the \ngastrointestinal wall. "}, {"page": 41, "text": "• An abnormality in balance between protective and aggressive factors (e.g. \nmucus, somatostatin from gastric D cells and others versus histamine, \npepsin, NSAIDs, nicotine, alcohol, stress and others) that increases the \nmucosal barrier’s permeability to hydrogen ions or in other words, decreased \nability to withstand normal gastric acidity. Gastric hypoacidity is not typical \nfeature.\n• frequently caused by H. pylori (an aggressive factor)\n• frequently due to duodenal reflux  after cholecystectomy:\n• Alkaline reflux gastritis: Reflux of bile and pancreatic enzymes damage \nthe gastric mucosa. The damaged mucosal barrier permits hydrogen \nions to diffuse into the mucosa, where they disrupt permeability and \ncellular structure. A vicious cycle can be established as the damaged \nmucosa liberates histamine, which stimulates the increase of acid and \npepsinogen production, blood flow, and capillary permeability. The \ndisrupted mucosa becomes edematous and loses plasma proteins. \nDestruction of small vessels causes bleeding.\n• Usually antral region, adjacent to the acid-secreting mucosa of the body\nGastric ulcers: pathophysiology \n"}, {"page": 42, "text": "Duodenal ulcers: pathophysiology \n• Pathophysiology: Hyperacidity: Gastric acid and pepsin penetrate \nduodenal mucosal barrier leading to ulceration\n• Genetic factors, often between 20-40 years, increasing prevalence \namong women, 90-95% H pylori\n• Manifestation:\n• chronic intermittent pain in the epigastric area when the stomach is \nempty. (The pain begins 30 minutes to 2 hours after eating, when \nthe stomach is empty). It is not unusual for pain to occur in the \nmiddle of the night and disappear by morning (It can lead to \noverweight). The pain results from sensorineural stimulation by acid \nor muscle spasm, or both. Pain is relieved rapidly by food or \nantacids.\n• Bleeding from duodenal ulcers causes hematemesis or melena."}, {"page": 43, "text": "Defensive factors:\n• Compact epithelial lining: tight junctions\n• Bicarbonate ions\n• Mucus covering\n• Prostaglandins and nitric oxide protect the \nmucosal barrier by stimulating the secretion of \nmucus and bicarbonate and by inhibiting the \nsecretion of acid.\n• Somatostatin, and secretin inhibit acid \nsecretion.\n• Acid secretion can be suppressed with drugs \nthat block H2 receptors, proton pump \ninhibitors.\n• Anticholinergic drugs may be used to inhibit \ngastric secretion, suppress gastric motility, and \ndelay gastric emptying. (Physiology: Through \nvagal stimulation, acetylcholine stimulate \npepsin and acid secretion. \nAggressive factors:\nPepsin\nAcid (also stimulates chief cells \nto secrete pepsin\nH. Pylori\nNicotine\nAlcohol\nDrugs (NSAIDs)\nDelayed stomach emptying\nStress\nPeptic ulcer: \ndefensive factors and aggressive factors"}, {"page": 44, "text": "1. A characteristics feature: the synthesis of urease\nIt catalyzes urea hydrolysis with the formation of ammonia (NH3) and carbon \ndioxide. \nBy this mechanism, \n• H. pylori neutralizes the surrounding gastric acid and protects itself from \nthe strong acidity of the stomach (NH3 + H+ = NH4\n+ is less acidic than \nH+)\n• Metabolites from urease activity are toxic to gastric epithelial cells: The \nformed ammonium reacts with hypochloride anion (OCl-) produced by \nactivated neutrophils to form highly toxic monochloramine (NH2Cl) in the \nstomach, a hallmark of H. pylori infection. \nPeptic ulcer: Helicobacter Pylori\n2. Spiral shape and the motility of their flagella: \nrender them resistant to peristaltic flushing of the \ngastric contents and enable them to persist in the \nmucous layer.\n3. Pathogen produces other enzymes including \ncatalase, oxidase, protease, and phospholipase, \nas well as it synthesizes specific adhesion \nproteins that enable them to adhere to mucous \nand epithelial cells\nMucosa \n(Mucous membrane)\nMucus \nwith \nmucins\nAcid"}, {"page": 45, "text": "toxic \nmonochloramine\n• Enterochromaffin-like cells secrete histamine. Histamine \nreceptors in the gastric mucosa are H2 receptors (unlike \nthose in the bronchial mucosa, which are H1 \nreceptors). They stimulates gastric acid secretion.\nInduced inflammation and ROS production\nGastric ulcers \nHelicobacter Pylori\nammonia"}, {"page": 46, "text": "Stress ulcer or Stress-Related Mucosal Disease\n• An acute form of peptic ulcer\n• Cause: the physiologic stress of severe illness; multisystem organ failure; \nor major trauma, including severe burns or head injury. Usually, multiple \nsites of ulceration are distributed within the stomach or duodenum.\n• Stress ulcers may be classified as:\n• Ischemic ulcers: within hours of an event(hemorrhage, multisystem \ntrauma, severe burns, heart failure, or sepsis) that causes ischemia \nof the stomach and duodenal mucosa. Stress ulcers that develop as \na result of burn injury are often called Curling ulcers.\n• Cushing ulcers: named after Harvey Cushing, is a gastric ulcer \nassociated with elevated intracranial pressure. Cushing ulcer results \nfrom decreased mucosal blood flow and hypersecretion of HCL \ncaused by overstimulation of the vagal nuclei after severe head \ntrauma or brain surgery."}, {"page": 47, "text": "Postprandial Dumping syndrome\nrapid emptying of hypertonic chyme from the surgically created, \nresidual stomach into the small intestine 10 to 20 minutes after \neating\n• After partial gastrectomy, bariatric surgical procedures, or \npyloroplasty"}, {"page": 48, "text": "The small intestine: content \n• Physiological review: Mucosa of the small intestine\n• Malassimilation: primary and secondary\n• The intestine obstruction or ileus\n• Inflammatory diseases: Crohn disease"}, {"page": 49, "text": "Hormone ACTION\nMotilin • Increases gastrointestinal motility\nSecretin • Stimulates alkaline pancreatic juice and liver to secrete bile \n• Decreases gastrointestinal motility (similar to glucagon and adrenalin)\n• Inhibits gastrin and gastric acid secretion\nCholecystokinin • Stimulates gallbladder to eject bile (contraction) and pancreas to secrete \nalkaline fluid\n• Decreases gastric motility and constricts pyloric sphincter and increased \nintestinal motility (similar to insulin)\n• Inhibits gastrin; delays gastric emptying\nEnterokinase\n(enteropeptidase)\n• the pancreatic enzymes are activated by enterokinase that is secreted in \nduodenum\nIncretins (e.g. glucagon-\nlike peptide from L cells)\n• Increase sensitivity of pancreatic beta cells of the islets of Langerhans and \nthereby increasing insulin release (therapy in Type II DM)\n• Inhibits glucagon release from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans.\nMucosa of the small intestine"}, {"page": 50, "text": "Small intestine obstruction: general clinical \nmanifestation \n• Vomiting: initially arising reflexively, later from accumulation of contents\n• Abdominal pain (colicky, sharp pain arising from distension)\n• Constipation and meteorism or tympanites (flatulence and abdominal \nbloating due to increased intestinal gas)\n• Abdominal swelling (we describe it as \" belly above niveau \").\nX-ray image of \nthe abdomen \nshowing \nhydroaeric\nphenomenon: \nhorizontal layers \nof intestinal \ncontents (white) \nand above them \ndilated intestinal \nvilli filled with \ngas (dark areas)"}, {"page": 51, "text": "• Distention (pain: colicky character) begins almost  immediately due to accumulation of fluid and gas inside \nthe lumen proximal to the obstruction. \n• Fluids accumulate: within 24 hours, up to 8 L of fluid enters the lumen from \n• enhanced movement of fluid from the vascular space to the intestinal lumen\n• impaired water and electrolyte absorption. \n• saliva, gastric juice, bile, pancreatic juice, and intestinal secretions\n• Gas from swallowed air, and to a lesser extent from bacterial overgrowth, contributes to the \ndistention. \n• Distention decreases the intestine’s ability to absorb water and electrolytes and increases the net \nsecretion of these substances into the lumen.\n• Compared to the proximal small intestine obstruction in the distal small intestine causes more \npronounced distention because a greater length of intestine is proximal to the obstruction \n• Distention push against the diaphragm: decrease lung volume leading to atelectasis and pneumonia \n(particularly in debilitated individuals)\n• Extracellular fluid volume and plasma volume decrease, causing dehydration, elevated hematocrit, \nhypotension and tachycardia. Severe dehydration leads to hypovolemic shock.\n• Copious vomiting (↓K+, ↓ Na+)\n• Obstruction in the proximal small intestine causes mild distention and vomiting of bile-stained fluid.\n• Obstruction in the distal small intestine vomiting may not occur or may occur later and contain fecal \nmaterial.\n• Electrolyte disturbances:\n• Pyloric or higher obstruction: metabolic alkalosis\n• Excessive loss of hydrogen ions that normally would be reabsorbed from the gastric juice\n• Prolonged obstruction or obstruction lower in the intestine: metabolic acidosis\n• bicarbonate from pancreatic secretions and bile cannot be reabsorbed\n• ketosis result from starvation. Lack of circulation permits\n• lactic acid\n• Occluded arterial circulation: ischemia, necrosis, perforation, and peritonitis, overgrowth of bacteria (fever, \nleukocytosis) or sepsis.\nSmall intestine obstruction: pathophysiology "}, {"page": 52, "text": "• Mechanical:\n• Obturation: Impacted stool\n• Volvulus: Disorder of intestinal \nrotation or fastening to the back \nwall of the abdominal.\n• Strangulation: external \nobstruction \n• Neurogenic\n• Paralytic ileus\n• Spastic\n• Mixed\n• Vascular: due to \narterial embolism or \nvenous thrombosis\nSmall intestine obstruction: types of ileus \n"}, {"page": 53, "text": "• It is caused by the impacted stool \n• Manifestation:\n• Colicky pains: not very strong, the intensity gradually increases \nand later permanent pain from distension with a decrease in \nperistalsis.\n• Vomiting: occurs later due to stagnation of bowel contents above \nthe obstruction. The more distal the obstruction, the later it occurs.)\n• Tactile soreness: it is mainly localized in the area of ​​the obstacle. \nAfter moving the palpating hand away, the pain disappears\n• Peristaltic waves: visible on the abdominal wall and \naudible effortful peristalsis\nSmall intestine obstruction: \nSimple mechanical ileus (obstructive)\n"}, {"page": 54, "text": "Small intestine obstruction: \nStrangulation ileus\n• It is caused by external obstruction to the small bowel, which results in \nischemia and loss of bowel peristalsis\n• Causes: adhesions in the abdomen that form after surgery; hernias; \ncolon cancer; certain medications; or strictures from an inflamed \nintestine caused by Crohn's disease or diverticulitis\n• Manifestation\n• Colicky pains: very strong from the beginning\n• Tactile soreness: in the place of the obstacle, does not disappear \nwhen the examining hand is moved away.\n• Vomiting: from the beginning\n• Flatulence: with stoppage of passing gases and stools; troubles the \npatient from the beginning of the episode.\n• A mixture of blood and mucus in the stool.\n• Disappearance of peristalsis.\nA drop in blood pressure, an increase in heart rate, pallor and even cyanosis \nof the face ."}, {"page": 55, "text": "Small intestine obstruction: \nParalytic ileus =\"dead silence„\n• Causes: post-operative conditions, intoxication, etc.\n• Manifestation\n• Weaker pains from bowel and abdominal wall distension, colic \npains are not present.\n• Gases and stools do not pass and the abdomen is evenly \ninflated.\n• The patient is in a relatively good condition: normal blood \npressure, heart rate, body "}, {"page": 56, "text": "Small intestine obstruction: \nVascular ileus\n• Causes: arterial embolism or venous thrombosis, we are interested in \nheart or vascular diseases.\n• Manifestation\n• Sharp to shocking pain.\n• From the beginning reflex vomiting.\n• Loose stool with blood admixture appears.\n• Increase in heart rate, decrease in blood pressure."}, {"page": 57, "text": "Small intestine obstruction: \nSpastic ileus"}, {"page": 58, "text": "Inflammatory diseases: Crohn disease\n• CD affects any part of the gastrointestinal tract from the mouth to the anus. \nThe distal small intestine (malabsorption) and proximal large intestine are \nmost commonly affected (patchy distribution in the colon or ileum).\n• The typical chronic lesion is a skip granuloma having cobblestone \nprojections of inflamed tissue surrounded by areas of ulceration. It starts in \nthe intestinal submucosa and spreads across the intestinal wall to involve \nthe mucosa and serosa in areas overlying lymphoid tissue. Complications \ninclude obstruction, fistulae, abscess formation, and chronic blood loss.\n• Th1 immune response and IgG associated with severity\n• Its causes are unknown: aggressive response to normal flora bacteria in \ngenetically predisposed individuals (e.g. TLR)"}, {"page": 59, "text": "The distal small intestine (malabsorption) and proximal \nlarge intestine are most commonly affected (patchy \ndistribution in the colon or ileum)\nInflammatory diseases: Crohn disease"}, {"page": 60, "text": "It starts in the intestinal submucosa and spreads across the intestinal wall \nto involve the mucosa and serosa in areas overlying lymphoid tissue. \nComplications include obstruction, fistulae, abscess formation, and chronic \nblood loss.\nInflammatory diseases: Crohn disease"}, {"page": 61, "text": "Complications include obstruction, fistulae, abscess formation, and chronic \nblood loss.\n• The affected serosis leads to adhesions in which fistulas are formed.\n• The fibrosis leads to stenosis and colicky pain (subileus state)\nInflammatory diseases: Crohn disease"}, {"page": 62, "text": "The large intestine \n• Diverticular Disease of the Colon\n• Colon polyps: cancer\n• Megacolon\n• Irritable Bowel Syndrome\n• Inflammatory diseases: Ulcerative colitis"}, {"page": 63, "text": "Diverticular Disease of the Colon\nDiverticula: herniation or saclike outpouchings of mucosa through the muscle \nlayers of the colon wall. \n• anywhere in the gastrointestinal tract but the most common site is the left \ncolon. They rarely occur in the small intestine\n• The diverticula form at weak points in the colon wall, usually where arteries \npenetrate the tunica muscularis to nourish the mucosal layer.\n• A common associated finding is thickening of the circular and longitudinal \n(teniae coli) muscles surrounding the diverticula. Hypertrophy and contraction \nof these muscles increase intraluminal pressure and degree of herniation.\nDiverticulosis: asymptomatic diverticular disease. \nDiverticulitis represents inflammation. \nCauses: unknown but is associated with age greater than 60 years, combination\nof decreased dietary fiber, increased intracolonic pressure, abnormal\nneuromuscular function, and alterations in intestinal motility"}, {"page": 64, "text": "Colon polyps\n• Non-neoplastic polyps: hyperplastic polyps, or inflammatory polyps\n• Neoplastic polyps (epithelial dysplasia or abnormal \ncells ): adenomas (benign) and carcinomas (malignant): colorectal \ncarcinoma"}, {"page": 65, "text": "• An abnormal dilation of the colon\n• Congenital or aganglionic megacolon or Hirschsprung's \ndisease: the ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus are absent.\n• Acquired megacolon: Parkinson diseases\n• Toxic megacolon in ulcerative colitis due to excessive \nproduction of nitric oxide colitis.\nMegacolon\nHirschsprung's disease"}, {"page": 66, "text": "• a massive dilation of the large bowel (the cecum) that occurs in\ncritically ill patients and immobilized older adults. \n• cause: excessive sympathetic motor input or decreased \nparasympathetic motor input.\n• absence of mechanical obstruction\nAcute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)"}, {"page": 67, "text": "Irritable Bowel Syndrome\n• Abdominal pain and altered bowel habits (diarrhea-predominant, \nconstipation-predominant) \n• Common in women, with a higher prevalence in youth and middle age.\n• Causes: anxiety and depression\n• Pathophysiology:\n• Visceral hypersensitivity or hyperalgesia, particularly with \ndistention of the rectum but also other areas of the gut.\n• Abnormal gastrointestinal permeability, motility, and secretion: The \nmechanisms may be related dysregulation of the brain-gut axis or \nto the role of serotonin\n• Overgrowth of intestinal flora\n• Food allergy or food intolerance\n• Psychosocial factors"}, {"page": 68, "text": "Ulcerative colitis (UC)\n• UC is a chronic inflammatory disease that causes ulceration of the colonic \nmucosa and extends proximally from the rectum into the colon: \ncontinuous lesions, are limited to the mucosa, and are not transmural. The \nmucous layer is thinner than normal bud edema and thickening of the \nmuscular mucosae may narrow the lumen of the involved colon. \n• In chronic disease, inflammatory polyps (pseudopolyps) develop in the \ncolon from rapidly regenerating epithelium(also in CD). Mucosal \ndestruction causes bloody diarrhea with passage of purulent mucus but \nsevere hemorrhage is rare.\n• T cells, plasma cells in the inflamed colon and colonic epithelial antibodies \nIgG in the circulation\n• Its causes are unknown: bacterial, genetic, immunologic or dietary factors"}, {"page": 69, "text": "References \nLook up the particular theme in the following indexes\n• Porth's Pathophysiology: Concepts of Altered Health States (9th \nEdition) by Sheila Grossman, Carol Mattson Porth. Wolters \nKluwer Health | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2014. \n• McCance K. L., Huether S. E.: Pathophysiology. 8th Edition. \nMosby, 2018. \n• Silbernagl S, Lang F. Color Atlas of Pathophysiology, 3rd Ed. \nThieme, 2016. "}]}];